      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>FOOD PRICES.</head>
        <p>FOOD PRICES.</p>
        <p>Vigorous Protest At Trade Union Conference.</p>
        <p>A resolution denouncing the inactivity of the Government in dealing with the con- tinued increases in food prices and request- ing organised labour to demand increased wages equivalent to the increased cost of food, was adopted at a conference held on Tuesday even- lug in the Socialist Hall. The meeting was composed of delegates from the trade union branches of Warrington, and was presided over by Mr. E. Plinston.</p>
        <p>The Value of a Sovereign.</p>
        <p>The Chairman referred to the abnorthal cost of living since the war commenced, And said the desire of the Trades and Labour Council in calling the conference was to adopt as far as possible something to meet that abnormal advance. The purchasing power of the sovereign to-day was said to be 12s. id., the cost of living having gone up over 65 per cent. The time had come when every one interested in the trade union movement should look into the impover- ished conditions of the working classes, lie was afraid it was useless to look to the Goveru. ment to deal with this question, and the only remedy he could think of was for an increased wage for the working classes. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Hamblett, who moved the resolution sent up by the Labour Executive, said the question was most momentous. A virile nation could only be built up by supplying good food and plenty of it, and they were all anxious to come to some understanding whereby they might be able to stop this alarming increase in the price of food. No sane Government would find any difficulty in adopting these proposals. Millionaires And Starving People.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Howard seconded, and said the war, had placed many problems before them which _they would have to face, and not the least of these was the food prices question. Even before the war there was a general tendency to increase the price of foodstuffs. Mr. Asquith had said that the only way the position could be met was by an equivalent increase in wages. Everybody but the workers, who really matters, benefit out of the war, and the workers had only to sacri- fice, sacrifice, sacrifice, until they had nothing to sacrifice but their lives. They were making millionaires on the one hand and starving people on the other. Mr. Chamberlain (No. 2 Branch of Railway- men) submitted that trade union organisations were perfectly justified in demanding that the law of supply and demand should operate in respect to the wage of the workers. So long as they saw the profits of the profiteers going up by leaps and bounds, the working classes were justified in taking such action as would suit their circumstances. He instanced as an illus- tration the case of the railwaymen, whose de- mand was so tardily recognised. He supported the resolution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>Advance in Prices.</head>
        <p>The Advance in Prices.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Sinclair (wireworkers) gave some inter- esting figures showing how food prices had in- creased since July, 1914. Bacon 50 per cent.,</p>
        <p>potatoes 55, fish 100, beef 85, tea 23, butter 40, being some of .the increases. The average in- crease in wages was about 15 per cent. lie compared these facts with shipping freights, which had advanced from 10s. to 150 s. per ton.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Grealey (Bakers' -Union) said that while the worker, was being conscripted, the capitalist was bleeding the dependents whom the soldier left behind. That was what the capitalist was living for, and the Government was helping him to do it. ." They, are English- men who are starving you," said Mr. Grealey, "not Germans."</p>
        <p>Mr. A. E. Seabury (secretary pro tem. of the Trades Council) said prices were determined in the international market. If the Government fixed a retail price here, the commodities would fail to come across the sea, and would go into the markets where higher prices could be obtained. The only solution at .the moment was an equivalent advance in wages, The resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>Municipal Trading.</p>
        <p>An amendment proposed by Mr. Smith (Blackburn Philanthropic Insurance Society), and suggesting that as against municipal trad- ing the Government should consult with the Co-operative movement, which had done so much to keep down prices, opened a long dis- cussion. He said the municipalities would have to trade through capitalists, and the people would be exploited to a greater extent than at present. Mr. Haley, opposing, said they wanted quick action by the Government with regard to food prices. Mr. Seabury was of opinion that if the Co- operative Societies had the interests of the workers at heart they would abolish dividends and reduce prices accordingly. Only two delegates voted for the amendment. . .</p>
        <p>- A second resolution to take immediate steps to extend .the scope of the conference and its activities by the convening of a further con- ference to be held in one of the large halls locally was adopted, and the following appoinied on a committee for the purpose: Messrs. Berry, Bennett, Elwell, Whitby, Sinclair, Haley, Harrop, Hamblett and Bowen.</p>
        <p>,I.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>NEW COUNTY COURT JUDGE</head>
        <p>THE NEW COUNTY COURT JUDGE</p>
        <p>The newly-appointed County Court Judge should have taken his seat for the first time at the Warrington County Court on Thursday, but at the hour of opening the Registrar (Mr. T. J. Ridgway) announced that owing to the unavoidable absence of his Honour all cases to come before him would be adjourned until the 19th inst. The new Judge, Mr. Narnard Lailey, K.C., has been appointed to succeed Judge Scott Fox, K.C., who has been trans- ferred to the Leeds County Court. Judge Lailey, who was called to the Bar in 1890, and had chambers at Lamb Buildings, Temple, E.C., and 45, Parliament-street, Westminster, has, prior to his appointment here, practised on the Smith- Eastern Circuit. He was formally welcomed at the Birkenhead County Court on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>"Girl wanted for telephone of nice manners and appearance."—" Leicester Mail." "Happy adVertiser ! We have been seeking a telephone like this for years."—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
