      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 7, 1916. JOTTINGS BY AN OLD WORKERS AND THE WAR, DRINK FOR WOUNDED JOURNALIST. SOLDIER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 7, 1916. JOTTINGS BY AN OLD WORKERS AND THE WAR, DRINK FOR WOUNDED JOURNALIST. SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>" Carry On." One hails with extreme satisfaction and pride the assurance that the Warrington Musical Society is not likely to come to a condition of even suspended animation, but that it is to "carry on" this winter as last. All concerned are to be warmly congratulated on the result of the recent deliberations. As to various other places of which I have had experience, in one only has it been possible thus far to continue the concerts in connection with similar organisa- tions. In one case the war has put an end to the hitherto unbroken half-century's career of the local Musical Society, and k famous prize- winning Male Voice Choir in the same district has suffered in like manner. So far as my observation has gone, Warrington is one of the rare and fortunate exceptions. What's in a Name.</p>
        <p>It all depends. In a paragraph in last week's "Woman's World" column, " Marjdrie," writing of simple names for high-born babies, noted incidentally that the Crimean War was responsible for a great many Almas and other Russian names. I am not certain that I have not previously through this medium expressed the opinion that girls would hardly- thank their parents for " dating" them in this manner. I know one professiiinal of that name who is still obliged to do something for her living, and the " Alma "is an embarrassing handicap. I sound this warning note because of an unmis- takable tendency in the like direction nowadays increasingly manifest. Another Hint.</p>
        <p>At the same time there should by plenty of variety, and, indeed, there is scope for origin- ality. Every district of which I know anything has its own common and peculiar surname. Take, for example, Greenwood. I know there are lots of Greenwoods all over the country, but in one district in which I lived some years, one might at any time have tossed a stone hap- hazard in any direction and endangered a Greenwood. Moreover, it seemed to b: an In- variable rule—a point of honour—to name a first-born son "John." Of course, it gave rise to endless mistakes. At house after house in one street lived four of these burdened and heavy-laden John Greenwoods who constantly through inadvertence opened each other's letters, for despairing postmen had long before abandoned the hopeless task of precise identifi- cation. I never came acro-Ss a Matthew or a Mark Greenwood; I knew only one Luke of that ilk; but the Johns were a company whom no man could number, and the name of the consequent errors was Legion. No wonder nick- names abounded in those regions; in fact, they were an indispensable necessity. Nevertheless, I never met or heard of more than one mother thereabout who, to minimise these complications in ever so slight a "degree, would consent to depart from tradition and. call her son "Sydney." In this as in all else, a sense of humour is decidedly hetpful. "The Codlin Moth."</p>
        <p>No doubt those whom it may concern would duly read, mark, learn, and inwardly digest the "Farm and Garden" paragraph under this heading. It strikes me as being pre-eminently and emphatically a case where " Codlin " is not "the friend." The Problem of Flight.</p>
        <p>Last week's paragraph on " Aeronautics " con- cluded "The conquest of the air is not only well lagun; it is much more than half done." When one comes to consider how rapidly things have developed since the problem was solved only a very few short years ago, the effect is quite startling. I refer, of course, to genuine aeroplanes apart from monstrous gasbags. It seems little more than a couple of years ago since Bleriot's crossing of the Channel thrilled the world. One wrote of it twelve Months after the accomplishment of the then sensational feat : " The people who saw him start on his adventurous trip may be counted by the dozen, and when the early morning mists had swal- lowed him up the few spectators turned to each other and said with exultation that they had just seen something which the world would never forget, and which every man alive would have given the world to see'. It was ,as thouglt one had been present at the Battle of Hastings or the signing of Magna Charta, or had seen Nelson die on the deck of the Victory. It was something to think of for ever 'afterwards; a tale to be retold a thousand times'. There was romance in it, and something that could not die."</p>
        <p>And yet so much has happened in the mean- time that, until at this moment I jogged his memory, the average reader had probably for- gotten Bleriot and all his works—and small • blame to him.</p>
        <p>The Great Unpaid. As I read how last week the majesty of the law was especially in a particular instance sup- posed to have been-vindicated by Warrington Justices, I recalled one Bench of Magistrates within the range of my acquaintanceship whose practice before deciding a case was to ask how much money the accused had in, his pockets when arrested, and to 'regulate , the. penalty accordingly. "Any money ?" ," Three and six, your Worships." "Well, prisoner, you'll he fined 3s. 6d. including costs." Thus the farce went on, neither to the terror, of evil-doers nor the praise of them that did well—the police. In some old publication many years ago, I found mercy defined as "The rod with which the noble-minded chastise," and magistrates habitually leaning too much to the side of the hardened culprit caught red-handed are welcome to such consolation as they are able to derive therefrom, along with the consideration, say, of Aristotle's definition of justice, and of the poet's picture beginning—" Impartial justice on the Bench he deals." •</p>
        <p>Waste and Its Opposite. The Bishop's conviction that this is an age of waste, avowed at Warrington Parish Church as reported in last week's issue, set me thinking of other phases of the problem which his Lord- ship does not appear to have taken into account on that occasion. What I have in mind at the moment was once put by a brother scribe thus: one passes along the street and watches men doing various ,jobs one often is struck by the fact that a job is being done in the worst possible way—in a way bad for man, master, and client. The result is waste of effort to no- body's advantage; and if such waste could be avoided there would be a revolution in the economic world. What is, lacking is a sense of fitness: physical fitness, mental fitness, fitness in clothes and in tools."</p>
        <p>Scientific Management. The question is how to get the best out of men, so as to involve better wages and shorter hours as well as ensuring better men. Some time ago I read the story of how Mr. F. W., Taylor, of the Midvale Steelworks in Phila- delphia, introduced "scientific management" into his steelworks with such success that he was asked to introduce it into the works of the Bethlehem Steel Co., which belongs to the Steel Trust. The story was told in the New York "World's Work" by the editor, Mr. A. W. Page, and in it there was a lesson for all who direct or do labour. And it has, too, very great importance for human happiness, as it makes for cheerful breadwinners, and therefore (I write of normal times) happy women and chil- dren and a contented State. I cannot quote from the body of the article, but here are Mr., Taylor's four heads (1) Determine accurately by scientific analysis the elements of each piece of work, and decide how it can best be done. (2) Select men who are fitted for the work—even for the lowest kinds—and train them in the way that has been determined to be the best way of doing that task. (3) By adequate super_ vision and a system of payment which gives th men an inceoive, make sure that the men prac- tise the best methods all the time. (4) Divide the work between the management and the men so that the management does all the work which it can do better than the men. True it un- doubtedly is that from the time of the. Ten Commandments there have been plenty of good principles which would be likely to strike one ah being easier to formulate and to follow than these; 'but there is evidence enough that these have actually stood the test of application. This I have at my elbow as I write. •</p>
        <p>The Sunlight-loser. Kaiser (waking in the night as his sainted Grandfather's clock strikes three) : "The British are just putting their clocks back an hour. I wish I could put ours back about three years."—" Punch."</p>
        <p>R.P..rvl.C. Men Who Were False To Their Duty.</p>
        <p>HEAVY PENALTY. "You are a danger to the State and false to the duties for which you are paid." These remarks were applied by Councillor Henry Roberts, chairman of the magistrates at the Petty Sessions on Wednesday, to two Privates, Lupton and Fallas, of the R.A.M.C., stationed at the Lord Derby War Hospital, who were summoned for procuring intoxicating liquor for g wounded .sbldier in uniform at the Longford Hotel on Thursday, August 10th. Two other summonses, one against the land- lord and the other against his daughter, were also heard. Joseph Horlock, the landlord, was summoned for selling :intoxicating liquor to Private Jas. Davies, of the Oxford and Bucks Regiment, and his daughter, Miss Amy Horlock, was summoned was supplying Same. Mr. T. R. Dootson, solicitor, Leigh, prosecuted on behalf of the County Police, and Mr. T. S. Steel defended. The magistrates supporting Mr. Roberts were Messrs. C. E. Parker and Joshua Ball, and Dr. Latham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>Mr. J. A. Seddon And His Lost Friendships.</head>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Seddon And His Lost Friendships.</p>
        <p>LESSON OF THE TRENCHES</p>
        <p>It was rather a pity that the Royal Court Theatre was not filled on Sunday evening, when matters of moment were trenchantly dealt with at a patriotic meeting convened by the War- rington Branch of the British Workers' National League. Having effectively dealt with the "pacifists," attention was turned to the "plunderers," and the meeting passed a reso- littion condemning those who are exploiting the</p>
        <p>public by the present high prices of foOdstuffs, and calling upon the Government to control the</p>
        <p>• supplies. Mr. James Sexton (Liverpool Dockers' Union) presided, and the principal speakers were Mr. J. A. Seddon, former Labour M.P. for Newton Division, and Mr. Joseph Terrett, London.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that certain agencies were actively at work to create, an impression that this war was no affair of the working classes: that whoever won the war they were bound to be losers, and therefore their interest was to secure peace as quickly as possible, and not to be particular about the terms. They strongly resented the efforts of the pro-German in this country. (Applause.) He noticed there was not a Socialist Branch, a Labour Club or a Co- operative Society in this country which had not been deluged during the last twelve months with a peculiarly obnoxious kind of literature. He knew perfectly well that from no, working-class source whatever were the vast sums raised for this printing, postage and propaganda. It was necessary to challenge the position of these people. "The difference between a pacifist and a member of this organisation," he said, "is this: He is prepared to settle the war on any terms. We have only gat one condition on which we are prepared to settle the war, and that is the complete breakdown of the military power of Prussia." (Applause.) Having con- tended that before the war we were from an economic standpoint drifting to the dogs, he mentioned that out of 247 millions of new capi- tal issued in this country in 1913, only 50 millions were actually invested in Great Britain and Ireland. Unless the national effort which was being thrown into the war was continuPd after the war to reconstruct the British Empire, the' war would have been fought in vain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>MR. SEDDON AND PEACE TALK.</head>
        <p>MR. SEDDON AND PEACE TALK.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Seddon said that after two years of superb valour they were beginning to see a movement which had created in the breast of the German Chancellor, according to his speech in the Reichstag last *eek, first a whine, then a snarl, and lastly a display of his teeth. Endorsing what Mr. Lloyd George had said as to peace interventions. 'Mr. Seddon said that when our men were grimly struggling and hold- ing on the neutrals were silent. We wanted them to remain silent now that victory is com- ing our way. (Applause.) We were determined to be governed only by the conviction that our cause is right, and we would fight to the bitter end, until we had crushed finally and for ever the aspirations of German domination. (Ap- plause.) "I am told," said Mr. Seddon, "that by taking up my present work I am sacrificing old friendships. I would rather lose friendships I than lose my my honour—cheers)—and whatever course I am pursuing at the present time it is a course dictated by the experience of this war, which is changing the view of every right- minded man." (Applause.) There was only one thing that could defeat Britain or her Allies, and that was division amongst the civilian population.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>Spirit of the Trenches.</head>
        <p>The Spirit of the Trenches.</p>
        <p>Mr. Seddon, proceeding, said: "There is an I education' going on in the trenches which ex- cels all the universities •that the hand Of man has created. It is beyond all your sciences, in that it is bringing together every section of the British nation in the community of sacrifice, suffering and death. In those trenelles, the doggerel of Kipling can be -applied with literal truth. Cook's son, duke's son, son of a belted earl '—there they are, companions day by day, and in the presence of Death. Those men are getting an education which is going to be a vital factOr in the reconstruction of our national life, when this horror is past and gone. We have scrapped all our prejudices. There are some things which it is very hard to give up, when one has believed in them all one's life. I have been all my life opposed to military compulsion. But I have been out to France, and what I have seen there has, compelled me to search my own conscience, and ask whether I have been right. The lesson to me," he con- tinued, "is this: When this war is over there must be a change in our military methods and service. Speaking for myself, if it means ex- clusion for ever from the public life of this country, I am not going to have it on my con- science that I was a party to or the assentor in the assassination of another British Army</p>
        <p>like the assassination of the 'Army that defended our honour and saved Paris. Therefore, I have given up what was to me a Cherished convic- tion, in face of the most bloody lesson that this or any other nation has ever had to witness." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Parasitic Conscience.</head>
        <p>The Parasitic Conscience.</p>
        <p>Mr. Seddon spoke scathingly about the national parasite who would avail himself of the security won by others, but who regarded the war as no affair of his. He had protested all his life against various sorts of parasites— the social and commercial parasites—and he had come to the conclusion that he could ,not tolerate the military parasite whose conscience would allow him to feed himself at the price of the blood shed to keep the seas open to the food supply, but whose conscience would not allow him to assist in the work of safeguard- ing the food by which we lived. He referred next to the constructive policy of the British Workers' National League, which had from its inception been setting up machinery to assist the broken soldiers and sailors and the dependents of those who had fallen. He mentioned that the bureau</p>
        <p>which the league had established with this object was receiving assistance from one of Warrington's most distinguished townsmen, Colonel Crosfield—(applause)—who ,had proved by sacrifice his devotion to his country. There must be no cheeseparing policy in the distribu- tion of grants. (Hear, hear.) Dealing with after-war problems, Mr. Seddon urged the importance of making the most of our agricultural' resources. According to bank balances we were the wealthiest nation in the world, but what was our wealth in vital statistics? Had they read the recruiting figures for the early stages of the war? In our thickly. populated towns the rejects in some cases reached the appalling figure of 40 per cent. That was the greatest indictment against our social system before the war. (Hear, hear.) We had to alter those statistics, and it could only be done by having a rural as well as an urban population. We want men returning from the Front to take their part in building up a higher and nobler state of society. The resolution mentioned above was moved by Mr. F. A. Baker and seconded by Mr. W. Brad- bury. The Chairman, in putting it to the meeting, said he had no delukons as to what may happen after the war is over. They might have,to fight the same employers over again, but they would be fortified by the fact that they did not sulk in their tents when the nation was in danger. • The resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>BRICK STREET MISSION SALE OF</head>
        <p>BRICK STREET MISSION SALE OF</p>
        <p>AA ORK.</p>
        <p>A Successful Effort.</p>
        <p>We are pleased to state that the total amount realised at the above sale of work was 020 clear, which the workers considered was highly satisfactory. The following names were inadvertently omitted from the list of stallholders publishe in last week's report:— Drapery Stall.—Mrs. Wright, Mrs. burton and Mrs. Smith.</p>
        <p>War-</p>
        <p>Scholars' lib Stall.—Misses E. and J. Bibby, Messrs. F. Bate, A. Yates Dutton and Master T. Bibby. _ _</p>
        <p>Refreshment Stall.—Mrs. T. Lightfoot, Mrs. IL Sutton, Mrs. W. W. TIM, Miss M. Booth and Miss E. H. Bibby. • A buttonhole was presented to his Worship the Mayor and Councillor g. Henshall by Miss Ivy Forster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LICENSEES SUPERVISION.</head>
        <p>A LICENSEES SUPERVISION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dootson, in opening the case, said the proceedings were taken under the Defence of the Realm Act. The Longford Hotel was much frequented by people who were inmates of the Lord Derby War Hospital. In consequence of complaints being received by the superin- tendent of police, P.C. Lang, in company with P.C. Smith, called in the evening of Thursday, August -10th, at the hotel, and went into the smokeroom. Both officers were in plain clothes and they called for some intoxicants. In the room were 12 soldiers, eight of whom wore the hospital dress of wounded soldiers, the remain- ing four were. R.A.M.C. men, including Privates Lupton and lianas. It was observed by P.C. Lang that Private Davies (one of the w9unded soldiers), handed a silver coin to Plivate Lupton, who immediately left the room and returned with a glass of whisky, which he handed to Davies, who consumed it—not in sips, but in one gulp. Later Private Davies spoke to Private Fallas and asked him. to order another whisky, and Davies was seen to give money to pay for it. The bell was rung and Miss Horlock came into the room and Fallas ordered a glass of whisky, which she handed to Fallas: who, in the presence of the young lady, passed it on to Davies. The second whisky was consumed by the Wounded man. Then it was that Davies noticing how easy it was to procure whisky, though he was wounded, ordered a glass of whisky Mr himself. When the whisky was brought in a significant remark was made by Miss Horlock to the effect that "you like your whisky! Are you a Scotchman?" It was evident that she had noticed that the other glassel had been given to him. The informa- tion of the police officers was that the whisky was drunk in Scotch fashion—neat. He (Mr. Dootson) contended that Mr. Horlock</p>
        <p>had exercised no supervision, and that he was the responsible person to see that .the regula- tions were carried out.</p>
        <p>"MADE A FINE COP THIS TIME."</p>
        <p>P.C. Lang, who is stationed at Atherton, bore out counsel's statement. He saw Davies in the room, wearing the distinctive dress of a wounded soldier. Witness saw Davies hand something to Lupton, who left the room and returned carrying a glass of whisky, which he placed in front of Davies, who drank it. After drinking it Davies turned to Private Pallas, who rang the bell and Amy Horlock came into the room. An order was given for more whisky,' which she brought and handed to Fallas, and he passed it on to Davies in the presence of the young lady. The whisky was paid for by Fallas. The bell was again rung and Miss Horlock again answered and received an order from Davies for a glass of whisky, to which she replied; "You like your whisky. Are you a Scotchman?" The company in the room were all laughing at the way the whisky was drunk.. No water was added; Davies sinaply poured it down. The third whisky waTg' Tiaid fBr 1,1 Davies, who gave her two sixpences. Witn6ss did not see what change was given. The two R.A.M.C. men and Davies were then invited by witness and P.C. Smith to go into the adjoining room, where Davies said, a You have made a fine cop this time?' Fallas and Lupton were told they would be reported for procuring whisky for Davies, to which Lupton replied, "It is the first time we have done it."</p>
        <p>By Mr. Steel: Fallas was in the room when Lupton brought in the first glass of whisky. All three were in, the room when witness Avent there. He did not see Davies have a drink of a n-intoxicating character. He did not see Mr: Horlock serve him with a "small lemon." Neither the landlord nor his daughter was in the room when Lupton handed the whisky to Davies.</p>
        <p>Do you know that Fallas ordered a pint of beer, and when Miss Horlock came back with it at the request of Davies, he ordered some whisky ?—I don't know.</p>
        <p>WHISKY AND BEER A SCOTCH DRINK. Was it not,then that Miss Horlock passed the remark to Fallas, "You are like a Scotchman, having whisky and beer?"—l never heard that remark at all.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Do Seotehmen drink whisky and beer at the same time?</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: I am told they do. It is a com- mon drink in the Army. I don't profess to be an expert on the question.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: It is news to me. Mr. Steel (to witness) : Can you tell me what drink Lupton ordered afterwards F—lle was drinking beer. Was it not whisky? I put it to you that you are confusing another whisky ordered by Lupton with the one which you say was bought by Davies ?—No. In further cross-examinttion, witness said that when they went into an adjoining room Davies never denied anything. He never spoke about anything until he got with the licensee, and then he changed round. The Chairman: Are you positive Davies ordered whisky from Miss llorkTk, who served him with it and received pay for it ?—Yes. Mr. Steel : I understand you to say Davies paid two sixpences for the whisky. What would two sixpences represent for whisky ?—I could</p>
        <p>not Say. Corroborative evidence was given by P.C. Smith.</p>
        <p>WITNESSES GONE THROUGH DELAY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel, in opening .the case for the de- fence, pointed out that he was placed at a great disadvantage in view of the fact that the summonses were not received until seven weeks after the alleged offences had been committed. The witnesses he would have been able to call, whose names and addresses were taken, had re- covered from their wounds and had probably gone back to the fighting line. A number of the wounded men who were in the hotel at a time of the alleged offence he had been unable to trace. He maintained that if he had led the opportunity of calling those witnesses then, would have been such a body of evidence that would have brought the magistrates to the con- clusion that the police officers had made a mis- take. Mr. Horlock had been the licensee of this particular house for 15 years, and had held a licence for 24. years, and this was the first summons that had ever been brought against him. Mr. Horlock had endeavoured, since the new regulations came into force, to carry out the law as strictly as possible, and had given specific instructions with regard to the serving of wounded soldiers. It was not suggested that Mr. Horlock was in the room at the time, or that he ,saw anything of what took place. When Davies first came in the licensee served him with a small lemonade. Davies then com- plained of feeling faint, and asked Lupton to get him a whisky. The Chairman You are up against this fa : that eight men in hospital uniform were in the room, and this ought to have induced the licensee to take particular Care. Mr. Steel: So he did up to the time he wan called away. He could not be in both places. Continuing, Mr. Steel said he wciuld call evidence to prove that it was Lupton who ordered a whisky.</p>
        <p>THE WOUNDED SOLDIER'S VERSION. Private E. J. Davies said he was wounded on March 2nd and arrived at Winwick on March 15th. He was blown up in a trench, which in- jured his spine, with the result th It his right leg was totally paralysed, and his left leg par- tially paralysed. On the date in question he was returning from Warrington to the hospital about seven o'clock. When near the Longford</p>
        <p>Hotel he felt rather dizzy, and went in the smokeroom to sit down. He sat beside Lupton and ordered a lemonade, which was served by Mr. Horlock, only a portion of which he drank. He asked Lupton to get him, a glass of whisky and gave him sixpence. Lupton went to the bar and brought the whisky into the room. Fallas then came in the room and' ordeted a bottle of beer, but as they had none in he had to have a pint of beer. Miss Horlock took the order and left' the room. Whilst she was out he asked Fallas to get him a glass of whisky, and he gave him a shilling. On the return of Miss Horlock, Fallas gave the order for the whisky, which caused her to remark, "You are like a Scotchman, having your Whisky with your beer." Witness drank the whisky. Miss Hor- lock was not in the room when he drank it. Re never gave a direct order for whisky to the land- lord or any other. person. The remark of Miss Horlock about the Scotchman was made to Fallas and not to hikt. Afterwards he heard Lupton give an order for whisky to Miss Hor- lock, and Lupton paid for it. On September , 27th he was interviewed by a police inspector at Winwick, and he made a voluntary state- ment. In the statement no mention was made with regard to the licensee, whereupon the in- spector referred to Mr. Horlock. Witness added a postscript to the statement to the effect that he emphatically denied that the licensee or his daughter served him with whisky or saw it handed to him. By Mr. Dootson: _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>If whisky had been ordered, only by Fallas and Lupton can you suggest any reason why the officers should get up ?--I have already admitted that whisky was served to Lupton and Fallas for me, and a further one was ordered by Lupton for himself.</p>
        <p>Who ordered the beer and whisky ?—Fallas; but I drank the whisky. Was there any mixing 9—No. If there was no mixing what need was there for Miss Horlock to say, "You are like a Scotch- drink.:cag whisky and beer ?":--I suppose she said that because the majority of Scotch- men generally do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>"A SERIOUS THING."</head>
        <p>"A SERIOUS THING."</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Were you aware of the fact that liquor being supplied to you involved some- one in a breach of the law ?—I was quite aware of that.</p>
        <p>Were you aware that you were getting these men into serious trouble ?—I was not aware.</p>
        <p>Although you were inducing them to break the law ?—I was not inducing them to break the law. I asked them to get me one because I felt dizzy and bad. Do you think it was quite fair to put these men in jeopardy by asking • them to do some- thing for you which would involve them in serious punishment if caught?-1 would not have got whisky if I had not needed it. Was it a fair thing for you to get comrades into this serious trouble ?—No, it was not.</p>
        <p>Two officers have sworn distinctly that they saw you order, supplied, drink and pay for the whisky. Do you suggest that is a misstatement of fact ?—I say that statement is untrue. Private Thos. Lupton testified to bringing the first glass of whisky for Davies. Pallas ordered a second ft.r Davies frora Miss Horlock, hut she was not in the room when it wam handed to Davies. The third whisky was for himself, not as the police state, for Davies. He never drank the whisky, because he was called out of the room by the officers. By Mr. Dootson: 'Why didn't you return to the room and drink it P—l had had enough, sir.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: What do you mean by enough? You realised the serious position you had got yourself into ?—Yes. The Chaiiman: Didn't you realise what a serious thing you were asked to do ?—Not at first, but I do now. Herbert Fallas corroborated the previous wit- ness's statement. He ordered the second glass of whisky for Davies. Miss Horlock was not present when he handed it to Davies. The re- marks about a Scotchman were passed to him by Miss Horlock. He had had beer and whisky mixed several times before. Davies drank the whisky.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>DENIALS.</head>
        <p>DENIALS.</p>
        <p>Private, Thomas Smith, of the Manchester Regiment, who was on a visit to his brother-in- law in Warrington, said he was positive that Miss Horlock was out of the room when the whiskies ordered by the two men of the R.A.M.C. were handed to Davies. He heard Lupton give another order for whisky. Edward Bibby, 287, Winwick-road, brother- in-law of , the previous witness, corroborated, his account of what took place in the smoke- MOM.</p>
        <p>• Miss Amy Horlock denied that she served Davies with a whisky. She remarked to Pallas "You are like a Seotehman with your whisky and your beer." As far as she knew Fallas had the whisky. The only wounded soldiers she served were when orders were given for hop bitters and lemonade. Joseph Horlock said when the officers called his attention to the alleged offence, Davies, .1n their presence, denied that his daughter had served him, but admitted that Lupton and Fallas had ordered whisky for him. By Mr. Dootson : Did anyone try to prevent the police from getting away afterwards?—No,</p>
        <p>but I heard someone shout to them that they ought to be in khaki. A DANGER TO THE STATE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, addressing Lupton and Fallas, said: "The -Bench are unanimously of the opinion that you men, holding a postion of such responsibility and trust, have been guiltl of as grave an Offence as possible. We cannot find any palliation or circumstance to reduce what in our minds is a most grave matter. We have had doubts in our minds whether We should send you both 'to prison without the option of a fine. As it is the first case of the kind we have decided to impose upon you a tte of £lO each, or one month's imprisonment. hope it will prevent any other cases of a like kind. There is absolutely no excuse for Youir conduct. You are a danger to the State an° false to the duties or which you are paid." With regard to Mr. Horlock they, did 110" think he was personally cognisant of the offence:. and the charge would be dismissed on paymellb lof costs. They were sorry that they were cora" pelled to find Miss Horlock guilty, and a fine of £5 would be imposed. On the application of Mr. Dootson special costs and advocate's fee were granted.</p>
      </div>
