      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>IJ:dim toot Oita</p>
        <p>THE "MAJESTY " OF THE LAW-</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have just read your leading article under the above heading, with every line of which article I entirely concur. It is to be hoped that those whom it may immediately concern will, in the words of the familiar Collect of our Church, "read, mark, learn, and inwardly digest" it. If it is not presumptuous in me to do so, I should like to compliment the writer of the article on what, according to my judgment, is a concise and terse example of a piece of good English prose. H. J. WESTBROOK. " Newlands," Lower Walton, Saturday, 30th September, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CONCERTS FOR THE MAWNIAA).</head>
        <p>CONCERTS FOR THE MAWNIAA).</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have received a letter from Mr. Sydney Nieholson, organist of Manchester cathedral, and Director of the Entertainments in Hospitals Society, asking me if I would act local secretary, and perhaps you will allow In% through the medium of your valuable Paper, to bring the matter to the notice of the local organists, choirmasters, musicians, and any ladies and gentlemen interested in the above. I should be glad if any lady or gentleman Willing to help will communicate with me on or before Thursday next, 12th October, in order that I may make arrangements to call a meeting at an early date to place the scheme before them. • - W. B. RANDALL. 7, Hanover-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>ARE INTOXICANTS " WASTE " ?</head>
        <p>ARE INTOXICANTS " WASTE " ?</p>
        <p>Sir,—The Lord Bishop of Burnley, during the course of his harvest sermon, denounced the " waste" of barley used for brewing, as being "devoted for the ruin of the health of the body_ and of moral life." I have before me a Manifesto signed by fourteen leading authorities on medicine, chemistry and dietetics, which' aPpeared in the " Lancet " some years ago. The signatories state that in their opinion: "In disease alcohol is a rapid and trustworthy restorative, and in many cases it may truly, be described as .life-preserving." They also ex- press their approval of alcohol "as an article of diet." Since this manifesto was first issued its conclusion's have been repeatedly confirmed by the pronouncements of our greatest scientists and physicians. Not only is the Moderate use of alcohol justified from the health point of view, but from the motal Standpoint it has the sanction of the Christian Church and the Scriptures. One text will suffice, but "there are many more to be found equally strong. In Proverbs we read: "Give strong drink unto him that is ready to perish, and unto those that beo,ef, heavy hearts. Let him drink and forget his poverty,' and remem- ber his misery no more." -</p>
        <p>J. E. SIMPSON. Birkenhead. October 2nd, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>"GREAT CONFESSIONS OF FAITH."</head>
        <p>"GREAT CONFESSIONS OF FAITH."</p>
        <p>Rev. Irvine Lister And The Influence</p>
        <p>Of St. Augustine.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning the Rev. Irvine Lister delivered the first of a series of addresses on a Great Confessions of Faith" at Cairo-street Chapel. His subjgct was, "The Confessions of St. Augustine." After speaking of the place and importance of Augustine in the history of the Early Church, and showing how he had helped to mould and strengthen the orthodox faith, Mr. Lister went on to speak of his most famous book, "The Confessions." This, he said, had become a classic of devotion, and Whilst everyone could not agree with all that Was in it—for example, Augustine's view of sin and man's nature—yet all could derive benefit from a careful and sympathetic reading. The keynote of the whole book was that of a moral struggle, a struggle carried on by Augustine throughout his whole life. He had, in early 3rears, lived a wild and thoughtless life, passing from school to university, and indulging in all the follies of his times, yet never able to forget that he was born for a high and noble purpose. Through the sainted Ambrose and his devoted niother, Monica, he came under the influence of. Christian thought and sentiment, and gradually, but surely, he was claimed for Christian service. Mr. Lister read Augustine's account of his own conversion, and likened it tO that of St. Paul—a great moral and spiritual upheaval, resulting in peace of soul. "This book should be read by members of all Christian Churches for the simple reason that it reveals all the struggles of a soul after the light, and because it shows how one of the greatest Christian thinkers entered into the peace which Christian faith and truth can give." Next Sunday morning Mr. Lister will speak on Cardinal Newman's "Apologia." Alteration of Time of Service.</p>
        <p>"We have decided," writes the Rev. Irvine Lister, "to hold our service at 3.30 p.m. instead of 6.30 because of the difficulty older people have in getting about at night-time. We are having a young people's service in the schoolroom at 7 p.m?,</p>
