        <p>• iisnamii. ,</p>
        <p>—"'"womwwe.v..•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WALKING STICKS FOR WOUNDFD SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>WALKING STICKS FOR WOUNDFD SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Royston's Help.</p>
        <p>The members and well-wishers of the "Pansy League "'continue to show their practical inter- est in Mr. Stewart Royston's Walking Stick Fund for Wounded Soldiers by sending a fur- ther contribution amounting in all to £l7 4s. 7d., for his deserving object. If the conductor of this League, which aims at training our boys and girls to be thoughtful for each other, can influence its members in this unselfish manner, it is deserving our whole-hearted encouragement. We notice that its able editor, Mr. G. IT. North- croft, has introduced a new rule allowing grown- ups to enrol themselves as associates of "Great Thoughts Pansy League on condition that at least one shilling is sent to Mr. Royston's Walk- ing Stick Fund. In giving the address of the Pansy League, Temple House, 37-38, Tallis- street, London, we do so in the hope that many' readers of "The Examiner" will lend a help- ing hand to provide more walking sticks, when they are so urgently needed in every place where a military hospital is -to be found.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Gift from Australia.</head>
        <p>A Gift from Australia.</p>
        <p>Mr. Royston has received the following letter this week:—</p>
        <p>"27, Westbourne-street, Sydney, N.S.W., Australia, August 2nd, 1916. "Dear sir,---Enclosed please find money order for 55., being a small gift towards the 'Wounded Soldiers' Walking Stick Fund.' It will per- haps interest you to know that my husband and I lived in Warrington five years ago, and he was a member of the 'Pansy League.' He has taken 'Great Thoughts in for years, and still continued to do so after we were married (a few months ago). We thoroughly enjoy the paper, and think there is none other to beat it. We also receive 'The Examiner each week from an old friend in Warrington, and saw particulars of the 'Walking Stick• Fund in both, so thought we would like to add our mite to it. My husband has had the pleasure of talking to you, but it was years ago, and I expect you would not remember him now. This, however, made him more than ever interested to watch the progreqs of the 'Fund,' and to read of your untiring efforts for our brave soldiers. We both wish that every effort you put forth may be crowned with success, and, with best wishes, we remain, yours sincerely, JAMES &amp; MAY STRINGER." Walking Stick Fund Authorised.</p>
        <p>Mr. Royston having made inquiries as to hi position in view of the passing of the War Charities Act, has received the following : "British Red Cross Society, Headquarters Cpllection Committee, 83, Pall Mall, London, S.W., Sept. Bth, 1916. "Dear sir,—ln reply to your letter of the 25th ultimo in reference to the War Charities Act, as the Joint War Committee of the British Red Cross Society and the Order of St. John is regis- tered under the Act, we are able to give you authority to continue your collections on our behalf, in accordance with our Standing Orders, in regard to collections, a copy of which is en- closed.</p>
        <p>"Thanking you for all the work you are doipg for the cause Of the sick and wounded, for which my committee is most grateful, I am, Yours faithfully, CHARLES RUSSELL, Chairman Collec- tions Committee."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CHEAPER HOUSES</head>
        <p>CHEAPER HOUSES</p>
        <p>Needed, But Time For Action Not ,Opportune.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes , Committee submitted to the Town Council on Tuesday evening the report of the sub-committee appointed to con- sider the proposed establishment by the regis- trar of the land registry of local offices in large towns for the registration of title under the Land Transfer Acts.</p>
        <p>"Your committee," said the brief document, "beg to report that after carefully considering the representations of the Registrar of the Land Registry and the views of the local Law Society and of the Warrington Property Owners' Association, they are of opinion that the time is not opportune for the establishment of a local registry in this town." The minute was, among others, adopted, but Alderman Bennett said he hoped it would be understood that the report merely meant that the time was not opportune for setting up a local registry. So far as the main question was con- cerned we should all do everything we could to make the acquisition of land as easy as pos- sible by as many people as possible, in these days of scarcity of houses. All were eager as to the settlement of a serious social problem. Knowing what they did respecting the housing question, no one would be against considering any method of making it easier to supply suit- able houses at prices within the reach of the average person. . .</p>
        <p>It was formally reported that at a meeting of the General Purposes Committee, a letter was read from the Warrington Trades and Labour Council with regard to the housing question.</p>
        <p>To Oblige the Doctor. Doctor: "A little building-up required, 1 think. Suppose you try a glass of port in the forenoon and' say another after lunch?" The Major: "Well, I don't think I've drunk less than a bottle a day for the last ten years. But I'll try and take another couple of glasses if you like."—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>4. • 9 • 4. WOMAN s WORLD, •</head>
        <p>4. • 9 • 4. WOMAN s WORLD, •</p>
        <p>Wash Market Food. The Pennsylvania Health Commissioner warns against eating raw food unless it is thoroughly washed. "Care should be exer- cised in the preparation and serving of -green foods, as they are subject to much handling between the garden and the tablg. Unless the hands through which they pass are absolutely clean they are more or less contaminated. Food exposed for sale in markets is often subject to indiscriminate handling by prospective pur- chasers, and is seldom properly protected from dust and dirt. As a protection berries and stuffs eaten raw should be thoroughly washed before being served. It is far better to risk a slight impairment of the flavour than to chance eating unclean foods.”</p>
        <p>Cream Sponge Cake. Mix a teaspoonful of baking powder with a teacupful of flour, and one of castor sugar. Break three eggs into the flour, &amp;c., beat to a light batter, and flavour with vanilla essence. Pour into fancy patty Pans, and bake in a very quick oven. Turn on to a gieve to cool. Whip half a pint of thick cream, flavoured and sweetened to taste. Scoop out a hollow in the top of each sponge cake, fill in with cream, which should rise above the level of the cake to a point. Garnish round the cream with small strips of angelica arid glace cherries. ' Baby's Bones.</p>
        <p>A baby should never be allowed to sit up until the spine has become strong. At first it can do this for only a short time, when it be- comes tired; if the little one is unrestrained and free from any fetters of either straps r pillow props it will roll gently over on its side, but if it is strapped up in a high chair or pram or is propped up with pillows so that it cannot lie down when the feeble spine becomes too tired to hold it up, it will fall forward into most uncomfortable position. Much harm re- sults from the too early use of high-chair and perambulator. If forced to walk' too soon by being dragged along by one hand, it will likely flevelop some form of curvature of the spine which will for ever after mar the form and lay the foundation for much ill-health. Bowlegs often result from urging the little one to stand or walk while the bones of the legs are still soft. It is well for parents to remember that every normal child will nse his powers as fast as they develop and in accord with his strength, and in thus using them will do himself no harm. The Useful Potato.</p>
        <p>The potato is probably more valuable as a special diet food than any other food known. Its real value has not been half appreciated hitherto. It has long been known that the potato is of special value -in the treatment of rheumatism and gout, but the importance of the potato as a means of supplying alkaline substances to neutralise the abnormal acids which constantly tend to develop in the boay has. not been appreciated until ' recently. Through the experiments of Hindhede, the eminent Danish physiologist, it is now known ' 'hat the potato is one of the most useful of all food übstances. Dctor Hindhede found that it wa Jossible to live exclusively upon potatoes, with the addition of some wholesome fat, not only for a few days, but for months, and even years. —Dr. Kellog</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>The Lady Playwright Agent. Women are now occupying responsible posi- tions in the theatrical world, hitherto considered beyond the capability of feminine brains. Some are in management and others have established play agency businesses. Some years ago Miss Elizabeth Marbury started a busi- ness venture of this character, and has since carried it on most successfully, but it is only lately that authors have discovered the lady play agent, and have found it to their advan- tage to place their work with her rather than to send manuscripts to managers. Managers are also availing themselves of the serviccs of lady agents, for although women may lack the creative sense necessary to produce successful dramatic work, they are excellent critics, and are able to visualise a play on reading it, with greater facility than men, whilst their quicker perceptions prove valuable in the business of a reader of plays</p>
        <p>Women in Dockyards. Women are proving themselves capable of successfully "carrying on" in trades and call- ings demanding physical strength and endur- ance. Women dockers are working in nearly all the ports—London excepted. Nearly two hundred are employed in shipyards as cleaners on liners, in electrical works, and in stores. Women are repairing ships, and doing the work of men rivet-heaters, discharging timber ships, and doing barrow work. One firm of ship- builders deeided to offer training in their mill- wright, blacksmith, and plating shops to fifty women. That number was quickly made up, and becoming overalls ad caps specially de- signed for the purpose were given to each woman. The experiment proved so successful that other firms followed this example, and now woman are assisting in drilling, caulking, carpentering and grinding, whilst others gather scrap iron, and do much of the labour in the dry docks. Gloves and Footwear.</p>
        <p>Gloves are much dearer this autumn than last, and as there is every prospect of a con- siderable rise in present prices, it'is advisable to buy now for the coming winter. The British fabric glove has vastly improved during the last year or two, both in the quality of the material used, and shape, but the price of this is higher by at least sixpence a pair. The thicker makes of leather are to be largely used, in dark, useful shades, more especially in grey, brown and fawn. The gauntlet glove—always so smart and warm—is to have a vogue later on. Already it is shown in glace kid in colours that go remarkably well with fur coats. The finer qualities of leather used in normal times for women's footwear are not now available for this purpose, and no doubt footwear will rise in price as time goes on. Here again to buy now will be wise, even it there be no special need at the moment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>•:* •:• •:•• •:* •:* •:* • • •:* •;• •:• •:* •:* • Children 8 Corner. •</head>
        <p>•:* •:• •:•• •:* •:* •:* • • •:* •;• •:• •:* •:* • Children 8 Corner. •</p>
        <p>• • • • • • Conducted by UNCLE JACK. . • • • 4.4 4. 4c. •4.</p>
        <p>THE BATTLE OF THE SOMME.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,— There have not been so many attempts made to give an opinion of .the great battle in France as I thought there would be. My boys especially have failed to come forward. Surely they have an opinion about the great things that are being done for us in France. A few weeks ago I got a kindly hint that the nieces were winning all the prizes. Of course, if they send in all the letters, or nearly all the letters, they must get most of the prizes. This week the prize goes to • 903 Lily McLachlan,</p>
        <p>8, Ash Grove, Latchford, who attends the Bolton Council School. I should say the children of the Bolton School are taught to be neat and methodical. Niece Lily's letters are always well written and neat and clean. This applies to most of the letters, that come from scholars at the same school. Other schools in town deserve the same praise; but some letters I get are written in such an untidy, careless manner as to make me think the writers do not care whether they succeed or not. It is really im- portant to be careful and to show a real inter- est in the subject about which you write. This week I will ask you to write me a letter on .</p>
        <p>Zeppelins. • There are many things to' be said about them at the present time.</p>
        <p>Your loving UNCLE JACK</p>
        <p>THE WINNING LETTER, By No. 903 Lily McLachlan, 8, Ash Grove; Latch- ford; age 12; Bolton Council School. My Opinion of the Battle of the Somme.</p>
        <p>I think the Battle of the Somme is one of the most important battles in history. Fierce attacks are made by the British and French, and are continued almost without interruption. The Germans strove to break through our grand line and spoil our position on the west of the river. By the help of our armed "tanks," which are plated with steel, our gallant men are able to go forward and gain numerous trenches, 'and so have not the same number of machine guns to face. I think our men must be brave and dar- ing to face death like they are doing, and to hear the big guns which have caused a great many men to be deaf. The Germans must be an</p>
        <p>awful enemy, because they throw poisonous gas bombs and liquid fire in our trenches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</head>
        <p>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</p>
        <p>Niece Amy Campbell, 56, Lancaster-street (Evelyn-street School) :—" The victory of the Somme is a very fine achievement. The Ger- mans themselves speak of it as the greatest and heaviest battle in history. The most important point about this victory is that the French and British possess all the highest points of the sur- rounding country, and'are therefore able 'to fire on the enemy below. For this victory great praise is due to our Commander-in-Chief, General Sir Douglas-Haig, our most superb artil- lery, and the last and most wonderful invention, the tanks."</p>
        <p>Niece Eva M. M orrall, 47, Prlce-street : "From what I read and hear I judge this great Somme victory to be just the beginning of our more violent and effective advance. It is also a fine result of the unity of the Allies, and their superiority in men and guns." Nephew Fred Rathbone, 112, Algernon-street (Parochial School):—" I think that the British have been doing very good work during the battle which is called the Battle of the Somme. The battle, I think, has been very dreadful." Nephew Frank Overend, 74, Lythgoes-lane, says the vigtory is a great honour to our sol- diers who have won it.</p>
        <p>Niece Maud Lunny, 108 A, Bridge-street, sends a nice account of a picture house film of the Somme Battle.</p>
        <p>Niece Emily Marshall, 183, Winwick-road, says :—" The Germans have been thoroughly beaten and routed. We will have to fight on through the war the same as the Somme. Every man fought bravely, and I think we should, at least, praise them. Many lords' sons have fallen on the Somme; cooks' sons and dukes' sons have both fallen together." Niece Elsie Johnson, 323, Lovely-lane, says the battle has been long and furious, but our men believe in the motto: "We will fight to the finish."</p>
        <p>Niece Doris Whittaker, 62, Baxter-street, says it must be awful to see them fighting out there. The Germans have had a lot to fight against, but they will have to have more before they have finished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>"A BEAUTIFUL TALE."</head>
        <p>"A BEAUTIFUL TALE."</p>
        <p>"47, Price-street, Warrington. Dear Uncle Jack,—l wish to thank you for my beautiful prize. Mother has read it. She says it is a most beautiful tale.—l remain, your loving niece, EVA M. MORRALL."</p>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS. 1014 Colin Campbell, 56, Lancaster-street; age</p>
        <p>1015 William Watkinson 7, Heaton-street; age 12; Hamilton-street School. 1016 ,Mary V. Flynn, 26, Pierpoint-street, Bewsey; St. Albin's School.</p>
        <p>anthem was repeated in the afternoon. Mr. F. Rose was at the organ.</p>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>The Vicar (the Rev. G. Vale Owen) was the preacher at both the morning and evening ser- vices at the Orford Church harvest thanksgiving services on Sunday, while Mr. C. A. Littler, of Great Sankey, addressed the children in the afternoon. At the evening service the anthems, "Thine, 0 Lord, is the .greatness" and "Ye shall dwell, in the land" were rendered by the choir, the solos being :taken by Mr. J. Gibson (organist) and Master J. Ellis.,</p>
        <p>WORKING MEN'S MISSION, BANK QUAY.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services of the Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay, were held on Sun- day. The preacher was Mr. F. C. Middlehurst, of St. Helens. In the afternoon a musical service was held, presided Over by Mrs. James Dodds, of. Penketh, and an address was deliv- ered by the Rev. C. P. Pitt, of'Elmwoothavenue Congregational Church. Solos were rendered by Miss Snelling ("But the Lord is mindful" and "Night is the coming of our dreams") and Mr.• George Gough ("Behold I stand" and "The mighty river"). The choir rendered the anthem "0 give thanks" and joined Miss E. Tanner in the solo and chorus "Hear my prayer." At the evening service the choir gave a good interpretation of the .anthem "He watereth the hills." Mr. T. Tanner was at the organ, and Mr. F. Atkinson was an able con- ductor.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a fruit banquet was held, over which Mr. T. Dale presided. Songs and duets were rendered by the Misses Thomas, H. Taylor, E. Oakley, 0. Dale, E. Millington and D. Tilling, and recitations by Miss L. Rigby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>SALVATION ARMY.</head>
        <p>SALVATION ARMY.</p>
        <p>Harvest festival services were continued in the Salvation Army Hall, Academy-street, on Saturday, Sunday and Monday. On Sunday afternoon "Christ and his soldiers" was given by the Latchford Wesleyan Choir, under the conductorship of Mr. N. Cook, the pianist being Miss Foden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>EMPIRE FARMERS TO HELP ALLIES.</p>
        <p>The Agricultural Relief of Allies' Fund, pro- moted for the reinstatement of the ruined farmers of our Allies, has taken definite steps to further its objects -throughout the Empire: Dr. J. W. Robertson, of Canada, the Hon. A. J. Fuller (former Minister of Agriculture for South Africa), Senator Keating (Australia), and Mr. Charles Elgar (New Zealand), who have just returned from a visit—arranged by the French Government—on behalf of the fund to the devastated farming lands behind Verdun and on the Somme, have been elected members of the committee, and have discussed measures for embracing the practical participation offered by farmers in the Overseas Dominions. , The newly-appointed members of the com- mittee on their imminent return have under- taken to inaugurate an active campaign on be- half of the fund in each of*the Dominions. The fund, which is under the patronage of the King and the presidency of the Duke of Portland, thus becomes fully representative of agricul- tural interests throughout the Empire. The subscriptions of English farmers today stand at £100,674, which has been raised mainly by means of gift sales in farming centres.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>LIFTING POTATOES.</p>
        <p>The lifting of potatoes that have reached their full size is very often postponed, more especially if the weather is dry, consequently many people are caught napping, a wet time occurs, with the result that the tubers grow out or disease sets in, this developing after the tubers have been lifted and stored.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SELECTING CACTUS DAHLIAS.</head>
        <p>SELECTING CACTUS DAHLIAS.</p>
        <p>When visiting the dahlia shows how often does one see persons jotting down the names of varieties that take their fancy? Far better would it be that those desiring to add some of the newer varieties to their collection should visit a nursery where dahlias are largely grown and see the plants in flower. All too often the flower that one selects at the show is, when growinc, on the plant, never seen owing to its' short ;talk and habit of hiding itself in the foliage. The moral is obvious. Do not select at the shows, but see the plants growing before ordering varieties that take your fancy.</p>
        <p>THE LOGAN BERRY FOR PRESERVING.</p>
        <p>Fo? this purpose I think the logan berry is really more valuable than for dessert, as the fruits can be 'gathered when not quite ripe and are of a delicious flavour. It is less sweet than the raspberry or strawberry and for this reason is preferred by many people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ROOM PLANTS.</head>
        <p>ROOM PLANTS.</p>
        <p>Many who grow plants in the house often find a difficulty as 'regards the watering. The days are now getting shorter and the sun has but little power, yet the plants are watered as usual, no thought being given to the fact that the demand on the roots is now much, less ,than it was in the summer. At this season before watering a plant, tap the side of the pot, and if a hollow sound is produced then the plant is dry and water is required. The best way to do this is to stand the pot in a ,tub of water, allowing it to remain there until the ,bubbles have ceased to rise.—" Wick," in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LIFE SAVED BY A BICYCLE.</head>
        <p>LIFE SAVED BY A BICYCLE.</p>
        <p>How a boy's life was saved by his bicycle is told in an incident froM Wokingham. A Raleigh carrier was being ridden by a Woking- ham tradesman's boy, when a steam tractor with heavy timber lorry attached, collided with it. The front wheel was twisted completely round, and the rider was jammed between the top rail 'of he carrier and the top tube of the bicycle, but not a single crack or break appeared in the tubing, notwithstanding the immense strain. The advance of the tractor was stopped. The rider escaped with only a few bruises and the shock following naturally upon seeing a huge tractor coming apparently on top of him. Had the tubing of the bicycle given at all the engine would undoubtedly have gone over the boy and probably have killed him. It is a remarkable testimony to the im- mense strength of the all-steel bicycle and the wonderful workmanship which ,is put into •the Raleigh at the great works at Nottingham.</p>
        <p>I=!MM</p>
      </div>
