      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 7, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 7, 1916.</p>
        <p>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>UNMERITED TREATMENT. PROMOTIONS AND DISTINCTIONS.</head>
        <p>UNMERITED TREATMENT. PROMOTIONS AND DISTINCTIONS.</p>
        <p>CoLincillors Protest Against Unfair Proposal.</p>
        <p>AN OLD SORE.</p>
        <p>A proposal to re-elect all the existing Town Council Committees for the ensuing year was "sent back" for further consideration by the monthly meeting of the Council on Tuesday evening. The proposal came from the General Purposes Committee, who recommended</p>
        <p>That the committees as constituted by the Council on the 9th Novmber, 1315, be re-appointed for the year 1916-17.</p>
        <p>There was a preliminary canter when the minutes of the Gymnasium Committee were submitted for approval. These minutes in- cluded the recommendation to appoint Alder- man James Evans on the Gymnasium Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE MEN NEEDED.</p>
        <p>Councillor G. Dale said he felt sure Alderman Evans would make a ,valuable member of the committee, but his appointment would be con- trary to the spirit, intention, and letter of Standing Order 32, which stated that no one except the Mayor should serve on more than eight committees and Alderman Evans was already on eight committees.</p>
        <p>The Mayor Alderman Evans does not wish to be on this committee, but I asked him to serve as it is so difficult to get a quorum. Councillor Dale: I am asking whether it is in order, but apart from that I think his hands are full enough, while there are other members of • the Council. who would be uSeful on the committee.</p>
        <p>The Mayor again explained that the attend- ance at the Gymnasium Committee was so poor that they had a difficulty in getting a quorum. He (the Mayor) thought the difficulty would be solved by putting Alderman Evans on the committee, as he attended another com- mittee the same evening and could attend this one without trouble.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough: Would he have to resign from one of his other committees if he accepted this?</p>
        <p>Alderman James Evans: I withdraw, Mr. Mayor. Let the thing drop. Alderman Tinnion said he formerly had the honour of being Chairman .of the Gymnasium Committee for many years and they never found it difficult to get a quorum. The work appeared to be more interesting then than now. Ile did not say the institution was not doing the same amount of good now, but for some .reason the young men and girls did not seem to take quite as di,,ep an interest as they used to do since the Gymnasium ' had been largely taken over by the Education Committee.</p>
        <p>Councillor Wilson said he would be sorry if Alderman Evans withdrew his name. The meetings of the committee had been fixed for a time at which it was impossible for him :the speaker) to -attend. Alderman Evans was on the spot when the meetings were held and he thought they could look over the Standing Order in this particular case.</p>
        <p>Alderman James Evans: I don't want the job.</p>
        <p>Councillor Wilson: But we want you to have it. Everyone seems to fight shy of this com- mittee..</p>
        <p>Asked by the Mayor whether he insisted upon his objection, Councillor Dale replied that he did.</p>
        <p>Councillor Henry- Roberts, replying to Alderman Tinnion, . said that whereas formerly certain selected schools or scholars from a limited number of schools, received a certain amount of physical instruc- tion, to-day physical instruction is given in all our schools; and while it was not of the high quality given by the Gymnasium Committee in special cases it is much more general in its application and the average instruction per scholar is better than it was. • Alderman Tinnion explained that he went back to the time when the committee had the whole of the schools under their supervision. Councillor Roberts probably forgot that in the early part of that time their instructors went round to all the schools and instructed the teachers in their duties. Afterwards a change was made, but certain favoured schools in the neighbourhood of the Gymnasium were allowed instruction there. Then there was another change, and while he agreed that suitable in- struction is given to the whole of the children now, he held that it is not as good as it was.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>COMMITTEE MEN NOT NEEDED.</head>
        <p>COMMITTEE MEN NOT NEEDED.</p>
        <p>The minutes, with the exception of that re- ferring to Alderman James Evans, were adopted. The Mayor proposed and the Deputy-Mayor seconded the adoption of the minutes of the General Purposes Committee. Councillor J. G. Craik moved as an amend- ment that the minuta dealing with the re- appointment of all the committees be referred back. He did not consider the proposal fair, and he protested against it; and he thought the ratepayers would protest against it, too. He understood there was only half the General Purposes Committee present when the minute was passed, and it ought not to bif allowed to stand.</p>
        <p>Councillor' G. Dale seconded. I was, he said, under a misapprehension at the General Pur- poses Committee when it was passed. Now 1 see it in its clear light I am prepared to' second this amendment. What is proposed is quite 'contrary to what has already been said upon the point I raised respecting Alderman Jas. Evans and the Gymnasium Committee. It was stated to be a difficult matter to obtain a quorum on a certain committee, and that obtains on a number of committees. I think it is absolutely wrong in principle to proceed on the lines proposed; especially when there are quite a number of committees-on which certain members never attend.</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph said that when he seconded the adoption of the General Purposes Committee he did not notice this particular minute. He quite saw the justice of the objection raised, and he thought the matter should be reconsidered and, if necessary, amended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>ELEMENTARY JUSTICE.</head>
        <p>ELEMENTARY JUSTICE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts: I support the amend- ment, Mr. Mayor. Unless it is carried, the •Council will not have the opportunity of doing me an act of elementary justice. I was, in my absence, removed from three important com- mittees two years ago. Why, I do not know to this day. I had attended those committees reasonably well, and on two of them, at any rate, I had helped to contribute to a certain amount of commercial management—(hear, hear)—which obtains even pow. I do not think the majority of the members of the Coun- cil, at any rate, wish either to deprive the town of such services as mine may be worth or to do me personal unkindness. At any rate, lam not going to believe it until it is confirmed a third time. As a sort of compensation to' me I was pat upon a committee, without being asked, of which I had no knowledge, and I have never attended; but it seems to me that it would be a piece of folly to continue me as a member of a committee which I never attended and do not intend to attend until this other mattet is put right. If the Council choose to say to me "We don't want you on any committee,!' very well, I will remain outside as an ordinary member of the Council. But as no reasons were given at all—l was simply left out—l felt very keenly indeed the unmerited treatment I received two years ago, and I am going to give the Council, by direct vote, an opportunity of repeating it or putting it right. I hope the sense of fairness and responsibility which actuates the majority of the members will show itself when the time comes. (Hear, hear.) The Mayor, for the information of those who were unable to attend the General Purposes Committee, said that at that meeting it was pointed out, though not by himself, that the re- election of the committees would save the necessity of printing special, notices or of hold- ing a caucus meeting of the Council before November 9th. It was on a par with the sus- pension of municipal elections, councillors hav- ing another year added to their term of office without the wishes of the ratepayers being con- sulted. It was true that some members had not attended the various committees. Everyone, however, had a chance of discussing the matter at the meeting of the General Purposes Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts: We did not know it was coming on.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: It was on the agenda. The resolution was referred back by eight votes to four.</p>
        <p>• The first definite news that Private Taylor's gallantry had been rewarded•was received at his home in Kendrick-street on Sunday morning, when the post brought the following letter from him</p>
        <p>"I am sure you will all be proud to hear that I have been awarded the D.C.M. I know it will be a great surprise to you all; it was to me. They sent for me and Major Woods shook hands and said, lam proud of you.' I have not been given the ribbon yet. I expect they are waiting till we go on rest and then it will be given to me by the General. I will let you know how Igo on later. My hand is at present nearly wrung off. My officer, who is very good to me, was nearly overcome with joy. Now all keep cool and try to keep it quiet." ' A Trinity School Boy.</p>
        <p>Private Taylor, who was 21 years of age last July, was in the machine. gun section of his battalion before war was declared. He has been out for nearly two years, and has dis- tinguished himself on previous occasions. He Is an apprentice to iron moulding in the employ of Messrs. Jas. Smethurst and Sons, Foundry- street, his father also being an iron moulder, employed by the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. He attended Holy Trinity Day School (of which Miss Davies is the head- mistress) and St. Paul's Church. Formerly he was a familiar figure at the Baths, where he won several prizes for swimming. His sister is engaged at Messrs. Jos. Crosfield and Sons. As we reported last week, Mrs. Collingwood, the widow of Captain Collingwood, has presented Private Taylor and three comrades who afterwards assisted to carry the injured Captain to the dressing station with a silver matchbox each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>D.C.M. FOR A YOUNG WARRINGTONIAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Carried Wounded Officer Under</head>
        <p>Carried Wounded Officer Under</p>
        <p>Fire.</p>
        <p>A GALLANT ACT.</p>
        <p>Private Charles Cranston Taylor, of the South Lancashires, son of Mr. and Mrs. William Taylor, 1, Kendrick-street, Warrington, has been awarded the D.C.M. for a gallant act per- formed August Bth, when he brought in, under fire, from between the lines, Captain Coiling. wood, who had been wounded and who, un- fortunate!., has since died. Private Taylor and his officer brought in the Captain, who was then conveyed to the dressing station by bearers. During this delicate proceeding Private Taylor got "a tiny piece" of shrapnel in his, leg, which, he says, "is nothing to speak about," this being the fourth attack in which he had taken part; and his officer was killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>How The Honour Was Won.</head>
        <p>How The Honour Was Won.</p>
        <p>"We had to take a trench between the lines when the boys went over," he writes to his father. "The Captain got hit straight away. My officer and I carried him to a trench and then I went for a stretcher. When I got it one of the boys and I went back and we took the Captain to the dressing station. I can assure you he was a brave man: he smiled all the way. When we left he said 'God bless you.'"</p>
        <p>D.C.M</p>
