        <p>PRIVATE C. C. TAYLOR,</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>PRIVATE JACK WYTCHERLEY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JACK WYTCHERLEY.</p>
        <p>"My Best Gunner" Receives Military</p>
        <p>Medal.</p>
        <p>A little piece of blue and red ribbon fluttered out of a letter which Mr. and Mrs. B. Wytcher- ley opened on Sunday morning just before leav-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>ELEMENTARY JUSTICE.</head>
        <p>ELEMENTARY JUSTICE.</p>
        <p>We do not think the claim for elementary justice, made at the meeting of the Town Couneil last Tuesday, by CQuzieillor HENRY ROBERTS, will fall altogether on deaf ears. Two years ago an injustice was done him when he was left off the Watch Cortimittee, the Gas Committee, and several other committees, in the work of which he took a givat interest, and placed on a solitary committee that had no attraction for him. He has since been lost to the town for all work except that of attend- ing the Town Council once a month to show those who voted him down how to make speeches. We do not know of the private reasons that induced the majority of the Council to place Councillor ROBERTS on the "retired list," but they could hardly be suffi- ciently strong to warrant the secondary in- justice being done to Whitecross Ward that sent him to represent them on the Coun- cil by a tremendous majority. Practically to disfranchise Whitecross of one-third of its representation by preventing one of its repro- switatives from having direct voice in the affairs of the town is a greater offence than is involved in. the personal part of the treat- ment. The same 'arguments apply to Coun- cillor CRAM, as representative of Fairfield Ward. It may be a reasonable suggestion to i•e-elect the committees to save work and fric- tion at the present time; but a slight excep- tion might be made to allow the unmerited treatment of which both Councillor § ROBERTS and CRAM complain being rectified. The facts elicited by Councillor DALE as to the poor attendances of the members of some com- mittees are further arguments in favour of some slight revision of the lists of the com- mittees.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WOUNDED SOLDIERS AND DRINK.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED SOLDIERS AND DRINK.</p>
        <p>It is to be hoped that the heavy penalty and the castigation the county magistrates administered to two offenders who had pro- cured alcoholic drink for a wounded soldier will have a salutary effect. So serious is the offence of placing drink at the disposal of wounded men that the military authorities have taken powers for dealing with it under the: Defence ,of the Realm Apt, and it is necessary in such an important hospital centre as Warrington that the law should be faith- fully and sternly administered. We are pleased to think that the magistrates in this instance did not hesitate to make it known how reprehensible they think it is either to serve wounded soldiers with drink or to pro- cure it for them., The decision will have a healthy effect; if it does not have such effect the magistrates will not have the doubt they appear to have had in this case, that prison, without the option of a fine, is the proper treatment for people who, are so reckless as to stand in the way, as far as they can, of the recovery of the stricken portion of our man- hood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>Alderman T. Burton after a very long absence, was present. at the monthly meeting of th,e Town Council on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>A few weeks ago I recorded that his Honour Judge Scott-Fox, K.C., had his charming wife and son as his companions in his judicial alcove at the County Court. This bright, golden-haired boy of about seven or eight years sat between his parents with becoming dignity, and never spoke a word for hours, though occasionally he threw wondering glances at his mother.</p>
        <p>I was, therefore, pleasantly surprised to notice at the Liverpool Autumn Art Exhibition this week a full-sized oil painting of the boy. It is, perhaps, one of the most attractive pictures on view.</p>
        <p>Anyone wishing to do a good turn to our wounded " Tommies" staying in the White- cross Hospital may do so by sending packs of playing cards to Mr. Arthur Bottomley (clerk to the Guardians), at Bewsey Chambers. Fifty packs are desired and they need not be new. As long as they are playable and " Tommy " is able to get tricks with them they will be good enough. But, 'of course, soldiers are worthy of our best.</p>
        <p>One of our public officials who seldom klcks amusing audacity was at the seaside the other week-end and met his match.</p>
        <p>Gossiping with one of the lady boarders upon the way children were welcomed by boarders in the old days, he remarked, "They used to give them kisses when they came into the room."</p>
        <p>"Did they kiss you, too?" she asked. "Yes," he replied proudly, longing that history would repeat itself. "Well, you must have altered!". she commented. Our friend only stayed the week-end.</p>
        <p>Those who collect or raise money for war charities of any kind have to be registered or exempted.</p>
        <p>That is as it should be. Mr. W. H. Woodcock has been appointed auditor of all funds raised in the name of the Mayor.</p>
        <p>The fee payable on application for registra- tion is to be remitted. .</p>
        <p>The Paving • Committee have given notice that in future damage done by carters " brake- ing " their carts on the kerb stones in the various streets will be followed by proceedings.</p>
        <p>I have often wondered why the cart owners themselves have not interfered. The damage done to carts must he far more serious than the damage done to the stones.</p>
        <p>Last Sunday Councillor Robert Henshall preached a harvest thanksgiving sermon in the Zion Chapel, Cherry-lane, Lymm. It was, to the day, the thirty-fourth anniversary of the, occasion upon which he preached his first ser- mon—which he delivered in the same place of worship.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Stanley Partridge, the well-known Warrington cricketer, who was wounded in February. has now gone to the O.T.C. quarters at Oxford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>PRIVATE JACK WYTCHERLEY. Military Medallist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>ing their home, 20, Eustace-street, to' visit their son, Private Jack Wytcherley, who is lying wounded in a Stockport hospital. With the drapery was this letter addressed to their soldier soil: "You have been awarded the Military Medal by the Commander of the 3rd Corps. I enclose strip of medal ribbon with congratulations."</p>
        <p>Oil the happy barents went to Stockport and gave the gallant soldier the good news with his parcel of "goodies." The new Military Medallist was wounded several weeks ago in the leg with shrapnel, and is getting on nicely, In recording his injuries at the time we included a letter which spoke highly, of his worth, and has proved a harbinger of glory. Written by his officer it stated: "He is only slightly wounded—in fact, I assure you it is not serious—a piece of shrapnel in the. leg. We all envied him as we pictured him having a long rest from the, horrors of war, and after a little time in hospital seeing old England again. My only regret is that I have lost my best gunner. He was always so reliable and wonder- fully cool and fearless, besides which he was such a general favourite. It is so sad to lose one's best man after being out together so long." Private Wytcherley was one of the first Boy Scouts in Warrington and also a member of the Church Lads' Brigade. He answered his country's call in. August, 1914, and, as the letter from his officer, and the medal from his King; prove, has "delivered the goods." He was edu- cated 4 Heathside School, and' before enlisting worked for the Whitecross Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Boom in Births.</p>
        <p>In the week ended September 30th there were 47 births in Warrington, against 20 ii the cor- responding week of last year.</p>
        <p>Dearth in Deaths.</p>
        <p>There were only eleven deaths against 22 in the corresponding week of last year. Causes of death were :—Heart disease, 2; phthisis, calmer, pneumonia and accident, one each; all other causes, 5.</p>
        <p>Harvest Festival at Stretton. Harvest thanksgiving services will be held at Stretton. Church to-morrow, when the R. Canon Stevenson will be the preacher. Special music will be rendeied by the choir.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr. William Silcock. In celebration of the seventieth anniversary of his birthday, Mr. William Silcock was Pre- sented with an umbrella and fountain pen on Sunday afternoon by members of the Bold-street Wesleyan Sunday School, over whom he has been the superintendent for many years.</p>
        <p>Liberal Women Sewing Class. It has been decided to run another series of ward socials in aid of the sewing class funds. Particulars will be published at a later date. The "Charlie Chaplin" jug, given by Mrs. Richardson, 5, Dial-street, was won by Miss Pennington, Mersey-street. The draw realised otl. 13s. 4d.</p>
        <p>Commercial Evening Classes. At Wood's College, 15, Bridge-street, adults and earnest junior clerical workers can obtain the best up-to-date professional instruction in Shorthand, Typewriting, Boa-keeping, Com- mercial Arithmetic. Time-table of the Even- ing Classes will be forwarded post-free on receipt of post-card.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Clever Man.</head>
        <p>A Clever Man.</p>
        <p>"Dr. Adams was a clever man and discharged his duties ably," said Mr. T. Antwis at Tues- day's meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council, in referring to the loss the district had sustained through the recent death of the Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Autumnal Sermons.</p>
        <p>Autumnal sermons will be preached at the Latchford Baptist Church on Sunday the 15th inst., by the Rev. W. Scragg,_ of Wigan.</p>
        <p>Anthems will be rendered by the choir, who, in the afternoon, will give a cantata, "From sow- ing to reaping."</p>
        <p>Runcorn Union Rates.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Board of Guardians on Tuesday received the Clerk's estimate of expenditure of .218,882 for the ensuing six months, and it was decided to make a call upon the Overseers for that amount. The rate will be Is. sd. in the £, the same as in the corresponding half of last year. The Rural District Council called for £3,891 a reduction of a halfpenny in the ot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Disappearing_ Disease.</head>
        <p>Disappearing_ Disease.</p>
        <p>There are now—only 26- local patients in the Infectious Diseases Hospital, in addition to 18 cases of phthisis in the Sankey Sanatorium. Scarlet fever cases have been reduced to 11; there are seven diphtheria patients and two enteric fever cases. Last week's notifications: Diphtheria, 3; scarlatina, 3; measles, 4; tuber- culosis, 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Attended the Connell.</head>
        <p>Attended the Connell.</p>
        <p>The following were present at the Town Council On Tuesday efening:—The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), the Deputy-Mayor (Dr. Joseph), Aldermen Tinnion, Jas Evans, John Evans, T. Burton and A. Bennett; Coun- cillors R. Henshall, G. Dale, Halliwell, Firth Lindley, E. Cunnington, Skelland, Craik, S. Gough, Dickenson and H. Roberts; with the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) and other officials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Generous Warrington.</head>
        <p>Generous Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Board of Guardians, at their meeting on Tuesday reverted to the dispute between themselves and the Warrington Union respecting the allowance paid to a Warrington woman, who is chargeable to the Runcorn Union. It was reported that the Warrington Guardians were paying the woman Bs. 6d. a week, whereas the amount authorised by the Runcorn Guardians was only ss. There was no discussion.</p>
        <p>Grammar School "Old Boy" Ordained.</p>
        <p>Mr. Victor Charles Roberts, 8.A., Oxford and Cuddesdon, a Warrington Grammar School "Old Boy," and sOn of Major C. J. Roberts, formerly of Manchester-road and now of Bos- combe, Bournemouth, was ordained deacon last yeek by the Bishop of Oxford. He was licensed to St. Edward's School, Oxford, whE-re he has a post and where he will become chaplain at the completion of his year of diaconate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Good Bread at the Cottatie Homes.</head>
        <p>Good Bread at the Cottatie Homes.</p>
        <p>The Padgate Homes and Schools Committee reported to the Board of Guardians on Wed- nesday that, owing to the great pressure of work in the bakery and kitchen, owing to mili- tary necessities, they recommended that the bread for the Cottage Homes be temporarily obtained by outside contract. Mr. Brooks urged the Board to adhere to the present arrange- ment, which was working well and under which the children would get more wholesome bread. It was stated that the bread at present was very good; and the minute was withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Independent Methodist Convention.</head>
        <p>Independent Methodist Convention.</p>
        <p>About 250 visitors were present at a Conven- tion of the Independent Methodist Churches at Grappenhall on , Saturday, the places repre- sented including Nelson, Liverpool, Man- chester, Bolton, Leigh, Stretford, Atherton, Horwich, Wigan, St. Helens. The first session was devoted to women's work, and two very interesting addresses were given by Mrs. Knight, of Urmston, and Miss Clara Green, of Liverpool. Mr. J. W. Berry, of Swinton, gave an address on "Work Among Men," while Councillor Henshall presided over an evening meeting. Wiredrawers' Presentation.</p>
        <p>A concert and social took place at Rylands's Recreation Pavilion last Saturday to make a presentation of an easy chair to Mr. John Wrench, who has been appointed a manager at Messrs. Rylands Bros. The wiredrawers at Church-street very generously subscribed the amount required in recognition of 27 years' service to their Society. The Chairman of the District (Mr. F. 'A. Baker), made the presenta- tion, and was supported by Messrs. W. Bullock, W. Birch, T. Seed and others. The services of Mr. J. Wrench had been of a long and useful character, and he had held various positions with great credit to himself and his Society. The artistes were Messrs. James Wrench (humorist), Bert Hallas (conjurer), C. Furness (Lancashire reciter), with others. Mr. Harry Farrar ably presided at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Be a Light in the World.</head>
        <p>Be a Light in the World.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. James, addressing a well- attended meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. last Sunday, and taking for his text "Let your light so shine, others seeing your good works may glorify God," said that we should all be lights through our works in the world. Deeds, not words, were wanted that would glorify God and not ourselves. With such a goal in view we need not be discouraged if others did not appreciate our work. Mrs. Hazeldine sang "Come unto Me" and "The Beautiful Prayer," which were greatly enjoyed. Mr. Payton pre- sided at the organ. Mr. C. Cotton and Mr. Beswick also took part in the meeting. One new member received a hearty welcome, and Sergeant A. Spencer was also weleomed. The prize books were distributed. Mr. B. Farrar presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Soldiers' Home Benefits from Musical Evening.</head>
        <p>Soldiers' Home Benefits from Musical Evening.</p>
        <p>The funds of the Soldiers' Home, Bold-street, were augmented on Thursday evening by about £7 as a result of a musical evening, organised by .Miss Collins's class at Bold-street Wesleyan Sunday School. A first-class programme was presented to an admiring audience, under the chairmanship of Mr. W. Sileock, the artistes being in fine voice. Songs were given by Mr. Sephton, "The Trumpeter," and "I hear you calling me "; Miss Emily Taylor, "When you come home" and "Down Vauxhall way "; Mr. Poole, "Drake goes West" and "Bedouin love song "; and Mrs. 11. B. Houghton, "In an old- fashioned town" and "The dear home songs ''; recitations by Miss Collin, "The hell gate of Soissons " and "The telegram "; duets by Messrs. 'Sephton and Poole, "The battle eve" and "Watchman, what of the night?" Mrs. Ha,viour and the Misses Bouchier and Parkinson played the violin, 'cello and piano respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Photographic Society.</head>
        <p>Photographic Society.</p>
        <p>The opening meeting of the present session on Tuesday was well attended, about 36 mem- bers and friends hearing with delight another of the lectures by Mr. T. H. Greenall, of Chorley, on "Autumn and Spring in Northern Italy." Entering Italy from the French Riviera, he ignored all the modern tourist towns and took his audience sat once to Alassio, Chiavari, and a score more of villages that can boast as many years as Nice can its dollars. Pisa and Venice were visited, as well as Belluno amongst the Dolomites; and the lecturer showed how wealthy all the old quarters were in photographic subjects. He dealt reverently with the shrines and all the emblems of religion so treasured by our allies; but the heart of the lecturer was evidently all for the peasantry. And really his pictures show that our ideas of Italian beauty, both of boys and girls, is by no means exaggerated. The pictures of Lombardy and Piedmait, illus- trating the wine, silk and fruit harvests, were greatly appreciated and drew much applause all through the evening. Mr. W. Daintith moved, and Mr. Bishop seconded, the vote of thanks, which was most heartily approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Accident to a Footballer.</head>
        <p>Accident to a Footballer.</p>
        <p>During the progress of a football match Victoria Park on Saturday between HowleY and Latchford Recs. Charles W. Price, 48, Cumberland-street, Latchford, one of the full- backs of the last-named team, had his leg broken. Price, who is nineteen years of age, was brought to the Infirmary and is going Oil</p>
        <p>Whist Drive and Dance for B.R.C.S. Mrs. Edmund Osborne is actively engaged in organising a whist drive and dance in aid of the Warrington Branch of the British Red Cross Society. It is to take place in the John Crosfield Memorial Hall, Bewsey-street, on Thursday next, and so that it may be all embracing in its popularity, morning dress, and not evening dress, is to be worn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Music in the Park.</head>
        <p>Music in the Park.</p>
        <p>The Salvation Army Band will play the fol- lowing programme of music in the Park to- morrow : Hymn, "Amazing Love"; selection, "Wondrous Love" (Hawkes); march, "The Conflict" (Coles); selection, "My Guide" (Goldsmith); march, "The Commonwealth" (Slater); selection, "The Rose of Sharon" (Hawkes); "God save the King." Conductor Mr. James Lee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Growth of Richmond's.</head>
        <p>Growth of Richmond's.</p>
        <p>The directors of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., owing to the continued growth of the company's business and the necessary extension of buildings at Grappen- hall, have issued the unallotted shares of the company, viz., 1,179 six per cent. preference shares of .t 5 each and 18,384 ordinary shares of £1 each pro rata, amongst the existing registered holders of ordinary shares.</p>
        <p>• A New Military Hospital. The work of converting the Infirmary of the Runcorn Workhouse at Dutton into a military hospital has been completed', and on TuesdaY afternoon 50 wounded soldiers from Winwick were received as patients. The men arrived in motor cars and were given a hearty wel- come. The newcomers were provided with a splendid tea, consisting of pies, cakes, -jellies, &amp;c., and an informal sing-song was held in the evening.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. H. Burnage Improving. , A letter, was read at Wednesday's meeting .rro` the Warrington Rural Council from Mr. H. Burnage, assistant surveyor, stating that his condition was improving, and that he VC" anxious to resume his duties. Ile thanked the members of the Council for the 'sympathy they had extended to him. A doctor's certificate was enclosed which stated that Mr. Burnage' who had been suffering from fractured legs, was at present unable to follow his occuPa- tion, and would probably be unable to retarn to his duties for at least two mouths.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather said it was :gratifying to rec3ife such a letter from Mr. Burnage. Rev. A. M. Mitchell said the Council might express their satisfaction at his marvellous recovery. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Women's Co-operative Guild. The opening social meeting of the Warring- ton branch of this vigorous Guild was held on Thursday evening, in the Cooperative Hall, and was a great success, a large attend- ance showing hearty appreciation of a varied and most enjoyable programme. The President (Mrs. W. H. Sherburn) extended a heart/ welcome to all. The Hon. Secretary (Mrs. J. H. Jones) reported that the work of the Guild had been somewhat overshadowed owing to the war, and yet last year's memKenship had been almost maintained. Good educational work had been done and a further useful session was anticipated. The Guild Stall at the Crippled Girls' Exhibition realised £43 4s. 6d•</p>
        <p>Songs were contributed by Miss Eva Tanner, Miss Emily Wade, Miss Sherburn, and gr• Kaye; pianoforte solos by Miss Elsie Johnson; Lancashire sketches by Mrs. Nurse; and hunl" orous selections by Mr. H. M. Ashton. Air' Trow was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>The Position of Sunday Schools. Following the lead of the Rev. Percy Ineson, the Sunday School Union, of which Mr. A. Mounfield is president, has decided upon 11 Increase Campaign. At a meeting of repreeen- tatives of the Sunday Schools, the Rev. Pere)" meson urged that an effort at increase be made, saying that if they could know the difference between the day school and Sunda' school attendances the figures would probablY startle them. It was decided that the Sunday schools Of the town should be approached during tile next week or two, and the Sunday School Union will meet again for a study of methods and will offer recommendations to the schools. The help of ministers and teachers will tot: sought, and it is hoped that all the schools 01 the town will join in the effort. The object; aimed at will be an immediate increase 01 twenty-five per cent in the number of scholars and greater efficiency in teaching. A Teachers' Training Class has been organised to secure the last-named object.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's Schools-2.200 Wanted. St. Paul's Parish Magazine states that the managers of the schools have come to the scse. clusign that unless' £3OO is raised it will be wise to hand them over to the local Educatil Authority. The Vicar, who is a manager ail trustee of the buildings, will never consent this course of action. He has no quarrel wit_,l the Education Authority as such but he ot: never be Vicar of St. Paul's without tn' privilege of being able to go into the school 9 when he likes, and being able to open school with prayers, or taking a class VI Scripture. So then to save the situation tv.The money has got to be raised. The Rev. ,1; Downham, who is in charge of the PariL during the Vicar's absence on chaplain's 4itYo in the Army, is making an appeal to save t'n schools. He wants at once, he says, ten ni4e„, who will Make themselves guarantors for XL" so that the necessary work, which is iroPe% tive, can he put in hand. Already within hours seven friends have made themselves 1;e- -sponsible for £lO each, and in addition has received subscriptions amounting to .08*</p>
        <p>• 2.5 Fine for Lighting Infringement' Councillor Henry Roberts, chairman of the magisterial Bench sitting on Tuesday in tile Borough Police Court, remarked, when vaipOS' ing rather stiff fines on several persons Olt' failing to reduce lights in their houses, tha two of the cases were very serious. The follow' ing fines were inflicted :—John G. Walkeirl. Arpley-street, 405.; Norman Marsden, TheN lane Tannery, 405.; James Cliffe, 178, 3lanches- ter-road, 205.; and James Flanagan, 11, 13e11- nett-street, 10.2. Fourteen more offenders against the Lightil Order were fined as severely as at Tuesdel Court on Thursday, one—William Millingtol; c/o Toft, baker, Bridge-street—being fined &lt;e_ Detective Anderson and Special Constablne Westwell stating that on receiving informato that Zeppelins were not very many miles avitalr they went to Toft's bakehouse just af ase 1 a.m. and instructed Mr. Millington, who in charge, to screen his lights so that could not be seen. Forty minutes later tn" little bright effect from the windows was different, although he said he had</p>
        <p>-- t Sae° pU</p>
        <p>over them.</p>
        <p>• i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>DIED FOR THEIR eOUNTRY.</head>
        <p>DIED FOR THEIR eOUNTRY.</p>
        <p>Additional Local Casualties in the Various War Areas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>KILLED.</head>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>SERCIT.-NIAJOR SWEENEY.</p>
        <p>Popular Gymnast And Boxer Killed In Action.</p>
        <p>Tidings of the death of Sergeant-Major Fred Sweeney, who has been killed in action, will be received with great sorrow and regret by a very large circle of friends and admirers in War- lngton. He received wounds in the head, left Wrist and left thigh. Sergeant-Major Sweeney Was gymnastic instructor at the Orford Barracks with the South Lancashire Regi- ment, and was extremely popular in the town. He was an all-round athlete and a splendid gymnast and boxer. He took a great interest In boxing competitions at the Barracks and in the town, and on one occasion at least won a competition on the Football Ground. He was a born soldier, smart in appearance, direct in Speech, and cheerful under all circumstances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>PRIVATE E. STOKES.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE E. STOKES.</p>
        <p>Comrades Lose A Brave And</p>
        <p>Fearless Soldier.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Stokes, 36, Warburton-street have been officially notified of the death in action of their son, Private Edward Stokes, South Lancashire Regiment. The following letter was written by a comrade, Private Austin Dagnall, who said :—" I am very sorry to in- form you that Edward was killed yesterday (September 15th). I know it will come as a great shock to you, just the same as it came to us. I can assure you that your son suffered no pain, as he was killed instantly by a piece of shell. All his comrades are very sorry to lose such a brave and fearless soldier and send their deepest sympathy. . . . May God help</p>
        <p>You to bear this terrible strain." Private Stokes, -who was an old Territorial,</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN BITRRELL. Killed.</p>
        <p>and we all deeply. mourn his loss lloping you will be able to bear your loss as bravely as he met, his death."</p>
        <p>Private Burrell was 21 years of age. At the time he enlisted last January he was employed</p>
        <p>as foreman in the bottling department of Bolton's Brewery, Mersey-street.</p>
        <p>... . ~ • .::•-i,•:•.*:::‘.4:-.01.).1:i! '•*',"''''.,;•, -:::'::..":;;::.-.•;:::::T•i0.5.:;:,:':. ,„.,„:„vtai.kigtg:..,%;.,::::•.'..'.:.'iti,: ...,,...,-14Ktigi,10.4.moittRz::":141.k.:,':...•:.j,:::Z•i:::,'g0F: ..I.11:::k%11..tv. ~:.:,ii:e....,:.5t.,•: 0:1,!•silk," •.,•*,..,_..,.f. N5.....)...5..!.,:::.::,:.1,.tr:....!...:..:::,.:•:::.,.:,..,,•:,q1..::.:%:.......7.,..,•,..;:,:.:.•:...z,.:....,...., .:,•:.'v; .•':.:*::%iklN.r,z,..-.........„:.... . . ; .::::•::::::.:'..A:ii.:'A..":',...: :r..,4011.N.4 .t. . ,:&lt;:••••::!::::::*:::.:::...,,....,:.1,::;:..:',,::::. `'.%:.•:•:::-.:.„ ~,,,..,:;:•ii,ii'.?:,..:„). tli4iv4kAt',•:,:tki: ..i,,,, ''......-.e.:''',:'.';:ki?....4 4W;P:..q...,:i..i.i..!..::',..,.;!. . , 4,Si- - :.,..ii:.:*0".....::•.;':-,', 14‘k.11.:::::-'":i;:i.i.•:.::.:.,.i .'' ke,••' • • ""' ~,,,„..,.. „..:„.:..., ~.„. •,. •••••........,..., .....................s ~.„.„..„.„...:„..:..........,.,,..„.:. .„,„......,.. ...:„.:...„.:::.,...,..„,:..:,::.,:.:.,,,„,.:„,:„, a,:..1f.,...,,,,,:,:::,„„,,,„:,:::,„..„K.,:.:::..... ••••,,,*••:.4 ' ..` k,4•,..\414-....: • ' !.,..Qtiiii...:::fi....ii:ik..gfi::::.•:::::.:.••;.:::.::.::- ...• ~. . ~ • .. -• ...:1... t:;,..,,..i:.4...:.5t, .-i:...::::.:.:-i•:.:...:::::ii:i:',.:.-....%.?::,;"•;.;:iikui.... .. .. - ... „....... ' :.•,.... .::.:.iicti.41...,.,,:i::::.,.!...i.ti;i:,:5,;•,,,,,, c:.4... . , .., ......v.. ~ , ~.', •., ~:., s •iz:i.V `, -.... ,z . N.' . , „ .&gt;:.• , * . ~ . „„ • . . . • , . . 9 ..4, . ...., ..ft ... 4 ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>SERGEANT J. MURRAY.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT J. MURRAY.</p>
        <p>Saw Service In The Boer War Arid Egypt.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Burgess, 64, Academy-street, has been officially informed that her brother, Sergeant Joseph Murray, of the South Lancs. Regiment, Was killed in action on July 13th. Sergeant Murray, who was forty-seven years Of age, was a son of Mr. Francis Murray, who as agent at the Liberal Club for fifteen years. ne served for twelve years in the Army, and 4,llring this period he was with the South Lancs. .4tegiment in Egypt, and with the Royal War- wicks during the Boer War. He rejoined the South Lancs. Regiment at the outbreak of the present war, and was invalided home in Decem- ber, 1914, with frost-bite. He rejoined his regi- toent in March, 1915, and was eventually pro- footed Sergeant for good work in the field. He was formerly employed for about eight years at the Castle Rubber Co.'s works, and has two brothers serving in the Array.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Pi!Militir</head>
        <p>Pi!Militir</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. THOMASON.</p>
        <p>Wounded Twice ; Gallipoli And</p>
        <p>Somme Hero Now Killed.</p>
        <p>Private John. Thomason, South Lancashire Regiment has been killed in action. His wife, Mrs. Thomason, of Derby-street, has received many sympathetic letters, amongst them being the following from a comrade:- - "You will have great consolation in knowing that 'Jack' died instantly, therefore suffering 'no pain; and also by knowing that he died bravely fighting for his King and country. All the lads of his company send their heart- felt sympathy to you in your sad loss. Private Thomason enlisted on August 29th, 1914, and was sent on active service in June of last year. He was wounded in the head in Gallipoli, returning to the Front in March last. Again he was slightly wounded on July 22nd, but rejoined his regiment after a shop time in hospital. He met his fate whilst handing bombs to bombers during an advance. An "old boy" of Hamilton-street School, Private Thomason was formerly employed at the Warririgtol.i Corporation Gas Works. He</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>Ltd. He had been at the Front twelve months. As a by he attended St. James' School. His brother George, of the Rifle Brigade, has been wounded twice, and another brother, Ike, is in training with the R.A.M.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>PRIVATE W. COYNE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE W. COYNE.</p>
        <p>Reported Killed ; But Comrade Raises Hopes.</p>
        <p>Officially reported missing a little over twelve months ago, Private William Coyne, South Lancashire Regiment, son of Mr. and Mrs. John Coyne 159, Longford-street, is now reported by the War Offite to have been killed. A comrade has written as follows sto Mrs. Coyne :</p>
        <p>"There is still hope for as no one has yet given you definite news. I still hope and trust that he is a prisoner-of-war. . . . As I have said many times before, he was more than a brother to me." Private Coyne was 23 years of age, and, .as a youth, attended the St. Ann's School, worship- ping 'at the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church. He was employed, previous to joining H.M. Forces at the commencement of hostilities; at - the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., and was well liked and respected by all who came in contact with him. He took particular in- terest in all branches of sport,' especially foot- ball, having played for several local clubs. Another son, of the same rcgiment, was killed in June last year; while a third one joined the colours, but was recalled to work on munitions. A fourth son, Mr. J. Coyne, at present employed in the Office at Walker's Brewery, is to be called to the dolours this month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PRIVATE J. JONES.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. JONES.</p>
        <p>Killed, Missing And Wounded : Fate Of Three Brothers.</p>
        <p>In our last issue we reported that Private Joseph Jones, of the K. 0.5.8., son of the late Mr: James Jones and Mrs. Hesketh, 26, Powys. street, was officially reported as missing after an engagement on September 3rd. Official news has now been received that he met his death on that date. Private Jones, who was tiventy-seven years of age, enlisted about twelve months ago, and had been at the Front ten months. He was educated at Holy Trinity School, and was formerly employed by Messrs. John Melbourne, Ltd. His brother, Rifleman H. Jones, of the King's Royal Rifles, has been missing since July 23rd, and another, Walter, of the K. 0.5.8., has been invalided home from i France, and is now n Edinburgh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <p>was 35 years of age and leaves a widow and one child to mourn his loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>PRIVATE E. LIVESEY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE E. LIVESEY.</p>
        <p>"A Fellow Well Liked By All,"</p>
        <p>Writes Comrade.</p>
        <p>The two following letters conveyed to Mr. and Mrs. Livesey, 27, Farrell-street, the news of the death in action of their son, Private Edward Livesey, South Lancashire Regiment. The first letter, from Private C. Price, ran: 'Just a few lines to let you know that Ted was killed in action. . . . We were great pals, but when we came out here we were parted.. . .</p>
        <p>has gone to a• better home above. . . ." 'Private A. Wyrill *rote: " It is much to ray regret that I have to write these few lines to you in the place of your son, who was killed in action on August 28th. We made a charge on that date and your son was one of the unfor- tunate men who were killed. He did not suffer long in pain, a sniper hit him in the head with a bullet and he died almost immediately. . . . He was a fellow who was well liked by all his comrades,. although he had not joined the battalion many weeks. All the platoon send their sympathy." Private Livesey enlisted in January of this year and went to the Front in July. He formerly worked at the Bewsey Wire Ropery, attended Ellesmere-street Sunday School, was educated at the National Schools, and was 23 years old. His favourite sport was angling, and his two brothers, Corporal James -Livesey, A.S.C., and Able Seaman Frank Livesey, H.M.S. Warspite, are also serving.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SERGEANT S. WHITFIELD.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT S. WHITFIELD.</p>
        <p>Wounded On The Great Day— July Ist—And Now Killed.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Samuel Whitfield, of the Manches- ter Regiment, whose home address was 3, Mer- sey Mount, off Mersey-street, was killed on the 28th September while out with his officer on "important work." It will be remembered by his many friends that he was wounded on the Ist of July, and a few weeks later spent ten days at home. Sergeant Whitfield was 31 years of age, an "old boy" of the National School, and a for- mer employe of the Warrington Tanning Co. He had been in the Army two years and at the Front 22 months. His brother John is at the Front with the R.F.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>PRIVATE W. RYAN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE W. RYAN.</p>
        <p>Missing Since March ; Now Reported Killed.</p>
        <p>Reported missing since March 4th, Private William Ryan, of the Border Regiment, son of Mr. and Mrs. Peter Ryan, 29,•Ripley-street, is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>now officially reported to have met his death on that date.</p>
        <p>Private Ryan, who wis 22 years of age, had been in the Army since twelve months last November, and at the Front six months. He was formerly employed as a fireman on the L. and N.-W. Railway, and worshipped at St. Barnabas' Church and Sunday School.</p>
        <p>"Our Day "—Cafe Chantant.</p>
        <p>In connection with "Our Day," on behalf of the B.R.C.S. and Order of St. John of Jerusalem, a cafe chantant will be opened in the Parr Hall on Thursday afternoon, 19th inst., by the Countess of Stamford. The band of the 2nd Battalion South Lancs. will attend and a fine array of vocalistic talent has been secured for the occasion. There will be several stalls, a number of competitions, and other attractions. Helpers' meeting on Monday afternoon in the Parr Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>DIED OF WOUNDS.</head>
        <p>DIED OF WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE E. BRACEGIRDLE</p>
        <p>" Best Stretcher Bearer We Have Ever Had." .</p>
        <p>Private Ernest Bracegirdle, South Lancashire Regiment, son of Mr. and Mrs. W. Bracegirdle, 203, Thelwall-lane, has died of wounds in a French Hospital, received while acting as a stretcher-bearer.</p>
        <p>His parents first received a field card from their son, informing them .that he was wounded, which news was subsequently con- firmed by a comrade. Later, however, to their sorrow, Mr. and Mrs. Bracegirdle received the following letter, dated September 19th, from a Sister at a casualty clearing station "I am sorry to have to tell you very sad news concerning your son, Private Bracegirdle, who died 'here to-day. He was admitted on the 17th, sufferinc, from very serious wounds in his stomach. There was very little hope for him from the first, but, of course, we always try and hope for the best. He died very quietly this afternoon at two o'clock. I asked hint if there was anything he wanted me to write to you about. He just said 'Give my mother my love, and tell her I hope to see her again.' I could not possibly tell him, poor lad, he was going to die, so I am afraid he gave no othcir message. Please 'accept my deepest sympathy for you and yours in this sad time."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Officer's Glowing Tribute.</head>
        <p>Officer's Glowing Tribute.</p>
        <p>Captain Brian T. Dickson, writing to Mrs. Bracegirdle, says : "To-day we have received the very sad news</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>% .••••=•••••mmilasmmill.mliimEri</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <p>that your son has died of wounds received on September 15th, I am writing on behalf of the officers, N.C.O.'s and men of A Company to send you and all his friends our most sincere sympathy. I have no hesitation in paying that poor Bracegirdle was the hest stretcher-bearer we ever had, and I have often heard Dr. Law speak very highly of his work. Always cheer- ful and willing to do his duty under all cir- cumstances, he won the respect and esteem of everybody, and in losing him we have lost a true friend and a very excellent soldier. lie was hit by a shell on the day of the big push, and although he was severely wounded we had all hoped that he would pull through. He has, like many other good fellows, given his life for his country, and I trust that the knowledge of this may be some comfort to you and all his family."</p>
        <p>Private Bracegirclle, who was 25 years of age, was a former , scholar. of the Wycliffe Day School and St. Ann's Sunday School. He en- listed on the very day war was declared and had been on active service since February, 1915. He was formerly employed as a brick- setter at Earlestown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>PRIVATE W. E. CHETTER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE W. E. CHETTER.</p>
        <p>Dies From Wounde In The. Leg.</p>
        <p>A fine youth, standing over six feet in height,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>REPORTED KILLED.</head>
        <p>REPORTED KILLED.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE P. CORNELIA.</p>
        <p>Reported To Have Been Killed By A Shell.</p>
        <p>• No news is to hand concerning Private Patrick Cornelia, of the South Lancs. Regi- ment, who, along with Corporal Monition (whose wounding in action we report in another column) , was, according to a letter from a comrade at the Front, killed with his friend .by a shell. Mrs. Cornelia resides at 16, Old-road, and is naturally greatly concerned as to the fate of her son. - -</p>
        <p>Private Cornelia was a good football player, and, like his friend, had played in the Works' Competitions. He is 21 years of age and was formerly employed by Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd. His father is serving with the South Lancashire Regiment in the Isle of Man, and a brother-in-law is at the Front. Private Cornelia was educated at Our Lady's School, Latchford. Both he and Cor- poral Mannion served their time in the South Lancashire Regiment before the war and were balled up together.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>CORPORAL R. BARBER Wouuded.</p>
        <p>that he was -wounded by bullets in both hands in the Battle of the Somme on September 25th and is now in a Suffolk hospital. Corporal Barber, who was formerly a mem- ber of the Manchester City Police Force, en- listed in February of last year and had not been at the Front very long, when he had the misfortune to be wounded. He is in his 24th year, and was educated at Christ Church School, Wash-lane, Latchfcrd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>SERGEANT W. FALLOWS.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT W. FALLOWS.</p>
        <p>Knew Nothing Until He Found Himself In Hospital.</p>
        <p>Sergeant William Fellows, South Lancs. Regiment, and husband of Mrs. Fellows, 12, Quay Fold, has been wounded, hut is now on leave at his home. The following are the Ser- geant's experiences in his own words: "On the 3rd September, about 7.45 a.m., I was sitting in my dug-out waiting for the platoon to go over the top, to charge the German lines. The enemy artillery was shelling our lines, and one shell burst in my dug-out, and I was blown into the air. I knew nothing more until I found myself in hospital. We shall win the war," continued the Sergeant, "but at the Front you do not walk on the ground, but on dead bodies." The gallant Sergeant lost his speech for two days.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Fallows, who has eleven years' ser- vice to his credit, enlisted on the 19th August, 1914, but had only been in France seven weeks. He is 25 years of age, was formerly employed at Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., and is a 'native of Liverpool. He attended St. Peter's Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>RIFLEMAN A.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN A.</p>
        <p>WEIR.</p>
        <p>Satirical Over Boche Who Bombed Him.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Arthur Weir, of the Civil Service Rifles, son of Mr. and Mrs. J. B. Weir, 14, Rolleston-street, has had a remarkable escape from death at the Front, and has written some lively letters descriPtive of his experiences, uhd expressing his sentiments. He has been wounded ivith a bomb, and writing to his parents, says :—"I am at present in a rest camp for a few days as the result of stopping a few pieces of bomb with my left thumb and chin, but I am hoping to join my battalion shortly. In this last ' scrap ' my mirror was twisted all shapes, and I lost my cigarette ease, photo- graphs, &amp;c., as the result of the explosion of a. bomb blowing my pocket out." In a later letter Rifleman Weir said :—" I can thank my lucky stars I am here myself. have the consolation of knowing that the Boche who set the bomb off got a far worse 'packet'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>than I did. I doubt whether he will bomb trenches again for a long t:)..ac, If ever, unless he has a try at bombing Old Nick' out of the hot shop." Rifleman Weir, who is twenty years of age, joined the Army in January of this year, and has been in France about four months. Ile is an "old boy" of the secondary School, and was formerly employed at the Liverpool Customs House. His brother John enlisted in the early days of the war, but in eight weeks was discharged as medically unfit. He has since been called up, however, and is in train- ing with the R.G.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>COIIPORAL J. EATON, D.C.M.</head>
        <p>COIIPORAL J. EATON, D.C.M.</p>
        <p>In A Base Hospital Wounded In Tiw</p>
        <p>Foot.</p>
        <p>A local D.C.M. has been wounded in the per- son of Corporal Joseph Eaton, of the South Lancs. Regiment, who has written to his brother-in-law and sister, Mr. and Mrs. Hughes, 23, _Dickenson-street, to the effect that he has received a gun-shot wound in the foot and is in a base hospital. He adds that he wants to get back to his battalion as soon as possible. Corporal Eaton, who is twenty-eight years of age, was formerly employed by the Firth Co., Howley, and for several years prior to the out- break of -war belonged to the local battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment. , It was at Hooge on June 16th last year that he won his D.C.M., and was promoted to the rank of Lance. Corporal. He remained in a support trench for an hour under heavy fire, digging out a cora- rade who had been buried. Since then he has gained another stripe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Raddon Court Pound Day.</head>
        <p>Raddon Court Pound Day.</p>
        <p>Pound Day will be held at Raddon Court on Thursday, when any gifts, especially of groceries, eggs, fruits and stationery will be gratefully received. A concert and an exhibi- tion of fancy work by the wounded soldiers will be given in the afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>CORPORAL J. MANNION. Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <p>R.C. School. He was 20 years of age, and took an interest in boxing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <p>PRISONER OF WAR.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL P. J. DURHAM.</p>
        <p>Brother Of' Local Boxer A Prisoner-of-War.</p>
        <p>Corporal Patrick James Durham. of the Australian Contingent, brother to "Joe" Dur- ham, the well-known local boxer, is a prisoner of War in Germany. About six weeks ago his parents, Mr. and Mrs. M. Durham, 11, West-street, received official news that he was missing, but this week they were happily surprised when they received a card from their son stating "I am wounded</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>CORPORAL P. J. DURIIA3I</p>
        <p>Prisoner</p>
        <p>and a prisoner. lam in Barracks Hospital, 42a, Grafenwohr, Bavaria, and am doing as well as can be expected." Corporal Durhain, who is 26 years of age and married, emigrated to Australia eight years ago. He enlisted in the Australian Forces im- mediately the war broke out, fought in the Dardanelles, and was sent to France about four months ago. An old boy of St. Benedict's R.C. School, Corporal Durham, when in Warring- ton, was employed in the office at Walker's Brewery. He came home on a five days' leave before being drafted to France. His brother Joe has been in France with the Rifle Brigade for eighteen months, and his brother Michael, of the R.F.A., is in Mesopotamia after serving in France.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>I 1 , k , . , i , , : 4 , , A</p>
      </div>
