      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Ilh</head>
        <p>Ilh</p>
        <p>-5 -- 0_64 .</p>
        <p>4:ISTRICT iIEW3</p>
        <p>LiYMM</p>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC.</p>
        <p>Good old summer time! Gone, alas ! for a few months, at any rate.</p>
        <p>A local woman worker turned up for duty last Sunday morning an hour before the pre- scribed time, only to find the office locked up and the caretaker peacefully sleeping.</p>
        <p>We soon dropped into the change, but "little Mary" was not so fast. I venture to say that more breakfasts were eaten in bed last Sunday morning than any Sunday in the year. Of course, hubby had to suffer, he had to get up and. put the kettle on.</p>
        <p>Then there was the sudden darkening of the evening; in fact, it went dark an hour earlier than usual.</p>
        <p>People naturally thought it was going to rain, so turned out fully equipped with an umbrella.</p>
        <p>Mistaken identity was quite a colmon occur- rence.</p>
        <p>Two young ladies walking 1p ,Higher Lane noticed an elderly gentleman coming in the opposite direction..</p>
        <p>Mistaking him for their father, one of them shouted out "Good night, pa! Are you only just going home?" Whereas the gentleman gravely informed them that they were mistaken.</p>
        <p>I notice with regret that some of our public footpaths are getting in a very bad condition.</p>
        <p>One in particular, the hedge alongside covers three-parts of the allotted space for walking.</p>
        <p>Very inconvenient for those who are apt to</p>
        <p>walk two abreast.</p>
        <p>I am sure that something could be done to remedy this slight inconvenience.</p>
        <p>Whatever was the "Free Church Council" thinking about to let the harvest festival ser- vices clash as they did last Sunday?</p>
        <p>No fewer than four Nonconformist Churches held their festival services last Sunday:</p>
        <p>Then there were the Lymm and Oughtrington Parish Churches; in fact, it was a thorough harvest Sunday.</p>
        <p>The local 'platoon of Volunteers held a Zepp drill last Thursday evening.</p>
        <p>I suppose they are preparing themselves for future emergencies.</p>
        <p>A Buffalo funeral took place at the Parish Church last Thursday afternoon.</p>
        <p>' Something unusual, it naturally created con- siderable interest throughout the village.</p>
        <p>The departed brother they were laying to rest was the 'late Mr. Ernest Creasey,- late licensee of the Millstone Hotel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>ALL THE NEW 'COATS</head>
        <p>ALL THE NEW 'COATS</p>
        <p>lor WINTER now on show at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>"SUITS Ltd."</head>
        <p>"SUITS Ltd."</p>
        <p>The "Black Rubber" RAIN- COATS as worn by the Navy. The New WATERPROOF Raincoats—Ladies', Gent's. and Juveniles'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>p*-OVERCOATS</head>
        <p>p*-OVERCOATS</p>
        <p>Blanket Lined in the latest Military Styles.</p>
        <p>The Pouring Rain</p>
        <p>DEMANDS that EVERYBODY should at once protect themselves by WATERPROOF Attire. There are now so many stylish 'Coats that the difficulty is which to select. The new Black Rubber Coats are ELEGANT. "SUITS LTD.," of Horsemarket Street, 'Warrington, have these in Men's, Ladies' and Children's, at the lowest prices. Children's range only from 15/6, and Ladies' and Gent's from 25/-. Every Coat is Guaranteed. They are made from the finest Rubber, and in the newest styles are elegant.</p>
        <p>IT CAN RAIN AS HARD AS IT LIKES AND NO WATER CAN GET UNDER THIS GARMENT. Besides being weatherproof, it is also elegant in appearance.</p>
        <p>A LARGE STOCK of the very latest styles and materials in R A INCOA TS—LADIES' GENT'S and CHILDREN'S—at the lowest prices MUCH LOWER THAN ANY OTHER HOUSE can sell at—are at the disposal and inspec- tion of everybody who will pay SUITS LTD. a visit. Children's range from 10/6, and Ladies' and Gent's from 20/-.</p>
        <p>WOOLLEN OVERCOATS. Lined with the new Blanket Cloths, which are very warm, can he tried on in the biggest variety of materials and range from 20/-.</p>
        <p>A SPECIAL LINE of CHILDREV- OVERCOATS MADE FROM REM- NANT PIECES OF GENUINE ARMY CLOTHS from 12/6 should be seen at once. These are very warm and impossible to wear out. The number of these is limited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>114*C</head>
        <p>114*C</p>
        <p>17, HOIRSEMARKET ST.,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>SUITS Ld.</head>
        <p>SUITS Ld.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF THE LATE 1111. HERBERT RHAM</p>
        <p>Impressive scenes were witnessed at the Parish Church last Saturday afternoon, when the funeral of the late ex-Private Herbert Rigby, of the local Territorials, whose death we, announced last week, took place in the presence of a large crowd of local inhabitants. The cortege. proceeded from his.. parents' resi- dence at Rush Green, the hearse being followed by two mourning coaches containing his im- mediate relatives. The party were met at the church gates by the Rev. Tudor-Evans, rector of Oughtrington, who read the funeral service, and a large number of his colleagues from the Partington Steel and Iron Company's Works. The principal mourners were: Father, Mother, Mr. and Mrs. Shaw (sister and brother- in-law), Miss A. Rigby (sister), Mr. Chas. Rigby (brother), Mr.. and Mrs. Bayley (uncle and aunt), Miss A. Bayley (cousin), Mr. and Mrs. T. Dandy (uncle and aunt), and Mr. and Mrs. Minney (uncle and aunt). Other mourners present were Mr., Mrs. and Miss Leigh, Mrs. Cross, Mrs. Ratcliffe, Mrs. Hirst, Miss E. Leather, Miss A. Leigh, Mr. H. Leather, Mr. Frank T.Tdale, Lance-Corporal Fred "'dale, and Private Walter Hadden, one of his former com- rades who served with him i France. The bearers were Messrs. J. Hirst, H. Rat- cliffe, P. Leather, It. Millington, A. Hall and T. Pendygrass. The Partington Steel and Iron Company were represented by Messrs. T. Mitchall, T. Hamer, H. Bates, J. Bates, W. Hammond, S. Latham, W. Walsh, W. Mitchall, F. Shannon, A. Wally, F. Carr, W. Davies, E. Davies and G. Lewis. Wreaths and floral tributes were sent</p>
        <p>Mother, Father and family, "Ada," Mrs. Hadden and "His comrades out there," "Fellow workmates at the Partington Steel and Iron Co. " " -Uncle Tom and Alto Agnes," Messrs. Barker Morgan and Wass, jun. (Steel Department), Mr. and Mrs. H. Holt, "Friends and neighbours," "All cousins," Mr. and Mrs. Hayes, Mr. and Mrs. Leigh and Connie, Mr. and Mrs. C. Holt, "Aunt Annie and Uncle George," Mr. and Mrs. S. H. Davies and family, and Mrs. Stewart Smith. The funeral arrange- ments were conducted by Mr. Hugh Holt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>FORMER LYMM LICENSEE'S SUDDEN DEATH.</head>
        <p>FORMER LYMM LICENSEE'S SUDDEN DEATH.</p>
        <p>Buffalo Funeral At The Parish Church We regret to announce the sudden death of Mr. rnest Creasey, which took place at his residence, the Medway Hotel, Stockport-road, Manchester, early last Sunday morning. De- ceased was apparently in good health until the Wednesday night previous, when he suddenly collapsed and never again was fully conscious. Deceased was in his 42nd year. He was very widely known and respected in the district, and for some considerable time was licensee of the Millstone Hotel. Previous to that he was the proprietor of the butcher's shop in The Grove. The funeral took place at the Lymm Parish Church on Thursday afternoon, and was con- ducted by the Rev. G. B. Thurston, the rector. There was a large attendance of relatives and friends'. Being a member of the R.A.0.8., he was given a full Buffalo funeral, and. this in- teresting service was conducted by Bro. Sam Gray, R.0.H., chaplain to the Manchester Providence. During the service in church Bro. R. Hopkinson, the Buffalo minstrel, played on the church organ, "0 rest in the Lord," " Chopin's Funeral March," and the Dead March in "Saul."</p>
        <p>Besides the principal mourners, which in- cluded the late gentleman's wife and family, the following Buffalo Lodges were represented: Lymm Lodge, R. Parker, K.0.M.; J. Thofilley, C.P.; J. Burns C.P.; W. Lawrenson, S. D. Moston, J. Leigh, T. P. Curse, H. Holt, B. Chetham, T. Hatton, 11. Moss, and William Daniels. "Cosmopolitan" Lodge (Heaton Chapel), J. Butler, C.P. "Belle Vile" Lodge (Manchester), J. Hallworth, K.0.M.; and •J. Ward, C.P. "Brooklands" (Sale) and "Pride of Stretford" (Stretford), T. '.Temie- son, K.O.M. "Duke of Lancaster" and</p>
        <p>"Duke of Connaught" (Manchester), A. Straf- ford, C.P. " Longsdale " Lodge (Manchester), Bro. Robinson, C.P.; G. Conway C.P.; and J. Butler, C.P. Wreaths and ,floral tributes were sent by : Th-rOp lfwpd him dearly," Mr. and Mrs. Hughes and daughter, "Sam, Jim and Jack" (waiters), "Boys in Billiard-room" (Medway), "Emma, Arthur and Gwen," "Albert, Alpin and a friend," "Jack, George and Ike" (the</p>
        <p>Samuel OWen, Brookfield-terrace, received a letter from him saying: "You will be thinking that I am missing. I know you will have been very worried, but don't get downhearted, for I shall soon be all right again. At present I am in hospital at the base, and have_ been for the last fortnight. Luckily, I did not get what you would term a bad dose, so I will soon be getting over it and at it once again. I do not expect I shall see the Lymm lads again for some considerable time, but when I left them they were all doing as well as could be expected. Keep on smiling and look on the bright side, and remember that although this horrible war may last for years, it cannot last for ever. I can assure you that the Kaiser isn't half get- ting a blow down on the Somme, although the weather just lately has been a drawback to our lads."</p>
        <p>Private Harry Cadman is a well-known local man, and was mobilised with the Cheshire Regi- ment on the outbreak of war, and went out to France in February, 1915. He is 22 years old, and was educated at Lymm School, and wor- shipped at the Parish Church. He took an active interest in sport, and was a playing member of the Iymm A.F.C. Prior to the -war he resided at Booth's Hill, and was employed as a bricksetter by Mr. J. H. Holt, Barrs Bank.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>...7. !"^:i 5... 1 .....1_,...",- '</p>
        <p>- -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>Parish Church.</p>
        <p>Harvest festival services were held at the Parish Church last Sunday, and were conducted morning and evening by the Rev. Leonard Southern, of Snowdon Ranger Priory. Both ser- vices were well attended, and the church was tastefully decorated. In the afternoon a chil- dren's flower service was held, at which the children gave gifts of fruit and flowers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Oughtrington Parish Church.</head>
        <p>Oughtrington Parish Church.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services for the Ought- rington Parish Church were held last Sunday, and were conducted by the Rector, the Rev. Tudor Evans. 'All services were well attended, and 'the church was tastefully decorated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Congregational Church.</head>
        <p>Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>At the Congregational Church last Sunday the services were conducted by the church minister, the Rev. Shields Gill. All services were well attended, and in the afternoon the children held a flower service, at which they made gifts of fruit and flowers. The church was neatly decorated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>Here the services last Sunday were conducted in th,e morning by Mr. A. Appleton, and in the evening by Mr. John Upson. Both services were well attended. The church was prettily decorated. The collections were in aid of the church funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Zion Chapel, Cherry-lane.</head>
        <p>Zion Chapel, Cherry-lane.</p>
        <p>Harvest festival services were held at the Zion Chapel, Cherry-lane, last Sunday, and were conducted morning and evening by Coun- cillor Robert Henshall, of Latchford. Both services were well attended, and the chapel was tastefully decorated. • A coincidence well worth recording was the fact that it was exactly 34 years to the day since Councillor Henshall preached his first sermon in the very same church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Broomhedge Primitive Methodist.</head>
        <p>Broomhedge Primitive Methodist.</p>
        <p>At the Broomhedge Primitive Methodist Church last Sunday, the Rev. R. W. Ferguson, the church minister, officiated. All 'services were well attended, and the chapel was taste- fully decorated. On the Monday evening the Lymm Primitive Methodist Choir gave a service of sang entitled, "The Reaper's Hymn."</p>
        <p>Captain Ridgway.—Captain Thomas Ridgway has returned to the Front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <p>B XRGEAIAN'S TRAGIC END.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Found Dead In The Cabin 01 His Boat..</head>
        <p>Found Dead In The Cabin 01 His Boat..</p>
        <p>While his boat was passing through Lymm at 12.30 p.m. on Wednesday, Charles Scragg, native of Leigh, was found dead in the cabin. He was employed as a bargeman on the barge George, and being subject to fits it is supposed that he suddenly had an attack and expired under its effect, for when his 'workmate left him a short time before he was apparently all right, and when he returned to the cabin he found him lying dead on the floor. The boat was stopped, and the police, who vere im- mediately informed, had the body conveyed to the mortuary. At the inquest yesterday the verdict was: "Death from natural causes."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>BEECIIWOOD GARDEN FETE PROCEEDS.</head>
        <p>BEECIIWOOD GARDEN FETE PROCEEDS.</p>
        <p>The detailed balance sheet of the Beechwood Garden Fete in •aid of the local Red Cross Hospital, organised by the Working Men's Committee, has just come to hand. The total receipts were £l4B 7s. 6d. and the expenditure £2l 15s. 10d., leaving an available balance of £126 9s. Bd. The details are :- Income: Mrs. Tweedle, doll, £6 12 9d.; Miss Wheeler, doll £3 2s. 6d.; Miss Dalton, doll, .23 25.; Miss Carr, doll, £1 10s. 3d.; Miss Smith, cake, .21 16s. 9d.; Produce Stall, 0213 124. 11d.; Produce Stall, rooster, .21 Bs. Bd.; Whist Drive, £3 155.; Auction Sale, 025 10s. Bd.; Rifle Range, £2 9s. 4d.; Donkey Rides, 6s. 4d.; is. Teas, £l5 45.; 6d. Teas, 0211 4s. 114 d.; Refreshment Stall, £l5 Os. sd.•' Aunt Sally, £2 16s. 44(1.; Watch-la, c 1 £2 12s. 2.; Cocoanut, £7 Os. 7d.; Houp-la, .24 135.; Dart Board, £2 2s. 6d.; Bran Tub £2 148. 101-d.; Clock Golf, ,21. is.; Dish (Miss Carr), £1 12s. 5d.; Bowls, 10s. 6d.; Bob-la, £1 17s. 4d.; Badges, £3 4s. W.; Cycles (storing and washing), ss. 2d.; Concerts, £3 is.; Danc- ing, 7s. 2d.; Donation, .23 10s.; Collected at Cross (per Mr. Hurst), Gs. 6d.; Tickets sold, £l3 3s. 6d.; Gate Money, £l4 12s. 6d.; total, £l4B 7s. 6d.</p>
        <p>Expenditure: Band, £5 55.; Band Expenses, £4 2s. id.; Tyas, for hire of Houp-la, &amp;c., and Prizes £4 9s. 10d.; Printers, £1 Bs. 3d.; Hire of Piano, £1 Is.; Cleaning Hall, £1 ss. 8d."; Carting, 12s. 6d.; Telegrams, 2s. 2d.; Cloak- room Tickets and Concert Tickets, 2s. 4d.; Porterage, 35.; Concert Party Expenses, 17s. 3d.; Advertisements, £1 155.; Wilson and Saville; 6s. 9d.; total, £2l 15s. 10d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>CIIESIIIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CIIESIIIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>LYMM PLATOON ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Tuesday, October 10th.-7.30 p.m., Recruits' drill; 8 p.m., Platoon drill. • Thursday, October 12th.-8 p.m., Platoon drill and semaphore signalling. Orderlies.--Sergt. S. D. Moston and Private Parker.-</p>
        <p>G. R. WILLIAMSON, Platoon Corimander.</p>
        <p>Progressing Favourably.—ln reply to a cable-. gram sent by Mrs. Joseph Clare to the base hos- pital at .which her -son, Pte. Joseph Clare,: ia being treated, the following message has been received: " ProgresNing favourably, but condi- tion still serious." Pte. Joseph Clare was one of our last lads to be wounded. He was wounded along with a comrade, Lance-Corporal W. Jack- son, Newfields, about three weeks ago. Both have been wounded for the second time. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CIIESIIIRE VOLUNTEER REGINIENT.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending Saturday, October 14th. Monday.—Musketry at range for those only who have passed the class test, 7.30 p.m. Range Officer, Pte. W. H. Hartley.</p>
        <p>Tuesday.—Musketry • instruction. Fall fn at headuarters at 7.45 p.m. Thursday.—Squad drill at headquarters, 7.45 p.m.</p>
        <p>Friday.—Musketry at rauge for recruits. 7.30 . . p.m. Names of those required to attend will be nosted at headquarters. Range officer, Platoon- Sergeant C. G. Hutchings. Saturday.—Company drill. Fall in at head- quarters at 3 p.m. Orderlies.—Section-Corporal E. Pearce and Pte. F. C. Fairhurst. By order,</p>
        <p>F. A. FROST, O.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Church.—The first meeting .of the Wesley Guild winter session took place in the schoolroom on Thursday evening, and had the form of a social gathering. Rev. P. meson (president of the Guild) occupied the chair, and appealed to each member, 'of the Guild to rally round the officials and,to make the Guild more prosperous- against the time "the boys come home.' MSS' Houghton and Miss Whit- field contributed a couple of pianoforte duets, and Mr. G. Whitfield gave 'a reading. Songs were rendered by Mrs. J. A. Fairclough and Miss D. Speakman. .11efrestments were served by members of the. Social Committee, and the rest of 'the evening was spent in games, which were entered into most heartily. Mr. Herbert Ditch- field (social secretary), is to be congratulated on the succeSs of the gathering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>SETS AND CAKES.</head>
        <p>SETS AND CAKES.</p>
        <p>Raids On A Stockton Heath Confectioner.</p>
        <p>"Closed owing to want of labour. Business transferred to Prince's Cafe, Sankey-street." This notice appears in large lettering. on the drawn shop blind of Mr. Prince's bakery and confectionery establishment, London - road, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>If business is not being carried on as usual, there was a certain amount of unpleasant activity on the premises at the close of last week, which the aforesaid shop blind effectively hid from public view. But our vigilant local con- stabulary were on the alert, and Inspector Breese, who discovered that a large quantity of sweets and chocolates had been stolen, cast his official net, with the result that a big catch of thirteen juveniles are implicated in the matter. They have been formally remanded by the mag- istrates until the 10th inst.</p>
        <p>Entrance had been effected through a window at the rear of the premises.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 9th October:—</p>
        <p>_ Monday.—Company and Recruit Drill, Head- quarters, 7.45. Instructions in Care of Arms to N.C.O.'s.</p>
        <p>Thursday.—Recruits' Drill, Headquarters, 7.45 Friday.—Class Firing, Drill _Hall, 7 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Saturaay;—Shooting -aompetition at Appleton' Rifle Range, commencing at 2 o'clock, handicap terms, 24 prizes. All members are invited. ' Runday, 15th October.—N.C.O.'s Instruction Class, Chelford. Meet Victoria-square, 9 a.m. FRANK WARBURTON; Officer Commanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PENKETH,</head>
        <p>PENKETH,</p>
        <p>Licensing Transfer.—At the Warrington county Petty Sessions, on Wednesday, Mrs. Ellen Burthem applied for the transfer Of the licenoe of the Ferry Inn, Penketh.--Superin: tendent Foster raised no .objection, and said the applicant's husband was on active service. —The transfer was granted.</p>
        <p>Special Constables..-0n Thursday evening of last week and Wednesday evening of this, special meetings were held in the Council School to discuss the need there, is for special constables in this township. A fair muster put in an appearance at the first call, which was considerably augmented at the next. Forty-two names have been given for service, which will materially lighten the work of the member. Sections are formed under leaders, who are responsible whenever called out. Already prac- tical work has been done. The Commandant is Mr. J. G. Withinshaw, with Dr. Murray as secretary. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Anniversary.—The Padgate Wes- leyan Church anniversary services were held on Sunday, the preacher being the Rev. W. F. Flemington, of Liverpool. The morning text was John xjv., 1, "Let not your heart be troubled." He spoke of the confidence and trust which we must put in God, especially in these troublesome times, and urged his hearers to give Him loyal and devoted service. The evening discourse was on Luke xvi., 5, "How much owest thou unto my Lord ?" He spoke at length of the various benefits God had showered down upon us and our indebtedness yo Him. Special music was sung at both services, the morning anthem being, "I will extol Thee," and the evening anthem, "When I survey the wondrous cross," the solo in the latter being very beauti- fully rendered by Miss J. Ward, who also joined in the trio with Miss Appleton and Mr. W. J. Bate. The solo, "Into thy hands," was also ably sung by Mrs. Bate, the chorus being harmonised and sung as a trio by Mrs. Bate, Mr. T. Jones and Mr. Bate. Mr. T. Jones, of Widnes, who possesses a rich baritone -voice, was heard to advantage in the solo, "Break. break, break," a well-known setting of Tenny- son's poem. There were good congregations at both services, and the collections were in aid of trust funds. Mr. W. H. Ward presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>Support Needed.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—As an officer of the Padgate C.L. Cadet Corps I beg to bring before you the appalling laxity in the support of the parishioners of the above movement. After battling against un- congenial conditions and running fine concerts, which have lacked the support of so-called aristocratic parishioners, we have, however, come out "top dog." But this unsatisfactory state of affairs, which should be near to the hearts of all Churchgoers, since it is in direct accordance with the work of the Church, is very disheartening to the officers concerned, and can only be remedied by the support which we now lack. We are training our boys to be manly men and creditable citizens, but meet with results which are apt to make them the reverse. We do not wish for financial support so 'much as public opinion at the back of us. Yours, &amp;c., CHARLES W. PETERS, Lieut. Headquarters,</p>
        <p>Church House, Padgate,</p>
        <p>sth October, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>VISIT OF TNE REV. F. LUKE WISEMAN.</head>
        <p>VISIT OF TNE REV. F. LUKE WISEMAN.</p>
        <p>The trust services of ,the Padgate Wesleyan Church were continued on Tuesday, when a sermon was preached in the afternoon by the Rev. F. Luke Wiseman, of London. He took his text from Luke x., 36, "Which now of these three, tlinkest thou, was neighbour unto him that fell among the ,thieves?" He very favour- ably sketched the character of. the lawyer ,as shrewd, keen and alert, afterwards dividing his discourse into three theories, the robbers repre- senting the brigand; the priest and Levite, culture; and the Samaritan the human theory. Time neighbourliness, he said, was the outcome of love, and he urged his hearers to be prepared to give all and follow the example set by the Samaritan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Wesley's Tunes.</head>
        <p>Wesley's Tunes.</p>
        <p>In the evening the reverend,gentleman gave a lecture in the Schoolroom on "Wesley's Tunes," before a crowded audience. The meet- ing was presided over by Mrs. C. E. Parker, of Penketh, who mentioned that Mr. Wiseman had enriched Methodism by his music, and that she personally had a much more vivid recollection of his music than his sermons.</p>
        <p>On rising to speak, the lecturer had an enthusiastic reception. The subject, "Wesley's Hymns and Tunes," he said, was too large for him to speak upon in the short time at his dis- posal, so he appealed to his audience to decide whether he should speak Oil hymn or tunes, the majority being in favour of the latter. He stated that the English people at the time of the Methodist revival were not singing. Song is the language of, deep emotions, and it was not until these emotions were aroused that people began to sing. The first hymn composed by Charles Wesley was a 6 lines B's, "When shall my wondering soul begin?" usually sung to tune "Old. 23rd," but as he was not there to listen he very much doubted if this was the correct tune. He had with him a copy of the first issue of Wesley's Hymns and Tunes, pub- lished in 1742, which contained about 35 tunes, and also a later edition, called "Sacred Melody," published in 1760. The majority of the old Methodist tunes were in the minor mode, and he thought this type of tune was typically English, although we do not get a great deal of this type of tune in our services of to-day. Many people seem to 'think that the minor mode is for serious penetential singing, but he gave as an illustration a Scotch dance, "Charlie is my darling," which is in tly.a minor, proving that this idea is false. , If a rock is struck the ringing cadence from same is in the minor, and some of the finest tunes we have ;to-day, are written in this key, using "Aberystwyth" as an illustration. He also mentioned that the great feature of the first Methodist tunes was the melody, and prac- tically all hymns of the first period were sung in unison,</p>
        <p>The speaker's breezy humour was much appreciated by the audience, and he was heartily applauded for his singing of the hymns he used to illustrate his subject. A hearty vote of thanks to the lecturer and Mrs. Parker was proposed by the Rev. A. J. Morton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>FEARNHEAD.</head>
        <p>FEARNHEAD.</p>
        <p>Risley Independent Methodist.—The harvest festival services were held on Sunday, when two sermons were preached in the morning and evening by Mr. W. Mort, of Lymm. In the afternoon a children's service was held, presided over by Mx. L. Barton, of Croft, when special solos, duets, &amp;c., were rendered. A very in- teresting and instructive address on "Harvest Lessons " was delivered to the children by Mrs. Murray. of Feprnhead. Special music was given by the. choir at the services, "The reaper's song" and "Like the Garden of Eden" being sung under the conductorship i)f Mr. J. Barton. Mr. Percy Bate presided at the organ. The church had been very tastefully decorated with flowers, corn and fruit, which *ere afterwards sent to the Lord Derby Hos- pital, Winwick. Nr. C. Sherlock, of Fearnhead, very kindly allowing the use of his horse and trap for the purpose. The collections were in aid of trust funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>FOURPENNY RATE.</head>
        <p>A FOURPENNY RATE.</p>
        <p>For General Expenses In Rural</p>
        <p>District.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's meeting of the Warrington Rural District Cpuneil, Mr. J. C. Sutton (clerk) reported that the balance in favour of the Council was £3.322, compared with £2,394 in September, 1914, when the call was fixed at fivepenee, with £l,OBB in March, 1915, when six- pence was levied, with £2,813 in September, 1915, when the call was renuced to fivepenee, and with £2,289 in March last, when there was a further reduction to fourpenee in the 2. A payment by the County of £607 10s., made a week ago, had materially affected the balance, and it would be advisable to- continue the re- duced call of fourpence in the 2. Mr. G. Mather proposed that a call of four- pence in the 2 be made. The Chairman (Mr. W. Furness), in second- ing, said that if ,they reduced the call at the present time they would be running great risks. Their balance would not justify it. Mr. J. Robinson asked what right had they to keep over £3,000 of the ratepayers' money in hand?</p>
        <p>The Clerk. (Mr. J. C. Sutton) said the Council had riot the same moneys to receive from the County as they generapy had. He thought they. would find they were only fairly placed if they made a call of fourpence. Mr. Mather said as long as they kept to four. pence he did not think anyone would grumble. He agreed with Mr. Robinson to some extent that public bodies ougbt not to have all this money in hand unless there is a specific reason for it.</p>
        <p>The Council unanimously fixed the call at fourpence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>A PICTURISED ROMANTIC NOVEL BY ROY LMcCARDELL ADAPTED FROM THE SENSATIONAL "FLYING A.' SERIAL FILM BY.ALEC J: BRAID.</p>
        <p>meters /a this story are purely imaginary, living</p>
        <p>and have no connection with or relation to any person .j</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIX.</p>
        <p>FIRE, FURY, AND CONFUSION.</p>
        <p>During the confusion of the struggle in the saloon no one, least of all Marmaduke Smythe, had noticed that the diamond had been hurled through the air when Luke Lovell, struggling for it like all the rest, had struckithe arm of Kansas Kate, loosening her grasp upon the chain of the locket as she -held it high aboye the throng. Over the heads of the crowd it had flashed and .fastened upon the deer-horn; and so the lawyer had borne it from the scene of struggle unnoticed by anyone, not even himself.</p>
        <p>Scarcely knowing what he did, the panic-stricken lawyer darted into the first haven of safety he encountered. It was a barrel that Pete's man had converted into a kennel for a puppy given to him that day.</p>
        <p>Those who had not been imprisoned or crushed by the falling roof of the dance hall dragged them- selves from the debris. Some ran wildly through the muddy streets of the little town crying for help and calling "Fire !" Others plucked at the ruins with torn and bleeding hands, and others still fought at each other's throats. Such a group as still fought, despite the death and disaster all around them, was Frank Durand, who had come to these wild regions for the dia- mond, to see it and to lose it, and Peter Huff. The two fought madly, still tvith Luke Lovell. The quick eye of Durand had seen the blow Luke aimed at Kate to gain the 'diamond. Durand had not seen where the diamond had fallen, but in- stinctively he had torn through the little knot that fought around him, and had struck Luke a mighty blow between the eyes with all his strength and skill, for Durand had been a boxer in his earlier career. But Luke had shaken himself as a dog might do, and had closed in on Durand. Luke was nearly a. match for the two, but finally they beat him to his knees and searched him, near the barrel where the lawyer was hiding. Then, with curses of disappointment, Dlirand and Huff rushed back to the dance hall just as the roof fell.</p>
        <p>"Are you hurt, dearie?" asked Kate, turning to Esther from the sickening sight of the wreckage and the hideous cries behind them.</p>
        <p>Esther was sick and faint. "I am not hurt," she said, tremulously; "but where is Arthur? Has he escaped? Is he killed?" "Was he your friend?" asked Kate. "Why, that's the boss, Mr. John Powell, the millionaire, who has just bought the mines around here. He had just arrived when a row broke out. Is he your friend?" Esther could have said that the ...young man known as "John Powell" was her only friend in all the world save the poor Italian, Quabba, and a gipsy woman, bereft of her reason, in far-off Rich- mond.</p>
        <p>There was no cry from the pinioned man whom Esther found. As one dead he lay, half-covered by the wreckage, but with face unscarred and un- disfigured. Already the red edges of the fire were creeping near to him. Esther grasped at his shoulders, but the task was beyond her strength, until Quabba and their friend the outcast, Kate, joined their efforts with hers. Together they dragged the unconscious man from the burning ruins of the dance hall. Men were arriving with stretchers from the mine, and upon one of these Arthur was placed; and then Esther, her strength and resolution leaving her, felt a deadly numbness come over her.</p>
        <p>"I am a doctor," said Durand. who had been stooping over Arthur, and thrusting aside several of the rough group who had gathered around the stretcher. "There are other people to help be- sides this one," remarked Durand to these. "Hustle. I'll take care of Mr. Powell."</p>
        <p>"You 'think he'll live?" asked Huff, " Are you really a doctor?" "He is badly injured internally," replied Durand. "Several ribs are broken and one of his arms. He should be taken to a hospital, as I think an operation is necessary. Yes, I am a doctor."</p>
        <p>"Oh. don't be sore with me," retorted Mr. Huff. "I don't care whether you're a hoss doctor or a human doctor, or no doctor. But as Mr. Powell's death may mean the stopping of these works, you use your own judgment as to whether we will try to take care of him here or get him to Los Angeles. You had better attend to him, and I'll stick around here and search everybody, living or dead, and dig in those ruins when they're cool enough,' for that big stone you say is called The Diamond from the Sky. Don't worry about me. I'll be on the square." It was no idle boast of Durand's that he was a physician and the possessor of a high degree. His skill had been marked in his profession, and had he chosen to devote himself to it Durand would have been famous both as a physician and a surgeon. Mixed in several scandals, he had been forced to fly from New York. Paris. and Rome. But of recent years he had wholly abandoned the dealing in illicit drugs for the more exciting and desperate profession of international gem crook— sensational success in this line having secured for him the pseudonym of "The King of Diamonds." Arthur was badly injured. How badly Durand had surmised at his first superficial examination. But Durand had also surmised that his patient's youth and strong constitution would enable him to stand a hurried journey to Los Angeles ere the reaction from the shock set in. Then, too, a daring scheme was already formulating in Durand's mind. Arthur crushed and helpless— " John Powell," the millionaire, as Durand knew him—was wholly at his mercy. Surely when "John Powell" recovered strength and consciousness, common gratitude would impel him to retain the services of the physician who had saved his life by hastening with his shattered form from the wilderness to the care and convenience of the city. •</p>
        <p>Besides this Durand remembered Vivian speak- ing of Blair StanleiTh and the strange regard and fondness the now injured man had always held for his cousin. For Vivian had informed Durand and her other former associate, De Vaux, that Arthur and Blair were cousins, though she had deemed it 'best to keep the secret of Arthur being</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>)ocee,creceeecececeeece,</p>
        <p>Flying - A Serial Film.</p>
        <p>J .c4l</p>
        <p>• other than " John Powell," as he was known in the West.</p>
        <p>Hastily but skilfully improvising an ambulance by the aid of the stretcher and "John Powell's " high-powered roadster, and taking with him a mechanic to aid him in running and caring for the great car, Durand departed from Mammoth, with his still unconscious patient swathed in blankets. When Esther had recovered the improvised auto- mobile ambulance was gone. Esther knew nothing of Durand save what Peter Huff could tell her in answer to her frantic inquiries. "He's a doctor, that's all I know," Mr. Huff had replied. "He's a big doctor, too, and an old friend of your friend, 'Mr. Powell'; at least, he's a friend of friends of his. You ought to be thankful. Dr. Durand told me Mr. Powell' wouldn't have a chance for his life unless he was taken to a hos- pital at once and operated on."</p>
        <p>There were tears -in Kate's worldly-wise eyes' at Esther's sorrow and distress. "If you're not sat- isfied you go follow them," Kate whispered. "Have you any money, pet?" "Yes, thank you," murmured Esther. "I have quite enough money. I will have Quabba get our horses from the stable, and we will ride to the station and take the first train to Los Angeles." "You will sometimes think of me, won't you.? asked Kate, huskily. "Here is the prayer from the locket. Did you know that it was in it ?" "No," said Esther. "I don't think anyone knew it. There seemed to be a secret spring that was touched."</p>
        <p>"Your mother put it there wheii•you were a baby," she murmured. "I know it, I felt it; there is a faint perfume like lavender about it. A mother's prayer !" She sobbed as she took the paper from her bosom, where she had thrust it when the fight had begun in the dance hall. "Even a sinner like I am is made the better 139 it," continued Kate. "And will you pray ,for me?"</p>
        <p>"Always," said Esther, brokenly, as she took the crumpled Paper with its faint, vague, old-time Perfume. Then Esther put her arms around the outcast woman and kissed her.</p>
        <p>The most surprised man in Mammoth was Mar- maduke Smythe when he discovered that he had the diamond. How was he to have known that the diamond knocked out of Kate's hand would settle on the• precious deer-head?' But there it was, in the kennel with him. No wonder the law- Yer was surprised. Marmaduke had grown accua- tomed to surprises since he had come to America in search of an elusive "him." He. too, was sunl- moned by the diamond's powers for good or ill. He felt it his duty to restore it to the Stanleigh heir, but he had no intention of keeping it about him. Twenty miles from Mammoth he hid it ill the hollow of a tree near a sign-post and made a note of it in his memorandum-book. His way led him to where two children—Toodles and Tommy—came toddling down the road with their dolls and playthings. A glass of milk would refresh him, Smythe thought. The children indi- cated their home to the gaunt but kindly stranger, andi there Smythe made his way and was regaled with milk and bread and butter and honey by the rancher's kindly wife. The children went down the road to play "house' as was their custom, beneath the live oak. Toodles found the gleaming bauble. She was a girl and knew it was a necklace, and with cries of apPre" ciation and delight she seized it. Immediately her little brother Tommy demanded it. A slap, scream, and Toodles jerked away the necklace and ' walked off indignantly with both her doll and her treasure trove, while the little boy pattered down the road to run home and tell his mother. Disaster fell upon Toodles. She was playing with the neck- lace in the bee yard when a bee resented the in- trusion and stung the little visitor. With a al of pain she lifted her arm and the gem fell on the alighting-board of a beehive. Amid the ruins of his dance hall Peter Hug watched the diggers with a keen glance that never, wavered. He troubled little that the "saloon had gone. Convinced that the diamond was among the debris he watched the men at work searching for the jewel. A little old squat-built workman aroused his suspicions, for the little old workman had furtively picked up and secreted some shilling object. Forced to disgorge, the workman showed a still warm silver dollar. With a bitter curse of disappointment and anger Pete smashed him in the face, knocking him sprawling. The old fellow picked himself up. "You think I had the diamond which was lost last night, eh?" he asked. "Well I'll tell you since you've been go handy with your fists. One of the boys says he's seen that stranger with the side-whiskers running out with the dia- mond when the fight broke loose."</p>
        <p>_ There was such an air of sincerity about the fellow's words that Peter Huff rushed from the work, commandeered the old automobile of a friend and went in pursuit of the fugitive stranger with the side-whiskers. So remarkable a figure as Mar- maduke, not to mention the deer's head, had left a blazed trail. Twenty-one miles from Mammo.th Marmaduke was overtaken and held up. The (113" mond was not on his person, but Pete and his P 5,1 found the memorandum of its hiding-place. Bach to the oak they bore the protesting Smythe, hut' the diamond was gone again. Pete and his Pa; parted from the lawyer with curses and return to Mammoth.</p>
        <p>There was great excitemeni in Los Angeles when news of the accident was spread. Only that morn- ing the papers had announced that "John Powell," the y9ung millionaire and mining magnate. vi giving a reception to mark the inauguration 0 his new home. Now he lay at the point of death. and the newspaper men were told that Dr. Fral Durand, the friend of the young man, regarde his condition as serious. . _</p>
        <p>When Esther arrived in the town she hurried t° see Arthur, but the servant dashed her hopes t° the ground. "Doctor Durand's orders," he Said' "are that no one can see 'Mr. Powell.' His con. dition is too critical." And while Esther Wa9 refused admission to the man she loved, the "b of Diamonds " had installed Vivian Marston 89 nurse.</p>
