      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>FOOTBALL FIELD.</head>
        <p>THE FOOTBALL FIELD.</p>
        <p>v • • • ••"•••••••••••••••••••:-:-:-:-:-:.:-:-t-4,4•4••:.:.:.4-:••:- 4.4 s• *4.4.4. 4-:-:-:-:••••••••• • • •.. • • .</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>full-back was superior to Dainteth, but many prom- ising movements by the threequarters lacked finis.</p>
        <p>(Lancashire Section.)</p>
        <p>The teams were :</p>
        <p>Warrington: Horrocks; Jordan, Potter, Tranter, Jolley (J.) (Runcorn); Douglas, Nicholas; Harmer, Matthews, Green, Cunliffe, Clegg and Jones. Widnes: Dainteth; Redmond, Reid; Taylor and Breen; Parkinson, O'Garra (J.); Smith, Beswick, Townsend, O'Garra (H.), Fairclough and Rosecleer.</p>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. 1 2 8 Warrington .. 1 1 5</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 7, 1916.</p>
        <p>with the score 1-0 in favour Of the home team. The second portion opened in favour of the visit•rs, and Hurst and Buckley were showing good combination, and after several futile efforts Buckley succceeded in equalising the score. Keen Play now followed and some good passing move- ments by the home forwards were intercepted by the opposing half-backs, and plaY was soon again in the vicinity of the home goal, where Buckley was just wide with a good effort. A little later Hurst was just too hi,&gt;ll with a fast shot. The visitors kept up the pressure and Clare gained the lead. The home team now made desperate efforts to get on equal terms, but their final shots were easily cleared. Corner-kicks to both teams fol- lowed, but not improved upon. After even play the visitors returned to the attack and Clare in- creased the lead with a high shot. Directly after the final whistle was blown, the result being : Orford Juniors, 3 coals; Bewsey A. 5.., 1 goal.</p>
        <p>PENKETH JUNIORS v. HIGHFIELD JUNIORS. On the Penketh Recreation Ground, before a fair number of spectators. Penketh Juniors: Ainsworth; Moss, Thomasson; Atkinson, Sankey., Boyle; ,Magee, Swift, Glover, Pearson, Winstanley. Highfield Juniors: Sadler; Jepson, Worrall; Barnes, Hankey, Wishlade; Moulsdale, Peattie, Highfield, Ford, Tomlinson. Referee, Mr. Holt. Very quickly each defence had to be on the alert. The home forwards were showing good combination and Magee put across some good centres, which were troublesome to the visitors' defence. After twenty minutes' play Magee regis- tered the first goal for the home team. After each end had been visited Highfield scored the equalis- ing goal. The home team now played in a very aggressive manner, nd Pearson, gaining posses- sion, put them ahead with a good shot. Penketh were now on good terms with themselves and again assailed the visitors' goal, where Sadler showed good form between the uprights, but Magee beat him again for the third goal. Interval: Penketh, 3; Highfield, 1. The Penketh Lads made the first advance in the second half, but offside tactics spoiled the effort. and after the free-kick some hard play was wit- nessed, but the home team began to show the better combination and gained the offensive, Sadler saving well on several occasions. However, Atkin- son got through and scored the fourth goal. After this the visitors acted on the defensive, but, in spite of their efforts, Atkinson scored again before the call of time. Result :—Penketh, 5; Highfield, 1.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD CADETS 'v. ST. ELPHIN'S.</p>
        <p>In Victoria Park,</p>
        <p>Latchford Cadets: Miller; Williams, Jones: Blackmore, Plumb, Massey; Davenport, Bradshaw, Gibson. Sankey; Cartwright. St. Elnhin's : M. Jones; Tunstall, Furness; Wat- son, Miller, Cooper; Percival, England, A. Jones, Gleave, Barton. Referee, Mr. Povey. The Cadets won the toss and played with the wind. They led off with an attack on their oppon- ents' goal, but failed to break through the defence of Tunstall and Furness, and play was forced to mid-field. The game continued to be vigorously contested and the teams appeared very evenly balanced. but after twenty minutes' play Gleave steered the ball safely past S. Miller and registered the " Saints' " first goal. Each goal was subjected to keen attacks, .but without any further success, the score at the interval being :—St. Elphin's, 1; Cadets, 0. Gibson restarted for the Cadets and the ball was rushed to the St. Elphin's goal, where Jones cleared smartly from Gibson. Play was transferred to the opposite end and a. corner-kick was conceded. Miller cleared well, and a little later A. Jones scored St. Elphin's second coal. This was followed by a strong attack on the " Saints'" goal, where M. Jones sayed in splendid fashion, and play was again transferred to the Cadets' goal, and St. Elphin's increased their lead a few minutes before time. Reslilt :—St. Elphin's, 3 goals; Latchford Cadets, nil. EMMANUEL v. CHESTER R.A.M.C.</p>
        <p>On the Chester Racecourse. Gradually the visit- ors were put on the defensive, and after 15 min- utes' play Sergt. Rothwell scored for the home team, and 20 minutes later Corporal Wilson added a second goal. Emmanuel now set up a strong attack, and were awarded a corner, but Eaton put the ball dead. Half-time score: Chester, 2 goals; Emmanuel, nil After changing ends Emmanuel put up a strong attack, but the R.A.M.C. goalkeeper cleared splen- didly. The home ..team attacked the visitors strongly. and put on two more goals, making the result: Chester R.A.M.C., 4 goals; Emmanuel, nil.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. St. Elphin's v. Penketh. Juniors; H. Berry. Latchford Cadets v. Orford Juniors; G. Blease Highfield,v. Howley United; 'A. N. Other. Latchford Recs. v. Bewsey A.S.; A. Holt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>WIDNE S NUN.</head>
        <p>WIDNE S NUN.</p>
        <p>• An Unexpected Reverse For The Warrington Team. There was quite a contrast in the teams at Wid- nes on Saturday, when the " Chemicals " opened their season. They had a strong team out, no fewer than eight regular players being included in their ranks, while, on the other hand, the visitors had about four of the selected players absent. Their greatest loss was that of Jolley, who always proves himself a tower of strength at full-back. In the threequarter line Baker and Mills were missing, and the Warrington full-back's brother, J. Jolley, of Runcorn, appeared on the wing. Widnes started with the wind, and the opening Phases of the game were in favour of the visitors, whose forwards bore down on the home line. Dain- teth mulled a kick, but the Warrington men did not make the best of the opportunity. The home threequarter-backs then essayed a passing move- ment, which Tranter and Potter stopped in fine style. Shortly afterwards Horrocks was called upon to save his lines, which he easily did, and from his kick Nicholas got possession The half-back ran strongly, and Jolley, getting possfssion, made a bid for the line, but was pushed into touch. A dan- gerous onslaught by the visiting forwards was checked, and O'Garra broke through and brought relief. A minute later Horrocks made a fine save from the feet of the home' forwards, but the home team soon came again, and Reid put the finishing touch to a passing movement by Taylor, J. O'Garra, Redmond and Johnson, and opened the score for Widnes after twenty minutes' play. Although the goal kick was taken from the easiest position by Dainteth, the ball'hit the goal post and rebounded into play. Following this reverse Warrington played desperately, Tranter and Potter making good efforts, but they were not supported as they might have been. Dainteth, the home full-back, was weak under pressure, and had he not been well covered by Taylor, Warrington must have scored. The home forwards now made another fine run and reached the Warrington line. Here a scrimmage took place, and Beswick picked up smartly and cut through the visitors' defence to score a good try. Dainteth add- ing the extra points. Warrington straightaway visited the home end, Cunliffe making a fine dribble. The ball crossed to Jolley, who took it finely and scored grand try, the same player im- proving. Half-time found Widnes leading by one goal two tries (8 points), to Warrington's one goal one try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>WARRINGTON FORWARDS TOO EAGER.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FORWARDS TOO EAGER.</p>
        <p>The game restarted , with dashing work by the visiting forwards, but they were too eager and were Penalised, touch being found at the half-way line. The visiting threequarter-backs then commenced a Passing movement, and Tranter made a good run, but Potter, failed to !dim his pass. Shortly after- wards Nicholas and Jolley made bold bids for the home line, but were pulled up inches short. •At the other end Horrocks made a good save from Tay- lor and O'Garra, while Redmond was also tackled when going strong for the line. Ai- time drew near the home forwards fore&amp; the pacc, hit. the War• riagton defence was souucz and' no furtlit.r soaring took place.</p>
        <p>Result: Widnes, one goal two 'tries (8 points). Warrington, one goal one try (5 points)</p>
        <p>THE PLAYERS AND THEIR PLAY</p>
        <p>For the home club. Parkinson played his first match and created a favourable impression at the base of the scrummage. He was well supported by J. O'Garra. Bes\vick and Townsend played well in the forward line. For the visitors. Nicholas played a great game both on the attack and the defence. Horrocks at</p>
        <p>- -_w</p>
        <p>Sold everywhere In boxes, labelled Is-3d and 3s-Od.</p>
      </div>
