      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Widnes</head>
        <p>Widnes</p>
        <p>Swinton Rochdale H. .. 2 1 7 Salford Barrow Leigh Leeds</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>SWINTON AT WILDERSPOOL TO-M%. '</head>
        <p>SWINTON AT WILDERSPOOL TO-M%. '</p>
        <p>In having Swinton as our guests at Wilderspool to-day we have a team which can boast of not yet having been beaten this season. In the Northern Union League they share with Hull and Leeds the honours of a 100 per cent. average. Last week they punished Wigan in summary fashion, beating them by 16 points to 3. The encounter at Wilderspool to-day may, therefore, be looked forward to with intense interest. That a keen game will take place one may be sure</p>
        <p>Warringtbn's team will be selected from: Jolley; Horrocks, Tranter, Potter, Baker, Lloyd and Griffiths; Nicholas. Douglas and Young; Harmer, Green, Cunliffe, Matthews, Jones, Glegg, Dumbell and Ward. Horrocks and Potter stand a chance of being pro- moted from the Manchester and District League, where they have done conspicuous service for Rylands's team. Towards the close of last season Lloyd travelled about with Rochdale Hornets, and this season has been playing Association with Ever- ton. His merits in both codes are high. Swinton team will be selected from the following: Ryder; Hall, Dean, Dawson (captain), Talbot and Winstanley; Kilgariff, Boardman and Williamson; Holden, Private Moss, Crudden, Rol‘leY, Price, Blewer, Price, Mee and Markland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>HEATHSIDE JUST BEAT RYLANDS'S.</p>
        <p>The Rylands's and Heathside Intermediate teams met in a friendly encounter on the Rylands's Recreation Ground on Saturday, and after a fast and hard-fought game the visitors ran out winners by two tries (6 points) to one goal one try (5 points). Heathside had most of the play in the first half, and opened their score through Dale. the goal kick failing. During some exciting play on the Heathside line, Rylands's were awarded a free kick, which Swanton turned to good account. Half- time: Heathside, one try (3 points); Rylands's, one goal (2 points). Rylands's attacked on resuming, but the Heath- side defence was very sound, Griffiths in particular playing a fine game at full-back. The visitors broke away and after a good passing movement Dale went over, but the goal kick was a failure. Soon after- wards. during an attack by the home backs, Hill scored, but the try was not augmented. Result: Heathside. two tries (6 points); Rylands's, one goal one try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN v. ECCLES BOROUGH The second home fixture of the Town Club on the Hood-lane enclosure attracted a crowd of 500 spectators to witness a keen and robust game, is- which the home team, from, the commoicement of play, always appeared to have the measure of their opponents well in hand, and well deserved their victory at the close of hostilities. The following players lined out, under the control of Mr. Rylance, referee : , Warrington Town : Kite; Leah, Heywood; Ste- phens, Whitfield, Ditchfield; Aspinall, Kendrick, Giblin, Owen. Eccles Borough: Howe; Gilmour, R. Walker; Wilson, A. Walker, Wilkinson; Lee, Foulkes, Cope, Miller, Mitchell. The visitors won the toss and decided to play down the slope, with a slight breeze in their favour. Siddall set the ball in motion and the home forwards quickly advanced on the visitors' citadel, a corner-kick resulting. This was well- placed by Aspinall' and a good clearance by Walker enabled the visitors to reply with a spirited raid into the home quarters, but Leah and Heywood checked their advance smartly by keen tackling. An accident to Kendrick caused a brief stoppage, but he was able to resume play. By the aid of a free-kick the visitors got well down and were getting into a dangerous position when they were pulled up for offside. The visitors' left were com- bining well and a good centre by Mitchell was well cleared by Heywood, and a little later Kite had not much difficulty in clearing a slow shot. After a short spell of mid-field play Foulkes passed neatly to Lee and the latter allowed a good open- ing to pass by showing too much finesse, after which he finally placed flit ball wide. Stephens enabled Aspinall to get away by a 'good forward pass, but Walker neatly robbed him and trans- ferred to Miller, who forced a corner at the home end. Mitchell placed well and Heywood was pro- minent with some strong clearances. Good. play by Ditchfield was allowed to pass owing to Ken- drick being too slow to accept the opening. Fol- lowing some good mid-field exchanges both ends were visited in turn and offside also spoiled good openings to both teams. Giblin and Owen again led an attack on the visitors' goal and the latter gave Howe a good shot to deal with, and then Gilmour cleared with a big kick. Play was mostly in the visitors' half and Foulkes presented Lee with a good opening to brealc.,away, but he was placed offside. The visitors were again put on the defensive, Gilmour conceding a corner-kick, and from this the visitors' goal had a narrow escape. A goal-kick gave them relief and Foulkes shot very wildly at the opposite end, and from this clearance Siddall was nearly through the back division, when he was brought to earth by Gilmour, a penalty- kick resulting, from which Aspinall scored the opening goal. From the centre-kick the visitors were smartly away and Lee was too closely watched by Ditch- field to allow him to become dangerous. After, another spell of mid-field play Giblin and Owen again advanced on the visitors' goal and the latter was just wide of the goal with a good shot. The visitors now made tracks for the home goal and Kite was called upon to save, and from this the ball was smartly returned by Mitchell, causing Kite to fall across the goalmouth to bring off a fine save. The visitors were now making strenuous , efforts to equalise and a fine shot by Walker from • mid-field just skimmed the bar. Play continued L in the home half and Miller tested Kite with a I hard drive, which was cleared successfully, and • mid-field play followed. Half-time: Warrington , Town, 1; Eccles Borough, 0. On resuming, the home team changed the front line, Aspinall going to his usual position at centre- forward and Kendrick taking up the extreme right. The visitors advanced keenly and Leah and Hey- wood cleared in turn. An attempt of the home forwards was broken up by Walker and Gilmour, and in front of the home goal Foulkes shot over the bar. Good combination by the home forwards enabled them to get to close quarters with the visitors' defence, and Kendrick forced Gilmour to concede a corner, but the ball was placed behind. Ar brief attack on both goals was followed by a smart piece of play between Giblin' and Owen, and the final effort of the latter was crowned by a splendid shot, which Howe made a desperate attempt to save, but failed. From the centre-kick the visitors attacked vigorously. Mitchell and Miller causing trouble to the home defence, and Cope was robbed in the nick of time by Heywood. For a while play was of the give-and-take order; then Aspinall made an individual effort and forced a fruitless corner. Play was now chiefly in favour of the home team, and, despite the efforts, of the visitors to get a start from their right wing, , Ditchfield was ever on the alert. and. converted many well-intended passes to Lee to the, advantage of his own forwards, and Owen was always a source of trouble to the visitors' backs. Walker, at centre- half, set his-forwards going, but their final efforts were weak and easily repulsed. A strong attack on the Berough goal by a good centre from Kendrick saw Howe fist the ball out three times in as many seconds, and then Aspinall was a shade too high with a fast shot. After a slight cessation of hos- tilities owing to an accident to Whitfield, the visitors appeared to revive their efforts and Kite had to. leave his goal to clear a long, shot, and a little later he made a splendid save at close quar- ters from Cope. Immediately afterwards he was conspicuous in clearing a fine effort from Lee. Play was again transferred to the opposite end, where Howe saved a low shot from Siddall. An. other visit to the home goal was without result, and at the call of time the result was :—Warrington Town, 2 goals; Eccles Borough, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS.</head>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Town 2 Eccles Borough .... 0 Atherton Hurst South Liverpool .... 6 Hyde Altrincham</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN AT ECCLES</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN AT ECCLES</p>
        <p>• TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Warrington Town, who had the merit of beating Eccles Borough at Hood-lane on Saturday, pay them a' return visit to-day, when 'the team will be: Kite; Leah, Millward; Stephens, Whitfield, Hey- wood; Midge, Siddall, Aspinall, Ditchfield and Owen—a good selection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISMICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISMICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. F. A. PTS. Howley United 4 3 0 1 14 3 7 St. Elphin's Penketh Juniors Latchford Recs. Bewsey A.S 4 2 2 0 15 11 4 Orford Juniors Highfield Juniors Latchford Cadets</p>
        <p>HOWLEY UNITED v. LATCHFORD RECS. In Victoria Park before a good crowd of spectators. Howley United: Walters; Rowlinson, Foster; Hind, Miller, Hoarty; Humphries, Woodhouse, Davies, Potter; Boyle. Latchford Recs. : Lockhart: Brandwood, Price; Bancroft, Green, Trainor; Heesom, Warburton, Willcock, Cooper, James. Referee, Mr. Burgess.</p>
        <p>Howley won the toss and played with the breeze in their favour. Willcock set the ball rolling and immediately advanced on the United goal, Row- linson clearing well when hard pressed. The United then settled down to their game and transferred play to the opposite end, where they gained a free-kick, Woodhouse sending in a fine shot, which Lockhart cleared in splendid style. The United continued to have the best of the exchanges, when offside against Boyle gave relief to the Recs.' defenee. The game was now keenly contested and each defence was severely tested, and a-few minutes before the interval' a, most re- grettable accident occurred to Price, one of the Recs.' full-backs, who had the misfortune to break his leg, and after being temporarily attended to he was conveyed to the Infirmary and detained. The interval arrived without any score being made. On resuming the second portion, the Recs. were handicapped in having only ten players, but, nevertheless, the game was contested at a very fast pace and each goal was hotly attacked, but without success. This order of play continued until the last twenty minutes, when the United gained the upper hand and heavily bombarded the Recs.' goal, Woodhouse on several occasions being very near to success with good shots. The Recs.' de- fence ,held out well. until the call of time, when the result was a goalless draw.</p>
        <p>BEWSEY ADULT SCHOOL v. ORFORD</p>
        <p>JUNIORP.</p>
        <p>On the Lovely-lane ground. BewseY A.S. : Stephens; Atkinson, Mains; Davies, Roylance, Dickenson; Schofield, Peyton, Bradburn, Warren, Hassall. Orford: Conley; Griffiths, Doughty; Austin, Witton, Walters; Rothwell, Buckley, Hurst, Clare, Belshaw.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. S. ,C. Essenhigh. The visitors quickly advanced and Stephens was called upon to clear and a free-kick in mid-field enabled the visitors to return, but the home backs were safe in their tackling and their forwards transferred, play to the visitors' end, where Witton was penalised. From the ensuing kick Ha ssall scored a ne goal for the Adults. The visitors now played strongly to equalise, but met with• strong opposition and were driven back. After end-to-end play, Buckley, with a fine open- ing. failed to score. Play was now very fast and each, goal was attacked without result, and then offside against Hurst robbesl the visitors of a good chance. After more even flay the interval arrived</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>ADDITIONS TO THE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>ADDITIONS TO THE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>At the monthly tneeting of the Warrington and District Junior League, held last Monday night at the Peter-street Dining Rooms, Mr. Simpson pre- siding, applications for membership were read by the Secretary from Widnes St. Mary's, Appleton Hornets, Widnes Juniors, and Grappenhall. These were accepted, and now the membership is twelve clubs.</p>
        <p>Fixtures are arranged for tI new clubs to com- mence playing next week. An application from Mr. Cartwright, of the Cadets, for the club to make a collection iti aid Of Price, who unfortunately got his leg broken in the Recs. v. Howley match, was granted,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>SANKEY ATHLETIC V. WARRINGTON CENTRAL.</p>
        <p>On the Atherton Quay ground</p>
        <p>Sankey Athletic:- Gle;ve; Brinksman, Gough; Worrall, Daniels, Hunt; Kenyon, Banner, W. Tay- lor, E. Taylor and Kenyon. Central: H. Warburton; Leah, Glover; Davies, Gilbert, W. Warburton; Green, Emery, Hands, A. N. Other and A: N. Other. The visitors at once made for the home goal, Where Leah cleared cleverly. After a series of even exchanges in midfield Hands scored for the visitors. The interval arrived with the score 1 goal against none in favour of the Central. On resuming the Athletic at once assumed the aggressive., and forced a corner, which was well cleared by Leah. The home forwards combined well, and S. Kenyon, re- ceiving from Taylor, equalised the score with a good shot. The game ended in a draw of one goal each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>ASSOCIATION NOTES.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION NOTES.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Association team, after a lapse of three weeks, appeared before their own supporters last Saturday, and played the first of their engagementS against the Eccles Borough team. As will be remembered, when the Borough visited Hood-lane last season, one of the most interesting games was played, and ended in a victory for the home team. On this occasion both clubs were strongly represented, and the supporters were fully confident that their favourites would again obtain both points, especially after their performance the previous week at Dingle Park.</p>
        <p>At the last moment tile home club had to make alterations in their team, Millward being unable to turn out, and Heywood, who was home on furlough, occupied the vacancy, and Kendrick was given a further trial, to the ex- clusion of Illidge.</p>
        <p>For the home team, Kite was. rarely in diffi- culties, although he had several very dangerous shots to negotiate.</p>
        <p>Leah and Heywood were very reliable in their tackling, and kicked strongly when hard pressed. Ditchfield was the most Prominent in a hard-working middle line.</p>
        <p>Owen and Giblin were the most dangerous wing, and Aspinall did better when in his usual postion as centre-forward. Siddall was ahard worker, but receives close attention from his former colleagues, and Kendrick was provided with many openings, but did not turn them to the best account.</p>
        <p>For the visitors, Howe did well in goal, althongh he was defeated twice and he received splendid assistance from Gilmour and Walker. The visitors' middle line were only a'moderate trio. Mitchell and Miller were their most dangerous forwards. Lee was not able to shine as is his usual custoni, owing to the watchful- ness of Ditchfield, who cleverly checked the serpentine performances of Lee, much to the delight of the crowd.</p>
        <p>The Town club officials can look back nom/ the performance of their players during the first month of the playing season with much satis- faction, especially so as they have appeared on opponents' grounds on three occasions and twice on their own enclosure. In the away fixtures they have met their heaviest reverse 5-0, at Prenton Park, and they also obtained both points at their second visit to the Dingle, a perform- ance which most of the Combination clubs will fail to accomplish when appearing there. And in their home fixtures they have seeured victories against Tranmere and the Borough. To gain these results the committee have un- doubtedly a good array of talent to make their selections from, and have already called upon the services of twenty-three players, and of these Kite, Leah, Ditchfield and A spinal' have</p>
        <p>appeared in every match. Their record is: Played 5, won,3, lost 2, and they have scored 7 goals against 9;., yielding 6 points. ,</p>
        <p>Many of the Town supporters would like to See'Ecele'stan have an extended trial.</p>
        <p>The Juniors have soon sdkled down in earliest to 'their allotted task, and are turning out in full numbers every week, and in Victoria Park their displays are eagerly followed by a good crowd of spectators.</p>
        <p>Last week much interest was centred in the Howley and Latchford Recs. game, for the pre- vious week the United gained a smart victory over St. Elphin's,hp were last season's cham- pions, and the Recs. had been busy strengthen- ing their ranks to make a strong effort to inflict the first defeat on the prospective champions for this season, as it is well known the United hay. spared no effort to get together a strong team to gain the championship. The game was very keenly contested, and ended in a goalless draw, but it was marred by a regrettable dcci- dent to Price, one of the Recs. full-backs, who had the misfortune to have a itg broken a few minute's before the interval. The unforturate Player was promptly conveyed to the hospital, and from the latest bulletin he is progressing very favourably.</p>
        <p>All the members of the Junior League extend to him the fullest sympathy and wish him a speedy recovery.</p>
        <p>The Bewsey Adult School team appeared on their own ground against the Orford Juniors, and, owing to their smart performance the pre- vious week away from home against the High- field team, when they snatched a victory in the closing stages of the game, their supporters were fully expecting them to repeat the performance against their neighbours, the Orford Juniors. On this occasion the Adults did not realise their own anticipations, and at the close of play they were defeated by three clear goals.</p>
        <p>The Orford Juniors have greatly strengthened their attack by securing the transfer of Buckley from St. Elphin's, and in this match he woo, their most dangerous forward.</p>
        <p>Penketh Juniors were visited by the Highfield Juniors in the return fixture on the Rec., and on this occasion the visitors gave a much more improved display than when the team played their opening game, but the Penketh lads again proved superior- and scored-on tve-occastotts, to which the Cisitors only replied once. Magee, Moss, Thomason and Pearson were the pick of the home team, and Sadler; the visitors' goal- keeper, stood out above his colleagues in spite lof the score against him.</p>
        <p>Latchford Cadets were opposed by St. Elphin's last week, and in spite of their, strengthened forces, they again had to acknow- ledge defeat The Elphin's, making several changes in their team from the previous week, were fully justified in their selections, and gained a good victory by three clear goals.</p>
        <p>Lockhart, who kept goal for St. Elphin's last season, gave a good display for the Recs. against Howley.</p>
        <p>Green als-o played well at centre-half, as also lid .lieesom at-outside-right.</p>
        <p>Stanley Woodhouse is proving an attraction at the Park this season, his dribbling is fine, but occasionally overdone.</p>
        <p>Miller and Foster played well in defence, but on the whole the Howley forwards were not up to their usual standard.</p>
        <p>There are complaints about referees who secure appointments and fail to carry them out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S v. PENKETH JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>At Steel-street</p>
        <p>St. Elphin's team: W. Jones; W. Tunstall, V Furness; WE. Watson, H. Miller, H. Gleave ; J Chisholm, G. England, A. Jones, W. Lee and J Holden. Reserves: D. Cooper and J. Barton. -</p>
