        <p>From Chemists 2/9 and 5/-; or The Garton Liniment Co. Pontefract, Yorks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>STAGE AND KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAGE AND KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Royal Court Theatre.</p>
        <p>"The Case of Lady Camber." At the Court Theatre, Warrington, on October 9th, for a week, the attraction will be "The Case of Lady Camber," which has recently had a long and prosperous run of over ZOO perform- ances at the Savoy Theatre. The play, which is from the prolific pep of Mr. Horace Annesley Vachell, is packed from beginning to end with situations of absorbing interest, and the dialogue is written in the author's happiest vein. Added interest is given to the cast by the fact that it includes Miss Dorothy Kendal Grimston, a clever daughter of the famous Mrs. Kendal, who plays Lady Camber. Harley Napier, the part created in town by Mr. H. R. Irving, will be represented by Mr. Edmund 'Kennedy, while Miss Dorothy Dewhurst, a young actress of whom a great deal will be heard in future, will appear as Nurse Yorke. Mr. Bernard Copping will appear as Lord Camber, and Mr. Frank Bertram as Sir Bedford Slufter. Other members of the company are Miss Edith Cruikshank and Miss Edith Madelle. Mr. Edmund Kennedy, it will be remem- bered, played in "Lucky Durham" at the Royal Court Theatre some time ago. The two matinees at this house on Mondays and Thursdays are going strong., Palace and Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>Monday's matinee was such a big success that the management have decided to continue these performances. All the artistes turned up on Monday and everything ran as smoothly as at the evening shows. That the managefhent of the Hippodrome are meeting a popular demand in branching out with a matinee is proved up to the hilt. .</p>
        <p>Next week the Pour Holloways, comedy acrobatic and trick cyclists op the tight wire are coming, and Jessica Black and Co. will pre- sent a catchy comedy farce, "A Girl in Every Port." The Bros. St. John, in an instgumental and patter act, and Mdlle. Cameo in living marble statue studios, will also appear in the course of an attractive programme.</p>
        <p>The Kinemas. There is now showing at the Star Kinema House, Church-street, a fine film entitled, "Little Mary Sunshine," introducing Baby Osborne. For the first half of next week, Mark Twainjs famous book: " Pudden-head Wilson," will be screened, and for the latter half we are to have Pauline Frederick in "The Spider."'</p>
        <p>At the Grand Electric Theatre the "star" picture now showing is "Carmen," and the first episode of "Peg o' the Ring." During the first half of next week there will be screened "The White Pearl," along with the nineteenth episode of "The Diamond From the Sky." For the latter half "Prince and Pauper" is the foremost picture, while there will also be the second episode of "Peg o' the Ring."</p>
        <p>The Pavilion, Sankey Green, is now showing Hall Caine's novel, "The Christian." For the first half of next week a fine drama, "The Out- rage," and the nineteenth e4isode of " The Diamond From the Sky" will be shown, and during the latter half of the week "The Battle of the Somme" and the first episode of "Peg o' the Ring" will be shown. Liverpool Olympia.</p>
        <p>Those who have not yet seen the first fav- ourite revue "Razzle Dazzle" must hurry, as next week is its fifth and final at the Olympia before it goes further North. This magnifi- cent production has created record revue busi- ness at this house, eclipsing all forerunners by its attractiveness and superb excellence. FOYER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>FUSES IN A GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FUSES IN A GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Serious Accident To A Lad At</p>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>A dramatically interesting story attaches to the explosion of a bomb fuse in the garden adjoining the residence of Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Parker, at Penketh, on Wednesday morning, when a lad named Ernest Appleton (16), son of Mr. John Appleton, of Chapel-road, suffered the loss of three fingers. It appears that staying with Mr. and Mrs Parker was a bombing officer, who has been awarded a Military Cross. With him he brought from the Front some bomb fuses, but his hosts not wishing them to remain about the house he took them into the garden and, as he thought, destroyed the lot. That one of the fuses was not rendered inoperative, however, is certain, for Appletoh, who works as a garderwr for Mr. Parker, got hold of it, and, it is said, in spite of the warning from his fellow gardener, he, lad-like, was curious to examine it. It is thought that he must have used some hard instrument to open it. At any rate, while he was playing with the fuse it exploded, and three of his fingers were blown off. Miss Murray, daughter of Dr. Murray, happened to be in the vicinity and rendered first-aid. The lad was taken to the Warrington Infirmary, and is reported to be progressing favourably. The wounded soldiers are very Much interested in him and cheer him up by comparing notes as to how they all fell in the wars. "He's the youngest soldier amongst us," they remark brightly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>CONSCIENTIOUS ALLEGATIONS.</head>
        <p>CONSCIENTIOUS ALLEGATIONS.</p>
        <p>has Representation Been Made To The Central Authority ?</p>
        <p>The following document, which had been re- ceived from the Home Office, was laid before the Warrington Board of Guardians at their meeting on Wednesday by the Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) :</p>
        <p>"Alfred Walker, a person who has been con- victed by court martial for disobedience to law- ful commands and whose case has been referred to the Committee on Employment of Conscien- tious Objectors by the Central Appeal Tribunal has alleged to the committee that he has a wife dependent on him, residing at 76, Priest- ley-street, Warrington, who is likely to become 60 necessitous as to require poor relief unless an allowance is made to her; and the Guar- dians are requested to be so good as to cause inquiry to be made into this statement and to fill in the particulars required on the back of this form."</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Dale: I think it is absolutely incor- rect.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Mather: Then he is a liar? Mr. T. Dale: I mean he has not made such a request. _ -</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) : But you cannot know that.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Dale: I do know it Mr. Chairman. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: May we have the statement?</p>
        <p>The Clerk re-read the letter as above and stated that inquiries were being made.</p>
        <p>Organ Recital at Bewsey Road. A week to-morrow (Sunday), special services will be held at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, when the preacher will be the Rev. Tom Dring. In the afternoon an organ recital will be given by Private J. Worsley Harrop, to be presided over by Mr. T. S. Steel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>MISSING.</head>
        <p>MISSING.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE G. WARBURTON.</p>
        <p>War Office Says " Missing " ; Comrade Says " Killed."</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Warburton, 11, Dixon-street, have received news from the War, Office that their son, Private George Warburton, is miss- ing, but this is contradicted by a comrade, Private J. Rotherham, who writes to say •he is killed. ,</p>
        <p>Private Warburton, who is only 19 years of age, enlisted on January 21st, 1916, and went to France in June. He was formerly employed by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., and was educated at Heathside School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>PRI‘ ATE 'F.</p>
        <p>"Like Being In Heaven To Get Into A Bed."</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Lynskey, South Lancashire Regiment, has written the following letter to his wife; Mrs. Lynskey, who resides at 26, Jockey-street. The letter runs: " Just a few lines to tell you how I got wounded. We were making a new trench in a big. wood. . . . They got me all right, and I very nearly had to stop there altogether. . . . lam getting a lot better now, and hope to be at home soon. It was like being in heaven to get into bed." Private Lynskey, who has been in the Army about fifteen years, went to France fourteen months ago. He is wounded in the chest, right arm and abdomen. He was born in Warring- ton, but spent his early years in Ireland. A previous employe of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Private Lynskey is 29 years of age. He has played football for Cadishead, and 'has three 'brothers serving.</p>
        <p>PICTURE HOUSE ASSESSMENTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>How Business Has Been Hampered At Warrington.</head>
        <p>How Business Has Been Hampered At Warrington.</p>
        <p>When the proprietors of the Picturedrome, Sankey-street, appealed against their rateable value at the Assessment Comnettee yesterday, Mr. W. H. Woodcock (chief assistant overseer) said that there had been certain restrictions on the business at cinema houses generally. The Lighting Order had made it that very little was done at the last house, because the streets were absolutely dark. Then there was the question of the tax. Mr. George Mather: I see crowds of people waiting to go in. Mr. Woodcock pointed out that the Picture- drome was rated far higher than any other place in the town.</p>
        <p>The committee referred the matter to the valuer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>OBJECTS TO KILLING.</head>
        <p>OBJECTS TO KILLING.</p>
        <p>But Quite Willing To Save Life, Says</p>
        <p>Conscientious Objector.</p>
        <p>Atk yesterday's Warrington Tribunal a painter and decorator named Thos Wm. Welsby, aged 36, married, appealed for exemption on con- scientious grounds.</p>
        <p>Dr. Josep-h : Let us hear the extent of your conscience. (Laughter.) Mr. F. A. Frost: No, give us a rough idea. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>Applicant said he had a conscientious objec- tion to blood-shedding. He is a member of the B.S.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. Elwell: If you wete personally attacked what would you do ?—Personal preservation is the first .law of Nature.' Does the personal policy extend to the national ?—That is a matter of opinion. Asked by the Chairman if he had 'any religious belief, he said, "None, particularly." Where do you find your conscience then?—No reply. Applicant added that he had made sacrifices in collecting for and giving to charitable objects. He considered the separation allow- ance would be too small for his wife to live on. He would be prepared to go anywhere where he would be of the most use.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion : Where you won't get shot?—Other than in actual fighting. He told the Chairman that he was quite will- ing to do Red Cross work or go into the R.A.M.C. Appeal refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>PASTOR'S ANNIVERSARY.</head>
        <p>PASTOR'S ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>At Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday, harvest and ministerial anniversary services were held, when the preacher for the day was the Pastor, the Rev. H. Ogden. In the afternoon a musical service was held. The Chairman, Mr. J. Griffiths, and the Speaker, Mr. F. Hickman, each emphasised the need of greater Church unity. The Primitive Methodist Choir, Legh-street then rendered the sacred cantata, "A song of thanksgiving." The principal vocalists were Mrs. Worthington Mrs. P Hoyle Miss Jones, _</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Tynan, Mr. J. Dakin. M. F. L. Bennett accompanied. Mr. Spruce conducted. At the evening service the preacher's theme was "The Sower and the Reaper," and the vocalist was Mr. A. W. Pearce, whose singing was much appreciated. On Thursday \a fruit banquet and congratu- latory meeting to the minister was held, when an excellent gathering assembled, and apprecia- tion of the minister's work was expressed by the deacons, and his work in the town was eulogised by the Rev. G. Curtis and the Mayor. A delightful programme of music was provided by Miss Colebourne, Miss Olive Dale, Mr. J. Poole and Mr. W. Tfnan. The accompanist was Mr. Tanner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>ARRESTED THOUGH EXEMPT ,?</head>
        <p>ARRESTED THOUGH EXEMPT ,?</p>
        <p>Singular Case Adjourned For Further Inquiries. .</p>
        <p>When a charge of failing to report under the Military Service Act was preferred; against a young man named Gilbert Tilley, 8, Beswick- street, at the Borough Police Court yesterday, the Chief Constable remarked that he had re- ceived a message from the Clerk to the local Tribunal to the effect that the man appealed for exemption on the 7th February last, and the following day was granted exemption .by the military authorities. Mr. Hallaway (the clerk) therefore did not know why the man had been arrested. He (the Chief Constable) asked that the case be adjourned until inquiries had been made, and this suggestion was adopted.</p>
        <p>Dangerous Pastime. Special Sergeant Piercey prosecuted three lads named Thomas Cunliffe, 140, Ellesmere- street; Sam Wernham, 7, Brook-street; and Thomas Whittle, 122, School Brow, for dis- charging fireworks in Greenall-street on the evening of the 22nd September. It was a very dark night and the fireworks made a consider- able glare.—Each defendant was fined 55..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>MILITARY TRIBUNALS.</head>
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNALS.</p>
        <p>"A WAPSINIGTON DAY 5' Al LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>Twenty-seven Appeals.</p>
        <p>There was a "Warrington day" before the Liverpool Appeal Tribunal on Monday, when, out of thirty-four appeals for hearing, only seven were not concerned with this district.</p>
        <p>The Lord Mayor of Liverpool presided, the chair being occupied later on by Alderman Maxwell. The Mayor of Warrington (Alder- man Peter Peacock) was present. Captain Dickenson was the military representative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>New Beer.</head>
        <p>A New Beer.</p>
        <p>The firm of B. Cunningham, brewers, appealed against the refusal of the Warrington Tribunal to grant exemption to their brewery foreman and the foreman of the bottling department. • Mr. T. S. Steel, who appeared for the firm, said that on the 13th July the Tribunal granted temporary exemption for one month. That de- cision the appellants did not contest, but owing to later circumstances they applied for leave to file a fresh appeal, and the application was granted. On the hearing of the appeal, how- ever, the Warrington Tribunal refused to grant exemption. It was stated that the appellants were the pioneers in the manufacture of a beer which had been largely supplied to munition works for sale to workmen. At the time of the first appeal they were only supplying three works, but since that date they had orders from twenty additional works; and if the two men appealed for were taken away for military ser- vice it would be impossible for them to main- Jain the supply. Before the introduction of this beer there had been numerous complaints of munition workers neglecting their work in order to obtain intoxicants, but since this beer had been supplied in the works it was reported that there was less difficulty in keeping the men at their work.</p>
        <p>The Lord Mayor: You haven't brought a sample here? (Laughter.) Mr. Steel: No, my Lord, I thought it might influence your decision. (Laughter.) Mr. Cunningham having given evidence,</p>
        <p>The Lord Afayor said the Tribunal did not see their way to grant any further extension of time.</p>
        <p>Young Farmer's Appeal.</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. J. Westbrook, solicitor, represented an appellant aged 26 years, employed on a Croft farm, and who had been exempted until Sep- tember 30th by the Warrington Rural Tribunal. The Lord Mayor said the Appeal Tribunal could not go back upon the, decision unless some further facts could be shown in support of the appeal.</p>
        <p>Mr. Westbroa urged that the man's employ- ment came within the list of certified exemp- tions, but the Tribunal refused the appeal.' Conditional Exemption For Man In Post Office</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>Work.</head>
        <p>Work.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, solicitor, pres- nted the appeal of a Sankey-street firm of cab proprietors who had endeavoured to get conditional exemp- tion for an employe aged 36, who is responsible in the yard for the arrangement of the work. The firm are contractors to His Majesty's Government in connection with Warrington Post Office, and this man did a great deal in connection with the postal department. The Warrington Tribunal, after ,communi- cating with the Post Office authorities, grante3 exemption to the 31st October, and considered that arrangements should be made to have this man's place taken ,by someone over military age,</p>
        <p>-The Appeal Tribunal granted conditional exemption.</p>
        <p>Marine Store Dealer.</p>
        <p>In the case of a marine store dealer (34), carrying on business at warehouses in Turner- street and Haydock-street----his own property.- Mr. T. S. Steel (solicitor) said his client started in business about twelve years ago. Prior to the war he had 40 employes, twenty of whom had joined the Army. Of those in his employ- ment all the men were ineligible, and amongst them were three cripples. Appellant has a motor lurry, five horses and 20 carts and three lurries. He deals in all classes of materials, which he supplied various munition works—the cloth and old rags go to firms engaged in making khaki. He had no one to carry on business in his absence, and he had to support his mother and young sister. There had been a suggestion by the local Tribunal that as a younger brother (26) is engaged in a similar business, one of the two might carry on the business of both. They had failed, however, to make arrangements: The two appeals were now heard together, and the military representative suggested that one should be granted temporary exemption and the other appeal dismissed. The Tribunal acted on this suggestion, giving the elder brother three months' exemption and dismissing the other appeal. Building Contractor's Letter.</p>
        <p>A single me an:aged 39, a partner in a firm of builders and contractors, had lodged an appeal, but did not appear.' Writing to the Military Representative a few days ago, appellant said he was making arrangements to enter the Royal Engineers, and expected to repert in Kent on September 26th or 27th. Owing to the short time at his disposal he was wcrking at high pressure to put his business in order, and he asked for the hearing of the appeal to be deferred for a few days.</p>
        <p>Captain Dickenson said that, according to the letter, appellant was actually in the Army. The Mayor of Warrington (Alderman Peter Peacock), in supporting an adjournment, said appellant had done good work for the Volun- teer Training Corps. An adjournment for a week was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>CIVIL SERVICE.</head>
        <p>THE CIVIL SERVICE.</p>
        <p>Tribunal And Combing Out At</p>
        <p>Government Offices.</p>
        <p>That the Warrington Tribunal is intent on combing out wherever possible, including Gov- ernment departments, was emphasised at their sitting at the Town Hall on Friday, when an applicant for exemption, 40 years old, at pre- sent employed at the divisional office of the. Labour Exchange, told an interesting story of his adventures before being passed for general service. Eighteen months ago he was rejected as physically unfit., He is deaf on the left side and got ricked back from lifting a heavy weight three years ago, and produced a medical certificate re same. He had also been examined by two experts, who, while con- sidering that he had cardiac trouble, refused to give a certificate. In reply to the Chairman applicant said the doctors were in military uniform, and Dr. Joseph said if they were in the R.A.M.C. they could not give a certificate when the Medical Board had passed the man fit.</p>
        <p>Alderman- Tinnion was anxious to know if there were any single men under 40 employed at the Labour Exchange, and applicant ob- jected, as he was bound to secrecy as to office affairs.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion said he. saw men of mili- tary age going in and coming out and appar- ently very fit. He did not think applicant would make an efficient soldier and he thought there were others that might be combed out.</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph said it was perfectly apparent that applicant was not fit for general service and the ease would be adjourned for a week to communicate with the Labour Exchange and see whether an efficient soldier could be got in his stead. Thrice Medically Examined.</p>
        <p>Mr. Westbrook was also solicitor in a rather peculiar case, when the man applied for, aged 22 years, and who is employed by his father, a draper and hosier in the Market, had been first rejected and then passed for general ser- vice. He appeared again before the Central Medical Board, London, and was passed for garrison duty at home.—The appeal was re- fused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTOR</head>
        <p>CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTOR</p>
        <p>Appeal Dismissed By County Appeal Tribunal.</p>
        <p>HIS VIEWS ON THE KAISER.</p>
        <p>A well-known member of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council and prominent Socialist, Thomas Howard, 3, Frederick-street, Warrington, appeared before the Liverpool Appeal Tribunal on Monday. He appealed on conscientious grounds, and alleged that it was better in the national interest that he should remain in his present employment, that of farm labourer.</p>
        <p>Howard's appeal to the local Tribunal had been dismissed in March, and the Lord Mayor (Alderman Mather), said he recalled from news- paper reports that after his appeal was dis- missed Howard went off. He was arrested and came before the Warrington magistrates, but being an ex-Volunteer, was held to be exempt. Howard: I was exempt under the Act, and everything was null and void. The Military Representative (Captain Dickenson) said that ex-Volunteers could now be called up for service. The local Tribunal, in their remarks on the case, said that Howard had been passed for Class Bl—a class very much wanted. Appellant said he was a Voluftteer from 1908 to 1912—that was before he began to study these matters. Since then he had taken a keen in- terest in international politics. In reply to the Lord Mayor, who asked when his conscience began to prick him, he said that when he was 19 he joined the Independent Labour Party and began to consider the whole international position. Then in 1911 the Moroccan crisis occurred, when the Germans sent the Panther to Agadir, and from then he took a deep interest in those matters. He began to see that war was wrong, that it did not help to settle international difficulties, but only helped .to create further difficulties, which did not mean any advantage whatever to the work- ing classes. The. Lord Mayor: That is purely political.</p>
        <p>Captain Dickenson: In 1914 you were a leader of the strikers at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co.'s, were you not? Howard: I was not a leader, only a striker. You were on the committee F—Yes.</p>
        <p>_ The Lord Mayor: What would you do if the Germans came over?—l should act upon the Government's instructions to non-combatants. Captain Dickenson: Wouldn-t you do as you did in the strike—have a heavy stick in your hand ?-oh, no. What is the use of a heavy stick against the Germans?</p>
        <p>The Police tell me that you went about with a heavy stick, dnd that ' a large amount of damage was done in the town.—Yes, but they do not attribute the damage to me. With reference to his present employn,lent. Howard said be was not now working on a farm at Rixton, but was acting as hay-cutter and trusser for a Latchford man who is agent for a Manchester firm. It was, he understood, work of great national importance. Kaiser the Victim of Circumstances.</p>
        <p>Captain Dickenson submitted that this man objects to fighting Germans, but had no objec- tion to fighting the police. Howard said he never undertook to fight the police. If he had done that he should have been convicted in a Civil Court. .He appeared before a Civil Court and was acquitted. Mr. Ormeral : Would you submit to force?-1 suppose _I would have to do.</p>
        <p>liow far are you prepared to defend what you believe to be the right thing?—By moral per- suasion.</p>
        <p>The Lord Mayor: Do you think you could morally persuade the Kaiser?—No, I don't. T think the Kaiser is a victim of cireumst4nces, as we all are.</p>
        <p>The Lord Mayor: If you could persuade him we would be very glad. Howard: The party to which I belong are try- ing their •utmost to persuade members of the same class in Germany that they are in the wrong, and that they -cannot receive any bene- fit from the war. Howard added that he would not use force. The appeal was dismissed.</p>
        <p>Pensions Committee and Labour Representation.</p>
        <p>A meeting of trade union representatives, will be held at the Town Hall at 8 o'clock on Mon- day evening to nominate a Labour representa- tive in place of Private Charles Dukes on the War Pensions Committee. Any trade union which may have been overlooked in the sending out of invitations is now asked to appoint two delegates to attend the meeting.</p>
        <p>) .&lt;</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
