        <p>THANKS FOR THE HARVEST. PARISH CHURCH. Harvest thanksgiving services were continued at the Parish Church on Sunday, when the preacher in the morning was the Rev. C. C. Elcum, vicar of St. Agnes, Liverpool, and in the evening the Rev. the Hon. C. F. Cross, vicar of Stretton. The choir rendered an anthem, "The heavens are telling" (Hadyn), the trio being sung by Master Neville Davies, Mr. Walter Dennett and Mr. J. B. Poole. HOLY TRINITY. The services twere continued at Holy Trinity Church on Sunday, the preacher in the morning being the Rev. E. L. J. Roberts, and in the evening the Rev. E. B. Whitley, rector of New-. toil. A children's iervice was held in the after- noon, at which the speaker was Mr. W. R. L. Laws. An anthem, "'Ye shall dwell in the land," was sung at the evening service, the solos being taken .by Mr. Taylor (bass) and Master Arthur Browne (treble). Miss Longdin pre- sided at the organ, and the offertories taken on behalf of the Curates' Fund realised £l5. • ST. JAMES', LATCHFORD. - The Rev. G. C. Briggs was the preacher in' the morning, and the .Vicar, thy. Rev. B. Firth, in the evening, at the harvest thanksgiving ser- viceS held. at St. James' Church, Latchford, on Sunday. At the evening service the choir sang a setting of the 23rd Psalm, "The Lord is my Shepherd," Mr. E. Davies being at the organ. ST.' THOMAS', STOCKTON HEATH. There were good congregations present at St. Thomas' Church harvest thanksgiving services held on Sunday, and the. offertories totalled £l3 12s. 9d. The Rer. R. Percival (vicar „of St. Peter's), preached in the morning, and the Rev. J. M. Buckmaster (curate of' St. Elphin's), in the afternoon. Stainer's Communion Service and an anthem, "Exalt His Name" (C. Simpvr), in which Mr. Frank Cook' took the solo, were sting in the itio'rnizgi while the</p>
