        <p>"ADOPTED" GIIILDREN. Mother Who Re-claimed Them And Promised Reform. The Clerk to the Warrington Board of Guar- dians (Mr. A. Bottomley) reported at the meet- ing of that authority on Wednesday that a woman, formerly of Warrington, now living at Burton-on-Trent, who had taken away her two children from a school at Preston, where they had been placed as the adopted children of the -Warrington Union had been visited by the superintendent relieving Officer. There seemed to be a possibility .of the man with whom she was living re- turning to . Warrington. The woman had signed the pledge and pleaded • that the children should be. left in her charge, at all events until Christmas, to allow her to prove that she had reformed. The .children were , attached to the woman and would not leave her In a letter she said no harm should come to them and they would see no harm, and their possession would be a new life for her. The authorities at Preston could prosecute her for taking the children away in clothing belonging to them. -Mr. Mather : I don't think so; she did not kidnap them. They were allowed out with her. The Clerk: She asked to be allowed to take them to be photographed and she would bring them back. She did not do so. Mr. Mather: I did not understand that. , Mr. Shearer, who visited the woman at Bur- ton, said she was making an effort to make a home for the children. She said she really in- tended to return the children, but it got so late that she thought they would get into trouble and she brought them away from Preston to Warrington. Mr. Flanagan moved that the children be taken back to the school. They should not encourage the idea that adopted children could be taken away in this way. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Is it quite clear that this woman is living with a man who, is not her husband? The Clerk: Yes. Mr. Brooks: And these two children belong to someone else. The Clerk: I think there are strong reasons why we should try to get these children back and prosecute the woman. A Photographic Negative. Mr. Brooks said after publicity had been given to this case an application was made to the Matron at the Cottage Homes for-leave to take a boy away to have him photographed, but the Matron said "We have heard about photographing before," and refused the appli- cation. (Rear, hear, and laughter.) Mr. Flanagan's motion was carried. _ -</p>
        <p>WHOOPING COUGH. Relieved at once and Quickly Cured by Veno's Lightning Cough Cure. Mrs. Prince, 135, Hyde Park-road, Southsea, writes My little Alice had whooping cough so badly that she could neither eat nor sleep for it. I tried heaps of things, which did no good; but at last I got Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, and after a few doses she got relief. At the end of three weeks she was completely cured." Trust Veno's to cure coughs, colds, asthma, bronchitis, influenza, catarrh, and whooping cough. ' Prices, ild., is. 3d. and 3s. everywhere. A Bit Depressing. I have just ,had the mournful task, writes a correspondent,, of taking a funeral in one of our cemeteries, and I was walking back with one of the sextons, and as we walked I noticed the rather unusual sight of five funerals com- ing up at once. "It must be rather depressing always being up- here," I said to my com- panion. "Well," he said, "it is. It's all right on a day like this, when we may get anything from twenty to twenty-five funerals, but somP days we only get three or four, and then it do be a bit depressing."—" Manchester Guardian."</p>
