        <p>PRIVATE EDWARD STOKES. Killed.</p>
        <p>had only recently been married to Miss Mar- garet Dutton, of School-brow, while on a short furlough. He re-joined at the outbreak of war *and had been in France since last February. He was in his 25th year. Private Stokes wa4 a keen follower of football, and played for his works' team—the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., Dallam Forge. He was educated at St. Benedict's R.C. School and attended St Benedict's Church. PRIVATE JOHN BURRELL. " Bear Your Loss As Bravely As He Met Death." Private John Burrell, 26, Leicester-street, South Lancashire Regiment, and son of Mr. Reuben -Burrell, 26 Leicester-street, a White- cross wiredrawer, has been killed in action. Private M. Parr, writing, as one of his chUms, to Mrs. Burrell, says:— ." Your son was killed in action, bravely doing his duty. He lies buried in a grave near the firing line, with a nice cross on. He was well liked by all the boys in his regiment</p>
