        <p>PRIVATE W. E. CHETTER. Died of Wounds.</p>
        <p>Private Edward Chetter, of the R.A.M.C., only son of Mr. and Mrs. J. Chetter, 42, Pierpoint- street, died on September 17th from wounds in the leg. Official news to this effect came as a great shock to his parents, who were not aware that he had been wounded. Private Chetter, who was nineteen and a half years of age, was formerly employed at the. Bewsey Wire Ropery,. and later at the White- cross .Co.'s works. He was an "old boy" of the Wycliffe School, and a former member of the Bewsey Boy Scouts, and of the St. Paul's Church Bible Class. He had been two years in the Army, and at the Front fourteen months.: ,</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. CORPORAL R. BARBER. In 7.4uffolk Hospital AVith Bullet Wounds In Both Hands. Writing to his parents, Mr. and Mrs. T. Barber 216, Knutsford-road, Latchford, Cor- poral Robert Barber, Grenadier Guards, states</p>
