        <p>PRIVATE FRANK HOLLINGSWORTH. Wounded.</p>
        <p>wounded. He is now in the Lord Derby Hos- pital, Winwick, suffering from a wound just above the ankle of his right leg. Enlisting on 25th May, 1915, Private Hol- lingsworth was sent to France on the 9th of February, 1916. An "old boy" of St. James' School, he is only 20 years of age. He took an interest in football, and, prior to his enlist- ment, was an employe of Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd. PRIVATE T. O'DAY. Gunshot Wound In The Right Forearm. Mr. and Mrs. O'Day, 1, Eldon-street, have been officially notified by the War Office that their son, Private Thomas O'Day, Royal Inniskilling Fusiliers, was wounded in the right forearm by gun-shot. Private O'Day comes of a military family, his father having served in the South Lancashire Regiment for twelve months. Two brothers, Private John O'Day, who has served seven• years in the South Lancashire Regiment in India, and Private Peer O'Day, South Lancashire Regi- ment, and W cousin, Private William O'Day, are serving with the Colours. Mrs. O'Day has two brothers and a nephew serving. Private Thomas O'Day himself enlisted in August, 1914, and had been at the Front fifteen months. He formerly worked at Messrs. Stubs, Ltd., Scotland-road, and was educeted at St. Mary's</p>
