        <p>PRIVATE W. TITCHARD. Wounded.</p>
        <p>son is suffering from shell shock, but there is no need to worry. He was taken away all right. . . . . The parcel you forwarded has, been divided among his chums, which is the rule in this platoon. . . . He was always a willing lau and a good soldier, willing to do anything be was asked to do." Private Titchard, who is M years of age, is an employe in the netting - department of Messrs. Wands Bros. He attended Silver- street School and St. Peter's Church. His two brothers are on active service, Arthur in the Army Service Corps, and John in Kitchener's Army. His father is a slater's labourer and works for Mr. Samuel Caldwell. PRIVATE J. C. WILSON. • Enlisted When Only Sixteen Years Of Age. Private John C. Wilson, Royal Irish Fusiliers, son of Mr. C. E: Wilson, 27, Golborne-street,</p>
        <p>.1 ' 44,3- - ' • . " :45 • 41A . • • .. „ . . . • : •</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. C. WILSON. Wounded.</p>
        <p>has received a bayonet wound in the foot, while serving in France, but is progressing favourably. Private Wilson, who was formerly employed! as a cinema operator by Mr. H. W. Griffin,. enlisted in the R.A.M.C. in November, 1914, when only sixteen years of age. He was transferred to the Royal Irish Fusiliers sir months later ad went to the Front in Feb- ruary this year. He was educated at thee Heathside School, and had been a member of the Gelborne-street Baptist Church and Sunday School since he was four years of ago. Another brother 21 years of age, is stationed in Eng— land with the Manchester Regiment</p>
