        <p>ANGLING. WARRINGTON WORKING MEN'S ASSOCIATION. • N.A. CENTRE. The majority of members paid a visit to the Gowy during the munition holidays. J,. Chadwick was successful in capturing a fine dace of 10oz. ldr.: the largest roach weighed was 10oz. sdr. The upper reaclfes beywid the mill were the most favoured, and nothing of note was done down below.' Those who travelled towards Little Barrow had fair sport, but specimens were lacking. Gudgeon loz. 6dr., dace 70z., roach 10oz., were caught, but nothing heavier. Weaver Top Hose. The Weaver, thes;oride'rful Weaver we should say, as reports keep on coming of matches doing better every week. One report from the upper reaches, Winsford way, says that well over 51b. of fish were taken in a match of two hours. There were sixteen prizes; lowest weight in, 21b. loz. 4dr. Another match from Manchester, four hours' fish- ing, but in water where there are contests every week, and where our clubs go (Newbridge): 1,. 51b. 13oz. 2dr.; 2, ?lb. 2Oz. 15dr.; 3; 31b. Ooz. sdr.; 4, 21b. Soz. 12dr.; S. 21b. soz. sdr.; 6,21 b. loz. 3dr. For numbers and smartness of angling, these weights prove it is about the finest match-fishing river in England. The open match at Norton should draw a fine entry, as £3 55., first; £2, second; and 1 2s. 6d., third, should bring out of town anglers. I believe the canal will be pegged off from the bridge, 'Williams' meadow„ and up towards Run- corn. Match, Saturday, October 14th; draw, Red Lion, Preston Brook; time to start, 3 p.m. All prizes paid out after the weighing-in at the Red Lion. All entries to Mr. T. Roberts, treasurer, Bay Horse Hotel, Winwick-street, not later than October 11th. A.S. MUNITION WORKERS AND RAIDS. State Guarantee Of Compensation For, Injuries. The Minister of Munitions has issued the following announcement:— " If a munition worker (as defined by the Munitions of War Amendment Act, 1916) is, on or after the first day of October, 1916, killed or injured by hostile aircraft or by measures taken to combat them whilst upon the premises of the factory in which he is employed, and if the in- jured worker or his dependents on proceeding under the Workmen's Compensation Act, 1916, fail to recover compensation, his Majesty's Government undertake to pay compensation on the scale of the Act."</p>
