        <p>THE LAND AND THE WAR. Proposed VTholf..,Gaie Exemption Of Farm Labourers. At the meeting of the Rural Military Tribunal on Wednesday, The Chairman (Mr. George Mather) referred to the report of a meeting in Preston on Mon- day, and the subject cropped up about farmers and farmers' men. The Chairman, who is the Chairman of the Appeal Tribunal in Preston, said that it would be wise to consider what was going on in other quarters with respect to ex- changed men, who had been in the Army. There was a scheme going on-whereby these men were going to be exchanged for farmers' men, and be said it would take three weeks or a month to get things into working order. People tliought because the harvest was got in that the, farmers were at the end of their work, but very im- portant work remained for skilled men in pre- paring the land for corning crops, especially the wheat sowing. The military representative was there and ought to inquire straight away.' It was said that farm men should be put back for some time until it was seen how the new scheme would work out. A telegram was received from the War Office which read." Cancel all Calling- up notice papers issued to men employed in agri- culture, dairy farming, market gardening and small holdings. No men so employed are to be called up pending further instructions." The cases were put back for a month. Mr. Mather asked should they at that Tribunal follow these lines? ! Rev. A. M. Mitchell did not think they should , act on what was said at Preston. The Chairman thought it would be common !sense to postpone the cases for a month. I Mr. Knowles said they ought to exercise dis- cretion. Mr. Furness (military representative) did' not I think the Tribunal would be justified in acting lon a telegram sent to Preston, the conditions !might be very different there from an agricul- tural standpoint. Until they received a similar notification from the War Office or the Local !Government Board he did not think that they would be justified in departing from the course they had taken. It was decided to proceed on the old lines. I The Tribunal, however, apparently kept the possible development in view, as in quite a , number of appeals by farmers for their sons or I other men employed on their holdings, the cases I were adjourned for a month. An Anonymous Letter. ! The case of a Fearnhead publican, aged 35, married, was reopened on the application of the military representative: It appeared that he I had been granted exemption on the under- standing that he would utilise his spare time in working for his brother on munitions. lApparently in consequence of receiving an I anonymous letter the military representative I paid two surprise visits. The man was working on the first occasion, but not on the second.— The applicant said the job his brother was en- gaged on woub4 be finished in about a month, ! and he did not know if he would get any more. Military Representative: In that case I suggest that you might go into the Army at the end of this month.—A month's exemption was granted.</p>
