        <p>MR. THOMAS JENKS. Death And Furzeral Of Well' Known Engine Driver. The announcement of the death of 311' Thomas Jenks, 48, Lovely-lane, Warrington, 11r the Salford Royal 'Hospital on Friday of lasj week, following an operation, has been receive. til with general regret. He was one of the oldes',,., e if not the oldest, Warrington employe of Landon and North-Western Railway Co. .I.` was 68 years of age and, as a railwayman, IV; whole of his career was well-known not Ow in Warrington, but also in Cre*e- and 111311/ other places in Lancashire and Cheshire. The funeral took place on Monday at Cliffe Burial Ground, when the Rev. E. L. Roberts, curate-in-charge of St. Luke.", officiated. The principal mourners were: Messrs. W" Jenks (son), R. Welsby and J. Scotson (sons-q: law), H. T. Dean (brother-in-law), Sergert,n,„ Major Hill and Messrs. Samuel Jenks, A. J01,17L; T. A. Dean and Wilfred Jenks (nephews). 11.!W, attending were Messrs. T. D. Williams and " Kettle (Runcorn), J. Toft; G. Ayland, Thonigsell (loco. foreman), Evans, W. Campbell, J. ram, G. G. Smith and other friends: The bearerg were six. of Mr. Jenks's fellow workmen' Messrs. W. Siddall, W. Spencer, W. Jackson,. J. Plinston, J. Bigney and W. Stephens. RINGING DOOR EELLS. At the Juvenile Court, on WednesdaY,. Detective. Anderson charged a boy with ring- ing the bell of Dr. Manson's house, in Musl,nelii street, on Tuesday night. The lad a(11.11' he had done so, but said he. thought it wa an empty. house. The Chief Constable said the police we': constantly getting complaints about this 111?,h tice and doctors were very much bothered wit the ringing of their door bells. re- Defendant was fined 2s. .Mr. Upson marking that the fine could have been 40s.</p>
